   130	- **Lowmarch in the cold (flag).** The ferryman's round shed has reed hurdles tied between its posts for the cold; its horse-walk is eight strides across; there is a stone trough inside the door, a mounting block, and a lean-to **rope-house** against it where he keeps his blocks and cordage. The hauling rope lies coiled against the drum. He has watched the river for "twenty cold terms". His ice: the shelf, then a skin that takes the snow, then "one night it goes blue"; it went blue on the 153rd night; he sounds it with an iron bar and marks his track with a double row of spruce boughs ("my green"), which swings wide at the bend above the landing, where a side water comes in under the western bank and the ice never shuts sound. He pulled the green on the 224th. Open water down the middle on the 230th. He has the scow on blocks before the 263rd and begins to knock them out on the night of the 270th.
   131	- **What four a month buys (flag):** the hearth's end; the pot at dusk, a bowl a head; and the crew's names said to anybody who comes asking. Not bread, candles, the back room or the mule. "A cold term's five by my book": twenty, in front.
   132	- **The cold-term board:** *Roof, the ferryman's rope-house; shingles found; five, when it has kept a thaw out.* · *The well-house at the south fork; a door hung and a hearthstone laid; three, and flour.* · the fence at forty. The five take the roof (five strokes). The roof is paid on the 268th and Pike says its line twice: **eleven lines** in his book at the movement's end.
   133	- **The axe-head hold (flag):** two days up the cold road, which follows the north water and then climbs a side valley; a gate with an axe-head nailed to its top bar; a long house under turf and a longer byre with sixty head. Its **factor**: short and thick, a tally stick, a sheepskin; his thumb-mark is shaped like a wedge; he asks no name. The long fence leaves the yard at the gate, climbs a four-hundred-yard hillside aslant and runs east along the foot of a band of grey rock; posts six strides apart. **Teague's crew dug all forty-one remaining holes before the frost and capped each with a flat stone and straw.** Two of Teague's standing posts, the twentieth and twenty-first from the gate, stood a hand north of the line; Brom re-set them. "At the hold above, in my father's time, a dog got in among the calves… It's pasture now."
   134	- **Shale-backs, added to Book 7's account (flag):** a herd will lie up for the cold under a lee, on rock that it has scraped bare; it rises hind end first; a calf in fright makes a thin grinding cry and its plates stand on end; a herd roused by a calf lies down again when the calf is quiet; they will follow a strip of uncovered lichen at about thirty-eight to forty yards in the hour and will take lichen from a cap. Seventeen in this herd, four of them calves. The read was tried at 350, 200 and 60 yards and, in earnest, at a pace and a half: no answer was seen. Karis has entered both and calls neither a test.
   135	- **The silent yard (flag):** a square trodden under the ash, twelve strides on a side, a hazel peg at each corner, a bench from the upper inn on its western edge. Teague's rules: three touches; "a touch is what the one who took it calls"; over the pegs is a touch; nobody strikes what a healer has had her hands on this season. The name is the three grey men's. Bouts on the 148th (three to none) and the 158th (three to one); a sack game on the 190th, three a side.
   136	- **Teague's spear, as seen (flag; see Owner flags):** a long quiet face under a wool cap, pale eyes, big chapped hands; fights with a seven-foot ash pole with a rag bound over one end; turns on the back heel; draws the pole back through the leading hand "like a bolt through a staple"; marks a touch taken by lifting the pole's butt a hand and grounding it once; shortens the grip to the pole's middle and uses both ends.
   137	- **Teague's wrist (flag):** the leather has been on it "since before the boy could lift a pack"; the joint is thicker than its fellow by the width of a finger; it "clicks"; "A Force paid where the line came out of him." The older healer binds it to her board at night and tells him to leave his Path shut till the thaw. A block lies on the second ledge of the cold road, with a herd lying up above it; he will shift it "as a carter would". The young one has brought him a hot cloth for it on every cold night since he joined. In his own window Teague is named as a Force for the first time in this edition. No Force is used on the page.
   138	- **Fragment observations (flag; no new numeric ceiling):** Wind's locked count is the same for four strides as for eight ("it's paying for the stop"). Ember's heat comes back to the hand that carries the lit thing. Compression on one heel leaves half the load in him; "It needs ground worth sending a load to." Pressure goes through what it strikes and on into what is behind it, and comes back off a curved surface. Blade's edge lasts "about as long as a struck match", and the steel pays. Leaned on for long, the read takes his near sight: an hour after the calf, longer after the ice; lying flat it reads through his chest and forearms at the same pace and a half. Sound ice takes a weight "in a wide shallow dish"; rotten ice takes it on one side; frozen ground "rings". A point of Anchor that he has set himself can be taken down with his hand on it.
   139	- **The ice road (flag):** a four-wheeled cart behind two mules, eighty yards out, with a dozen barrels and two pigs of iron for the smith; the carter heavy and grey-bearded, unnamed; one barrel of fish lost. Hauled out on the ferry's own rope and drum by Oryn's brown mule. Pike: "That's said. It's not entered."
   140	- **The kit as a tool (flag):** the cord is tarred hemp no thicker than a pencil, with a knot at every fifth stride and a brass eye whipped into one end; **five knots make a length** (twenty-five strides); odd strides are taken with a notched slip of ash "a stride long by the dead man's reckoning". Karis cut a peg of peeled hazel to the stake's length. The inn yard, from the plank's left-hand post to a scratch on the stone trough by the gate: one length, three knots and two strides. Karis measured the cord against the Ternhall brass on the 116th and again on the 170th: thirty-one lays "and a piece under half" to the first knot, unchanged.
   141	- **The edges (flag):** each strip's length pricked into the deal with a needle. In Cael's hand the Ternhall strip stops a hair short of its prick and Hesk's goes a hair past; the gap is one of Karis's own hairs, which she keeps in her notebook. True in the other four hands, and true again when he takes his hand away.
   142	- **Karis (flag):** her rule dates from the day, "as a girl", when a registrar laid her card before her and could not say what instrument it had been taken with. She once read the archive's name "on the spine of a catalogue that she was not allowed to open". The mark stands "beside the Stair twice over and beside the fourth place", and not beside the Fallow or the Hall (as Book 7's stakes have it). Her leaf: *What I should have to be able to show.*, eleven lines.
   143	- **Her case, as said on the 268th (flag):** "I think that they are instructions." (the same forty-one kinds in the same order on three floors; a line that carries the circle's own measure); a key to the index is kept; "There is a town on the border, inside the Line. It is called Treswick."; an archive under its wall and older than the wall, "cut out of the rock before the registry had an office anywhere"; "The few who have written of it call it the Deepstacks."; she does not think that anybody has been into its oldest part since before the Compact; a provision for scholars, which she has seen cited and never used; she holds "one paper still that such a place might honour: Iron, the third rank, certified and retained." **Seln:** a gate with a book in it; a registry office over the archive; a station on its crown; the paper that executes "on the hour when any of us shows a certificate inside the Line".
   144	- **Thornwater (flag):** the ford in thaw is thigh-deep over the sill, with the white stones and the bar under water; Lira finds the ledge by the smooth water and the line of standing waves below it. The ankle "had knit as it lay in a river, crooked"; an hour. Oryn: "It stops being mine when you take it." She will not undo the older healer's knot and will look at the shoulder "beside her". The bowman comes down off his platform during the mending. The steading's woman counts for a sixth.
   145	- **Roles (all unnamed):** the axe-head hold's factor; the carter on the ice; the ferryman and his boy; the lower inn's keeper; the older healer; the tall one; the bow; the young one; the bowman and the steading's woman at Thornwater; the hide-woman; the three grey men.
   146	
   147	## Owner flags
   148	
   149	1. **Teague's spear: a woman or a man? (blocks nothing here; needed before any later movement gives this person a pronoun.)** The packet, BOOK_MAP §3, §8 and §13 and the Book 8 ledger say "the tall woman". Book 7's closed text says "a tall man" in Cael's point of view (`chapter-52.md:45`, `:337`) and "He was not a man of many words" in Teague's (`chapter-51.md:357`; also `chapter-25.md:85`). This movement uses no pronoun and neither word for this person ("the tall one", "Teague's spear", "the spear", "its owner"). Options: change three lines in Book 7; or amend Book 8's plan.
   150	2. **Does Teague read?** The plan says that he does not, and the packet's beat is "he cannot read the sheet and does not need to". Book 7 `chapter-25.md:59–77` has him read a paper "from the first word to the seal". I staged the beat as distance (3:223: the cloth hangs in another house, in the dark, and he knows what it says). Nothing in this movement says that he can or cannot read.
   151	3. **Lira's "first ask since Norhold"** against Book 7's asking on the 82nd (above, under stagings).
   152	4. **The stroke and the bar.** Book 7's stakes carry it on thirty-two (the newer bar, and the kit's stake) and on thirty-four, and not on thirty-one or thirty-three; Karis's window follows that. BOOK_MAP spine 13 (the 280th) says "the same stroke and bar on all four sites". Movement 3 will need one or the other.
   153	5. **How long is a cord?** Book 7's long cord comes to about twenty-two yards (a perimeter of sixty-two cords and twenty strides round a radius of two hundred and twenty yards). BOOK_MAP gives the court as "about 1,107 yards across (159 cords)", about seven yards to the cord. This movement mints only the kit's cord (a knot at every fifth stride, five knots to a length) and gives no yardage for it.
   154	6. **The deployment count moves to twenty-nine.** Nothing in the plan fixes a later figure that I could find. If the coordinator would rather that the read in earnest were not counted, four log lines change (2:229, 3:305, 4:243, 5:217).
   155	7. **Four markers** (above).
   156	8. **Cutaways are about a thousand words under the packet's figure** (3,445 against 4,500), and **progression vocabulary is about 58 per 10k** against the plan's 82 for the first third. Both could be raised in a repair without new events: Karis's window has room for the kit's eight stations, and Teague's for the meadow as he reads it.
   157	9. **Months.** The cold stay is "four a month… five months… twenty"; no day-length of a month is stated anywhere. "Five months and twenty days." stands word for word (#56).
   158	10. **"west" is never spoken** in the movement (above, under stagings). If the owner wants Karis to say the word on the 263rd, it is one sentence.
   159	11. **The sweep probe** could not find source chapters in this packet, so the movement has no skeleton figure.
   160	
   161	## State at movement end (for the ledger)
   162	
   163	**Calendar.** The night of the **two hundred and seventieth day past the Line**, at Lowmarch. Days on the page: 131, 133, 138–146 (the fence; the herd on the 140th), 147, 148, 150, 158, 161, 168, 170 (Karis), 176, 190, 224, 230, 263, 264–265 (the saddle; the ford), 265–266 (Thornwater), 267–268 (down), 268, 270. No season, month or English weekday name is on the page. The river: shelf ice on the 131st; a road from the 154th; open at the middle on the 230th; "a week" from carrying a boat on the 268th; the scow's blocks being knocked out on the night of the 270th.
   164	
   165	**Where everyone is.** Six at the lower inn: Cael, Lira, Brom, Karis, Seln, and Oryn, who came down from Thornwater unasked because Lowmarch is her first stop after a thaw and the mill her second. Her brown mule is in the ferryman's shed. The mill's grey jenny is not mentioned after Book 7. Teague's five are at the upper inn; they do not appear after the 190th.
   166	
   167	**Cael.** Nineteen. **Ten fragments.** **Deployment since the Line: twenty-nine** (twenty-seven at entry; the read on frozen ground at the calf, the 140th; the read on river ice, the 161st). All ten tried in the cold term and written up as *The ten, by use*: Wind (the lock cannot be shortened, only placed), Ember (the burn), Shadow (held, not opened), Compression (both heels; ground worth a load), Pressure (seen coming; narrow and deep; comes back), Blade (short; the steel pays), the read (ground or air; a pace and a half; the eyes), Storm (one lane; ten counts; a fourth untried), Anchor (a point held to thirty; ten, twelve and fourteen feet), Tide (asked of Lira; told no; not used). Shadow used twice in all and shut. Anchor's counted uses still two. Tide: Oryn twice, himself three times, nobody else. **The still place** has not been read or touched. **The instruments:** on the 168th the kit's cord measured the inn yard at forty-two strides for Karis, Lira, Brom and Seln and at forty-one and a piece for him, three times; the Ternhall brass read a hair short in his hand and Hesk's a hair long; he has touched none of the three since; his ruling is *hold*; Oryn has been told and has no answer. Nobody has offered a cause. He lost to Teague's spear three to none (148th) and three to one (158th). He has entered Teague as the better captain. He has not ruled on Karis's case. He has written *West, and inside*. Body: sound; knuckles skinned on the 140th; near sight lost for an hour on the 140th and for an evening on the 161st, and back.
   168	
   169	**Lira.** Wind; Iron, Rank One. No burst. **Left arm released on the 266th: she undid Oryn's knot herself.** All eight marks pale. "It'll be slow for a month and it'll ache in the wet for a year… Don't fall on it." It has held a boot and lifted a bench. **Right ankle mended on the 266th**, an hour on Oryn's plank; she stands on the ball of the foot. She wears the carter's great boot without its packing. She still has Oryn's crossbow and its one bolt (not mentioned as returned). She coached Cael's feet from the bench ("out" and "home") and the young one's. **She asked for the ankle on the 266th; before that she asked nobody for anything in this movement.**
   170	
   171	**Brom.** Iron Skin; Copper. No declaration. **Right shoulder still in the older healer's webbing;** Oryn would not undo another woman's knot and will look at it beside her. He set forty-one posts left-handed and re-set two of Teague's. He fetched the carter out of the river lying flat, with the bill's hook. He keeps a second dead slip (the fence) inside his shirt. He lays six bowls and takes none back.
   172	
   173	**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three. **She has made her case to six** (the 268th): instructions; an index with a key that is kept; Treswick; the Deepstacks; the scholars' provision; her certificate. She said the sentence whole to Cael on the 263rd. She cannot read the four signs and has put no meaning on the stroke and the bar. She knows how the kit's cord is used and has taught the five. She has entered the anomaly as *Cause: not found. No inference entered.* and has written no sentence joining it to anything. Fifty-two sheets; the hide; the kit; the dead man's ledger, open now; a hair in her notebook. She keeps the purse.
   174	
   175	**Seln.** Shadow; Bronze. Unmarked. He kept the door at every trial with his back turned. He told only Brom about two posts. He counts the days since the clerk's packet went over on the 130th: a hundred on the 230th, a hundred and forty on the 270th. He knew the names Treswick and the Deepstacks before Karis said them. He has taught nobody anything. **The case is on him, shut.**
   176	
   177	**Oryn.** Tide; Iron, Rank Four. She released the arm only when Lira took it; mended the ankle; left the shoulder to the woman who tied it. She has seen the map with four rings and the line ("That's a line.") and has been told of the fourth site: twice the size, fenced, unentered. She has been told of the cord and the edges. **She has not read Cael** and has said that she will know what she is reading for before she does. At the word *station* she shut her mouth on a sentence and said nothing. She did not sit in the sixth place. She does not know that Seln is Shadow's source.
   178	
   179	**Teague and his crew.** He proposed and ran the silent yard to make his people better at fighting with no Path. He sent the young one to learn from Lira. His wrist is bound shut till the thaw on the older healer's order. His term stands. He knows nothing of the mechanism; he has seen a light in the shed and a guard at its door, heard a Wind's crack on two nights, and watched a pair of boots refuse something three times. The tall one, the bow and the older healer have said nothing to the five. Nobody in his crew is named.
   180	
   181	**Pike's book and the cloth.** **Eleven lines:** the tenth is the fence (*forty; paid*), the eleventh the rope-house roof. The season line's number is still blank, "on purpose". **Eleventh on the cloth**, unmoved all cold term. The ice road was "said, not entered".
   182	
   183	**Objects and money.** **A hundred and eighteen marks in hand** (96 − 20 for the cold stay; + 40 for the fence; + 5 for the roof; − 3 for the mule's shed). The reserve sealed and unopened. Owed: sixty yards of four-strand rope to the ferryman, wanted with the first boat; one reed hurdle; a letter to Ephram. Stake thirty-two has still never been driven. Both brass edges are in their cloths.
   184	
   185	**The Quieting, as known.** Unchanged from Book 7's end. Nobody has been near a line in this movement.
   186	
   187	**Who knows what.** The mechanism: six. The anomaly of the cord and the edges: the six. Karis's case: the six. Outside them nobody knows that anything works inside a line. Nobody has said who made the pattern, or why, or what the instruments' behaviour means.
   188	
   189	**Words said aloud for the first time.** *Treswick*; *the Deepstacks* (Karis, the 268th). Not said aloud by anybody: *west*. Not said at all: Cleon's name; Vastin's.
   190	
   191	**Open threads.** A ruling that Cael has put off until daylight. A ferry that runs in a week, and whatever a desk has had a hundred and forty days to send over on it. A provision that nobody has seen used. A sentence that a healer swallowed. A cord that reads short in one hand. Four signs and a mark. A shoulder in another woman's knot. An arm that is slow. A captain's term. A rope, a hurdle and a letter.
