   251	Across the inn yard, by the smith's rail, five people were loading two mules. There were sacks of salt on the snow, and a board lashed along one pack; a tall figure stood at the mules' heads with a grounded spear, and a broad man was easing his weight onto his right leg, and a boy of sixteen or so was trying to lift two sacks where he had been given one. A lean old woman in a cap stood by with a staff that she did not lean on. The man who was testing the girths had a band of black leather laced on his right wrist. Pike had guessed him a Bronze once, under that eave, and had never been told. He finished the buckle that he was at. Then his head came up, and his eyes went over the plank, and the heaps of money on it, and five people standing in front of three slips.
   252	
   253	He lifted his chin to Cael, by the width of a finger.
   254	
   255	Cael lifted his own. It was everything that passed between the two crews that morning. They had the same river to wait for, and each of them knew what the other meant to do when it opened.
   256	
   257	Teague's five went up toward the cold road in file, a crew's length apart, and not one of them looked back.
   258	
   259	"Hands for the roof," said Brom.
   260	
   261	"It's a roof," said Lira.
   262	
   263	"It's five." His left hand was up already. "And it's the only one of the three that we can see from our own door."
   264	
   265	Her hand went up, and Karis's with the pencil in it. Seln lifted two fingers. Cael put up his own, and Pike made five strokes at the slip's foot and said the whole of it twice to the snow.
   266	
   267	---
   268	
   269	He wrote the day that night at the meal-sack end of the deal, by Karis's lamp, while a strip of brass went down a calfskin on the other side of it.
   270	
   271	*Hundred and thirty-third day. Lowmarch, the lower inn. The cold stay is paid: four a month, five months, twenty in front. In hand, seventy-six. The reserve is not counted here. Nine lines, and no number against the season, on purpose. Eleventh on the cloth. The board has a roof, a well-house and a fence. The fence is ours, and we go out to it on the hundred and thirty-eighth. The roof is ours as well, at five, paid when it has kept a thaw out. The mule stands in the ferryman's shed, at a fee he has not asked for and will.*
   272	
   273	He ruled a line under that, and began a new leaf. He headed it as he had never headed one before.
   274	
   275	*The ten, by use. Trials, in the ferryman's shed, at night, with the door kept. Not uses. Not counted.*
   276	
   277	*Wind, from Lira: three bursts across the horse-walk, and then more behind the drum. The landing locks for one count whatever the distance. It can't be shortened. It can be put somewhere. Her word for it: a way of leaving.*
   278	
   279	*Ember, from Karis: one point, on a wick. The heat comes back to the hand that carries the thing. The left palm.*
   280	
   281	*Shadow: came up at dusk as far as my knees. Held. Not opened, and it won't be.*
   282	
   283	*Deployment since the Line: twenty-seven.*
