   285	He read the leaf over. It was a poor thing beside the inventory that he had made four nights ago, which had ten names in it, and a still place, and two promises. This had three fragments and the small plain facts of two evenings in a shed, and under them he had ruled seven lines and left them empty.
   286	
   287	He had made lists all his life of what he was and what he owed. He had never made one of what he did not know, with the gaps left standing in it on purpose; and he saw, with the pencil still in his fingers, who had shown him that a book might be kept so. A one-armed man had turned a ledger round at first snow, in front of fifty people, to show a boy the place where nothing was written.
   288	
   289	On the far side of the lamp Karis had the hide flat under her four pebbles. She was not ruling anything. She had the older strip of brass laid along her pencil line, and she was sliding it up the skin a hand at a time, past the fourth ring and off the edge, onto the bare deal, as though the table might have something to say about where the line went next.
   290	
   291	There were seven more fragments to do. He looked at the room that he had left for them, and then at the figures above, and the thought came back to him that had come on the landing.
   292	
   293	"Every figure that I keep has stopped," he said. "The purse won't move till the fence. The cloth can't move at all. I've nine lines and no post, and a number that I've promised her not to change."
   294	
   295	Karis did not look up. It was Lira who answered him, from the doorway, where she stood on the whole of her foot with a bowl in her right hand.
   296	
   297	"Then count something else," she said. "You've a leaf there with seven empty lines on it. I'd start with those."

