   106	## Changes made while drafting
   107	
   108	- **The hold's ground (ch2) was fixed to one picture with distances that add up.** The fence leaves the yard at the axe-head gate, climbs the hill aslant and runs east along the foot of the rock band; posts stand six strides apart; from the gate to the nearest grey back is 350 yards (Teague's standing run, then twenty-four capped holes). Lira's hay sledge was moved from a knoll by the gate to the last of Teague's posts, about a hundred and fifty yards from the herd, and she calls rather than speaks. Brom's two re-set posts are the twentieth and twenty-first from the gate. Added: a wind-bared rib of rock below the strip, which Cael buries by going below the herd; the cattle's salt trough, which is what draws the calf.
   109	- **Chapter 3 has six scenes.** Teague's window is in two parts (the wrist and the boy; the light in the shed and the cloth). The bench has the older healer on it beside Brom and Lira; the three grey men name the square "the silent yard". **Seln's hazel wand** carries his count of the desk's days (eighteen notches on the 148th, twenty-eight on the 158th, a hundred on the 230th; a second wand at forty on the 270th); with Teague's term it is the two clocks.
   110	- **Lira's no (ch3)** no longer says when she last asked for the ankle; ch6's narration says that she asked once, from a mule's back, and has asked nobody for anything since (Book 7's text has that asking on the 82nd).
   111	- **Chapter 4:** the young one asks Lira on the morning of the second bout; the tall one "calls" a touch taken by grounding the pole once; the pole is shortened to its middle and beats him three to one. On the ice one barrel is lost; Lira's part in the shed is given as told afterward. The kit scene closes the chapter (Karis opens the dead man's ledger for the first time, thirty-four days after Seln gave it to her).
   112	- **Chapter 5:** the cord is given its working (a brass eye, a knot at every fifth stride, five knots to a length, a notched slip of ash for the odd strides); Brom's hardest pull moves the figure by a thumb's breadth, the right way (short). Karis's window is in two scenes. Her interior no longer prints any part of the rule a second time. The sack game is dated the 190th. The ferryman's reminder about the rope and the mule was added before the 263rd.
   113	- **Chapter 6** has six scenes: the log is written in the ferryman's shed on the 270th, after a look with the read at the rotten shelf (a look, not counted, as on the first morning). Nobody at the table on the 268th says the name of the direction; Cael writes it. Oryn stands at the board's end and does not take the sixth place. The ferryman's pay and fee are settled on the 268th.
   114	- **A vocabulary pass** after the draft put the plain names back in about twenty places (fragment, Path, tier, rank, Iron, Copper, Bronze, deployment) and added a short paragraph in ch1 giving the four companions by Path and tier.
   115	- **Markers:** four, as planned. **Deployment:** twenty-eight (140th) and twenty-nine (161st), as planned.
   116	- **Cutaways** came out shorter than the packet's figure: Teague about 1,540 words, Karis about 1,905.

