   128	## New canon minted (flag where marked)
   129	
   130	- **Lowmarch in the cold (flag).** The ferryman's round shed has reed hurdles tied between its posts for the cold; its horse-walk is eight strides across; there is a stone trough inside the door, a mounting block, and a lean-to **rope-house** against it where he keeps his blocks and cordage. The hauling rope lies coiled against the drum. He has watched the river for "twenty cold terms". His ice: the shelf, then a skin that takes the snow, then "one night it goes blue"; it went blue on the 153rd night; he sounds it with an iron bar and marks his track with a double row of spruce boughs ("my green"), which swings wide at the bend above the landing, where a side water comes in under the western bank and the ice never shuts sound. He pulled the green on the 224th. Open water down the middle on the 230th. He has the scow on blocks before the 263rd and begins to knock them out on the night of the 270th.
   131	- **What four a month buys (flag):** the hearth's end; the pot at dusk, a bowl a head; and the crew's names said to anybody who comes asking. Not bread, candles, the back room or the mule. "A cold term's five by my book": twenty, in front.
   132	- **The cold-term board:** *Roof, the ferryman's rope-house; shingles found; five, when it has kept a thaw out.* · *The well-house at the south fork; a door hung and a hearthstone laid; three, and flour.* · the fence at forty. The five take the roof (five strokes). The roof is paid on the 268th and Pike says its line twice: **eleven lines** in his book at the movement's end.
   133	- **The axe-head hold (flag):** two days up the cold road, which follows the north water and then climbs a side valley; a gate with an axe-head nailed to its top bar; a long house under turf and a longer byre with sixty head. Its **factor**: short and thick, a tally stick, a sheepskin; his thumb-mark is shaped like a wedge; he asks no name. The long fence leaves the yard at the gate, climbs a four-hundred-yard hillside aslant and runs east along the foot of a band of grey rock; posts six strides apart. **Teague's crew dug all forty-one remaining holes before the frost and capped each with a flat stone and straw.** Two of Teague's standing posts, the twentieth and twenty-first from the gate, stood a hand north of the line; Brom re-set them. "At the hold above, in my father's time, a dog got in among the calves… It's pasture now."
   134	- **Shale-backs, added to Book 7's account (flag):** a herd will lie up for the cold under a lee, on rock that it has scraped bare; it rises hind end first; a calf in fright makes a thin grinding cry and its plates stand on end; a herd roused by a calf lies down again when the calf is quiet; they will follow a strip of uncovered lichen at about thirty-eight to forty yards in the hour and will take lichen from a cap. Seventeen in this herd, four of them calves. The read was tried at 350, 200 and 60 yards and, in earnest, at a pace and a half: no answer was seen. Karis has entered both and calls neither a test.
   135	- **The silent yard (flag):** a square trodden under the ash, twelve strides on a side, a hazel peg at each corner, a bench from the upper inn on its western edge. Teague's rules: three touches; "a touch is what the one who took it calls"; over the pegs is a touch; nobody strikes what a healer has had her hands on this season. The name is the three grey men's. Bouts on the 148th (three to none) and the 158th (three to one); a sack game on the 190th, three a side.
   136	- **Teague's spear, as seen (flag; see Owner flags):** a long quiet face under a wool cap, pale eyes, big chapped hands; fights with a seven-foot ash pole with a rag bound over one end; turns on the back heel; draws the pole back through the leading hand "like a bolt through a staple"; marks a touch taken by lifting the pole's butt a hand and grounding it once; shortens the grip to the pole's middle and uses both ends.
   137	- **Teague's wrist (flag):** the leather has been on it "since before the boy could lift a pack"; the joint is thicker than its fellow by the width of a finger; it "clicks"; "A Force paid where the line came out of him." The older healer binds it to her board at night and tells him to leave his Path shut till the thaw. A block lies on the second ledge of the cold road, with a herd lying up above it; he will shift it "as a carter would". The young one has brought him a hot cloth for it on every cold night since he joined. In his own window Teague is named as a Force for the first time in this edition. No Force is used on the page.
   138	- **Fragment observations (flag; no new numeric ceiling):** Wind's locked count is the same for four strides as for eight ("it's paying for the stop"). Ember's heat comes back to the hand that carries the lit thing. Compression on one heel leaves half the load in him; "It needs ground worth sending a load to." Pressure goes through what it strikes and on into what is behind it, and comes back off a curved surface. Blade's edge lasts "about as long as a struck match", and the steel pays. Leaned on for long, the read takes his near sight: an hour after the calf, longer after the ice; lying flat it reads through his chest and forearms at the same pace and a half. Sound ice takes a weight "in a wide shallow dish"; rotten ice takes it on one side; frozen ground "rings". A point of Anchor that he has set himself can be taken down with his hand on it.
   139	- **The ice road (flag):** a four-wheeled cart behind two mules, eighty yards out, with a dozen barrels and two pigs of iron for the smith; the carter heavy and grey-bearded, unnamed; one barrel of fish lost. Hauled out on the ferry's own rope and drum by Oryn's brown mule. Pike: "That's said. It's not entered."
   140	- **The kit as a tool (flag):** the cord is tarred hemp no thicker than a pencil, with a knot at every fifth stride and a brass eye whipped into one end; **five knots make a length** (twenty-five strides); odd strides are taken with a notched slip of ash "a stride long by the dead man's reckoning". Karis cut a peg of peeled hazel to the stake's length. The inn yard, from the plank's left-hand post to a scratch on the stone trough by the gate: one length, three knots and two strides. Karis measured the cord against the Ternhall brass on the 116th and again on the 170th: thirty-one lays "and a piece under half" to the first knot, unchanged.
   141	- **The edges (flag):** each strip's length pricked into the deal with a needle. In Cael's hand the Ternhall strip stops a hair short of its prick and Hesk's goes a hair past; the gap is one of Karis's own hairs, which she keeps in her notebook. True in the other four hands, and true again when he takes his hand away.
   142	- **Karis (flag):** her rule dates from the day, "as a girl", when a registrar laid her card before her and could not say what instrument it had been taken with. She once read the archive's name "on the spine of a catalogue that she was not allowed to open". The mark stands "beside the Stair twice over and beside the fourth place", and not beside the Fallow or the Hall (as Book 7's stakes have it). Her leaf: *What I should have to be able to show.*, eleven lines.
   143	- **Her case, as said on the 268th (flag):** "I think that they are instructions." (the same forty-one kinds in the same order on three floors; a line that carries the circle's own measure); a key to the index is kept; "There is a town on the border, inside the Line. It is called Treswick."; an archive under its wall and older than the wall, "cut out of the rock before the registry had an office anywhere"; "The few who have written of it call it the Deepstacks."; she does not think that anybody has been into its oldest part since before the Compact; a provision for scholars, which she has seen cited and never used; she holds "one paper still that such a place might honour: Iron, the third rank, certified and retained." **Seln:** a gate with a book in it; a registry office over the archive; a station on its crown; the paper that executes "on the hour when any of us shows a certificate inside the Line".
   144	- **Thornwater (flag):** the ford in thaw is thigh-deep over the sill, with the white stones and the bar under water; Lira finds the ledge by the smooth water and the line of standing waves below it. The ankle "had knit as it lay in a river, crooked"; an hour. Oryn: "It stops being mine when you take it." She will not undo the older healer's knot and will look at the shoulder "beside her". The bowman comes down off his platform during the mending. The steading's woman counts for a sixth.
   145	- **Roles (all unnamed):** the axe-head hold's factor; the carter on the ice; the ferryman and his boy; the lower inn's keeper; the older healer; the tall one; the bow; the young one; the bowman and the steading's woman at Thornwater; the hide-woman; the three grey men.
