books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-2-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-3-He logged the leg as a leg, in five entries, one a night, because the road was the road and the sites were not, and he wanted the two kept apart on the page.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-4-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md:5:*Two hundred and ninety-ninth.* East from the ford on the bearing, twelve and a mule, the Stair road under them to the Stair's bowl by the last of the light — the rim, the floor below it with its terraces going down, the iron a stride apart round it as the registry had set it; not entered; Karis at the rim with the cord for the hour there was, checking a number she already had; camp on the rim. *Three hundredth.* The road to Oryn's last three holds turned north off the bearing at a cairn at noon, and she looked at it once, straight, and walked on east with the roll on the mule, and nobody spoke, because the terms had been said and the day she had to was not this one. The Hall's marsh by dusk the same day; the stone at its edge where he had read himself twice by a fire in the autumn. *Three hundred and first. Three hundred and second.* Past the marsh, on the bearing, three days of ground none of the six had walked and Teague's four had walked once, in the autumn, carrying a healer to a rival's shoulder; two camps with no water. *Three hundred and third.* The crest.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-6-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-7-He entered, under each date, the same line, because it was true under each: *known*. Not followed — Seln had the count at every halt and there was nothing behind — but read, off a sheet, by somebody who had the sheet, and he did not stop entering it, because a feeling that recurs is a fact about the road.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-8-
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-20-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-21-"They've always known," Seln said. He had come to the crest's lip on his stomach, as he came to everything, and had the nearest stake in his eye from ninety paces, and read it as he read a lock. "That iron's been in the ground years. Not one winter. The scale's through it." He did not say how many years, because he did not have the number and would not make one. "The hut's a season. The fire's tonight. They've been here since the ice went out and they've been told to expect a crew off this crest, and they've been told how many, and it's the wrong number."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-22-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md:23:"How wrong," Teague said. He had come up the crest last, as a captain does, and had read the post before he lay down.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-24-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-25-"They've room round that fire for a watch that expects six," Seln said. "Not twelve."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-26-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md:27:Teague looked at it a while longer. Then he said, to Cael and to nobody: "Drawn?"
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-28-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-29-"Drawn," Cael said, and drew it, because a fight is drawn before it is fought, and because a captain who did not read had asked the one on the crest who did.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-30-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-31-The shelf. A cart's width, with the sink on its left hand — a drop of three men's height onto loose stone — and the iron on its right, and a stride beyond the iron the line, where ten of the twelve on this crest would go dark. The post across the shelf. The barricade across the shelf's narrowest place, with the hut behind it and the fire behind that. Seven. Two crossbows. A practitioner among them, by the way one stood at the barricade's end with his hands empty and his weight set, and low, because nobody posted a Silver to a hut. Nobody could go round. There was no round. Sink, or line.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-32-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md:33:"They've the ground," Teague said. "We've the numbers, and numbers don't count on a cart's width."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-34-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-35-"No."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-36-
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-38-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-39-"Somebody does." Cael looked at the barricade, and at the man's wrapped wrist on the turf beside him, and did not look at the wrist a second time, because the man had priced it and it was his. "Nobody declares. Nobody dies. They're seven people on a posting. Noted?"
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-40-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md:41:Teague looked at him, and it was the look from a marsh, the one that read a job off a face. "Noted," he said, and Cael entered it beside Vastin's.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-42-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-43----
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-44-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-45-They went down at first light on the three hundred and fourth, in the order the shelf took them, because the shelf was the thing they were going to be in.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-46-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md:47:Brom first, with the kit off and the box down on the crest and his shoulders bare of anything but himself, because a man who was going to be a rock did not carry. Then Teague, and Cael at his left hand, a stride back, to watch the exchange. Lira behind them, the flat of the blade out. The tall woman with the spear, then Vastin with the stick, then the broad man with the crossbow, then the young one beside him, because a man on a stick has a man beside him; then Karis with the case under her arm, on the path and on the count, because a wall counted who walked a site and she was the one who had found it; then Seln — and Seln was not on the path; Seln had gone off the crest's east side in the dark an hour before, and was somewhere along the perimeter with the cache, and Cael had a count of nine on the path and one that was not, as he had had at a cut. Oryn and the older woman a hundred paces up the path with the roll open on a rock.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-48-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-49-The watch saw them at two hundred paces, which was late, because they had been looking at the road and the crew had come down off the crest's face. The crossbows came up. The five without them went to the barricade in a line, with the empty-handed one at its end, and the hooded man at the middle of the barricade said the thing a Compact watch says, which is a declaration, at the size a declaration is said, and it went up the shelf to the nine of them, and nobody answered it, because nobody declared.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-50-
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-52-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-53-They had it. Seven people on a cart's width with a barricade and a drop and a line, and nine coming at them with nowhere to go but through. The numbers were nothing. The ground was everything. The watch had been given a place where what was coming did not matter, and had built across it, and had not run, and he did not think less of them for any of it, and entered that.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-54-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md:55:Then Teague went through the middle.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-56-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md:57:He walked the last thirty paces as he had walked across a meadow to a cart, without hurrying, and Cael walked at his left hand a stride back and watched. The five at the barricade set themselves behind the two timbers. The crossbows came up again, spanned, and the bolts came, and Brom, at Teague's right hand now and half a pace ahead, took one on the forearm as a man takes a thing he has decided to take, and the other went between them, and then Teague was at the barricade, and put his palm flat on the upper timber, at the height of his own chest, and set his feet.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-58-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-59-Cael saw the feet. He had seen them in a meadow and he saw them now: square, a stride apart, the weight on the balls of them, all the man from the heels up through the hips and the shoulder to the flat of the hand lined up as if a straightedge had been laid down him. The empty-handed man at the barricade's end saw it too, a half-breath late, and moved, and the timber went.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-60-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md:61:Not the timber. The barricade — both timbers and the posts that braced them and the two men who had their shoulders to the back of it — went, a body's length, which was the length of the man from heel to palm, backward off its footing and into the men behind it and down, and the shelf where the barricade had been was shelf, and Teague stood on it with his hand still out.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-62-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-63-And Cael, at his left hand, a stride back, had it.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-64-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-65-Not the man's. The *adjacent* thing, the thing that lived at a stride's distance from a Path and was not it, that he had been picking up off people since a training floor at fourteen and had twelve of; and he had it as he had had the eleventh in a cut and the twelfth in a chamber, in the middle of the minute where he was not looking, except that this time he had been looking, for all the exchange, from a stride, at a line running from a man's heels to his palm, and the line had gone into him along its own length and rung.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-66-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md:67:*Force-adjacent. Teague. Partial. Bronze. Impulse along the line of the frame at contact: from the ground, through the man, out at the hand, and nothing to either side of the line. Needs a thing to push against — the barricade, the ground under the feet; nothing at a distance; nothing from the air. A body's length. The cost back along the same line, every time, at the place the line comes out. Engagement field: close; braced. Tier equivalent unknown. Deployment: none. Thirteenth.*
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-68-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md:69:Teague shook the wrist. It clicked, and then the elbow did, which it had not in the meadow, and the man's face did nothing, and he took the cloth out of his own coat this time, because the young one was on the path behind him beside a man on a stick, and wrapped the wrist and did not wrap the elbow, because there was not time, and said, to the shelf ahead, "Through," and went.
