books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-90-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-91-"Three." Karis, with the case under her arm, because the wall counted who walked a site and she had found it, and her sigil went at the stride and she did not look at her hand; she looked at the shelf, for the strata.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-92-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md:93:"Four." The young one, because Teague had looked at him once and the look had said *go*, and he went over as the young go over things, all at once, and stood inside with nothing in him and did not know yet what that was.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-94-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-95-"Five." Vastin, over the iron with the stick, on a knee that had been told a month, standing inside a line where a wall could not come, with nothing in front of him but the decision to, which was, Cael thought, the thing the man had been for all along and had spent forty years being for the wrong people.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-96-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md:97:Five inside, two strides in, and four on the shelf level with them — Brom at the sink's lip with the rock in his hand, the broad man beside him with the crossbow across his back and the stick, Teague and the tall woman between them and the iron — and the line ran from the drop across all the shelf and two strides into the quiet, and nothing working in five of them but the one who did not count, and steel, and their feet.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-98-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-99-They walked.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-100-
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-114-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-115-The broad man went over the sink's edge at the fourth second.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-116-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md:117:Cael had not seen it; he had been counting, and looking at the watch. He heard it — a foot on the lip, the limp set wrong on loose ground at the shelf's edge where the line had ended at the sink, on a leg that did not walk well — and the man was gone, over, onto the slope three men's height down, and the loose stone went with him, and Teague, on the shelf a stride from him with the wrapped wrist at his side, did not turn round, because his crew was not the thing on the shelf that wanted doing, and the tall woman did not either, and Cael entered that as the hardest thing he had watched a captain do, and the most correct.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-118-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-119-Oryn was already coming down the path.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-120-
