books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-18.md-18-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-18.md-19-"Six," she said, and he said it back.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-18.md-20-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-18.md:21:"And five that aren't." The look went past him, to the road, where Teague's four stood with the tall woman's spear grounded and the young one looking at the ford as the young look at everything, and to the fifth, the older woman with the cloth bag, who was looking at the lean-to as a woman looks at another woman's table. Oryn looked at her. The older woman looked back. It lasted as long as it took, and neither of them nodded, and neither of them looked away first, and then the older woman looked at Brom's shoulder, once, as she had looked at it in a marsh and across a room, and Oryn looked at it too, and then they had both looked at the same thing and priced it the same, and that was the whole of the exchange between the two healers on the bearing, then or after, and Cael entered it as complete.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-18.md-22-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-18.md-23-"The rest," Oryn said, to the bank, to the six of them, in the register, "can wait till I've done the line. Sit down. Not you." That was to Vastin. "You stand there and don't put the stick down, because you've been putting it down, I can see it from here, and I said a month."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-18.md-24-
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-18.md-36-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-18.md-37-She did not sit. She stood at the edge of it with the roll on the table behind her and the ford at her back, square to the six of them as she stood square to everything, and said the terms, and he had known she would, because she had said them at a fork and at a table and by post, and a woman who says a thing three times at three sizes is a woman who has decided that it needs saying until nobody can mistake it.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-18.md-38-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-18.md:39:"I'm on my route." She said it to the six of them and past them to the road, where Teague stood, because Teague was the one on the road who had not heard it said. "Seven holds north of here owe me a visit and I've walked four of them since the fork and there are three I haven't, and there's an old woman at Thornwater with a knee that held through the winter and a ladder she'll not stop climbing. That's mine. Nobody at this ford gets a share of it." A breath. "Your bearing runs east. So does the road to the last three holds, for two days, and then it doesn't. I'll walk the two days because they're on my way. I'll walk further than that because there's a man on this bank with ribs I've been owed a reading of since a marsh, and because ten people are going into two places that stop Paths at a stride, and I've seen what that costs, and I'll not have it said I was a day north when it cost it." She did not look at Teague when she said the next thing, and it was for him. "I'm not a recruit. I've never been one. I'm a stop on my own route, and the route's mine, and the day I have to turn north I'll turn north, and nobody at this ford will ask me not to, because the one who'd have the right to has already said he won't."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-18.md-40-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-18.md-41-She looked at Cael, then.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-18.md-42-
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-18.md-44-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-18.md-45-"Good," she said.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-18.md-46-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-18.md:47:Teague, on the road, said nothing, which was the right answer, and Cael watched him hear it and file it, because the man filed things too, in his way — a healer of no Path and a healer of Tide, and both of them on their own terms, and neither of them his to move — and the tall woman moved the spear a hand's width, and that was Teague's crew hearing it, and it was done.
