books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md-18-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md-19-"I don't know. Time. More than the office has a column for." She went on.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md-20-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md:21:He catalogued the eleven on the plain's rock, because it was the last halt before the thing and he wanted the count. Brom with the forearm bound and the good one carrying, on a foot that had walked to a city and in one and out of it. Lira with two arms and the third box, and her eyes on the rim ahead and not on him, because she had looked at him at a pass's mouth and had said nothing and had decided to go on saying it until he said something first. Karis with the case and the map, reading the rock as she read everything, for the strata, with the bound hand holding the notebook she had been told not to write in. Seln at the back, where the count was kept, with nothing on the plain to keep it against. Vastin on the stick, the twenty-eighth day of a month, walking as a man walks who has two days left on an order and means to keep them. Oryn with the roll on her back and the mule's rope in her hand, three days past the end of the road that had been hers, walking east because a man's ribs were owed a reading she had said she would not take until he had stood where he was going, and because ten people had walked into four quiet places and she had been in reach for one. Teague and his three — the tall woman with the spear; the broad man on the leg that bore today, as she had said it would, and limped worse, as she had said it would; the older woman with the cloth bag — walking a stride apart from the six, as a crew walks that has buried one of its own the day before and has not yet found the distance it wants to walk at. And himself, with the middle box, and the brass in his coat, and the ribs saying their one thing at every stride, and thirteen things held to nothing, and the count.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md-22-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md-23-Eleven. He entered it at noon, and did not enter the twelfth, because the twelfth was under stone at a pass's mouth and the log had a line for that already and did not need another.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md-24-
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md-38-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md-39-Lira on the line. She had the job from the Ring and the Stair and the fourth: walk the perimeter with her sigil lit, a stride at a time, until it went out, and step back until it came on, and call it. She did it as she had done it at four sites, and it went out at the stride, at a place a pace inside the stumps' inner faces, and she said "Gone," and stepped back, and said "Back," and Karis put a stone down, and they went on. Cord and stride and stone. Brom marking. Seln at the mule with the case, keeping the count of the plain, which was eleven and a mule and nothing else, because there was nothing else, because there was nothing on the plain to hide anything behind, and Seln, who had spent fifteen years using the ground, stood in the one place on the continent where there was no ground to use and did not like it and said so once, to the mule.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md-40-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md:41:Teague's three sat at the rim while the cord went round, on the plain's rock outside the stumps, and did not help and were not asked to, because the pacing was the six's and the contract had said so. The tall woman watched Lira's sigil go out at the stride and come on at the stride back, twenty times, and at the twentieth she looked at her own hand, at the mark on it, and closed the hand, and did not open it again that afternoon. The broad man sat with the leg out and the crossbow across his knees and looked at the floor like water he had been told was deep. Teague stood. He did not sit all afternoon; he stood at the place where the path had come in between two stumps and read the floor as he read a job, and Cael, passing with the cord's far stone, saw him do it, and saw him come to the end of the reading with nothing moved, because there was nothing on the floor to move anyone to, and saw him not like that, and enter it, in his way, which was to stand there longer.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md-42-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md-43-The line was sharp. It was a hair sharp, as it had been at every site: a stride outside, Lira's sigil lit; a stride inside, gone; nothing between. The circle was perfect. Karis said so at the twentieth cord and again at the eightieth, in the voice for a finding, because a circle that stays perfect across eighty cords is a circle and not a guess. And it was larger, and it was older, and she had the first from the cord — "A thousand and twelve. Across. Call it a thousand" — and the second from the stumps.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md-44-
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md-70-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md-71-He had wanted this before the middle, and had said so, and they had given it him: eleven people at the rim of the oldest quiet, a stride outside the stumps, in a row, as ten had stood inside an iron a pace apart at a Span; and he catalogued them at it, one each, because it was the count and the count was going to be the thing he left here when he walked away from it.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md-72-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md:73:The sigils went first. He watched them go, because a healer had said it was what she read for and a woman with a cord had said it was the finding she was afraid of what came after: every mark on every hand at the line going dark at the stride, at the same stride, at the same instant — Lira's, and Brom's, and the tall woman's and the broad man's and Teague's, and Vastin's, on a hand that had held a plane across a stream-bed the day before, and Karis's, on a hand that was bound, and Oryn's. The same. A hundred lamps. He did not say the rest of it and neither did she, and she looked at her own dark hand as she had looked at a column, and entered it, and drew nothing.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md-74-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md-75-Lira. She stood at the line and looked at all of it — not the middle; the *whole*, the rim going round in its stumps and the floor lying level to the far side and the plain beyond — as she had looked at the tops of three walls from a road, and at the Ring from a rim, and at a ladder from a registry's yard, and every one of those times she had looked away, deliberately, because she had a thing about the tops of things and had had it since a hall. She did not look away. She stood with her two hands at her sides and her sigil dark and looked at the largest made thing on the continent for as long as she wanted to, and then she said, to it, "Right," and it was all that she had to say to it, and it was enough.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md-76-
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md-92-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md-93-Nobody answered that. He did not, because he did not know what she meant by it and did not want to, yet, and she did not say it again, because a healer says a finding once, and it stood there between the two of them at the line, unanswered, and the book of the year did not have a column for it, and he let it stand.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md-94-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md:95:Teague went in one stride, first, alone, with the other hand out — the good one — and set his feet as he set them at a cart, and the hand was a hand, and the line from heel to palm was a man's arm and nothing else, and he stood inside with that for the time it took to know it and stepped back over the line between the two stumps.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md-96-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md:97:He did not go in again. He stood a stride back from the line with his three — the tall woman with the spear grounded and lifted, the broad man on his stick with the leg that bore today, the older woman with the cloth bag — and read his people in the old order, face after face, and came to the gap, and went past it, and looked at the floor, and said, at the size he said a term: "My crew's stood at four of these. It's stood at enough." He looked at Cael. "Field's mine. This isn't field." And that was the contract, kept to its last line, by a man who had lost one of four to a line the day before and was not going to put the three across another, and Cael entered it as the contract's best clause, and the man's.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md-98-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md-99-Then the thing began.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md-100-
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md-2-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md-3-They buried him at the east mouth, under stone, because the west mouth was under stone already and the pass had made the choice for them.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md-4-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md:5:Teague's crew did it. Cael catalogued the order, because it was theirs and he wanted it kept as theirs: the tall woman first, with the spear laid down for the first time in a year and a stone the size of her head lifted in both hands; the broad man on his stick, who could not lift and did, one-handed, small stones, one at a time, from the stream-bed to the place, and did not stop when the older woman with the cloth bag put a hand on his good shoulder, so she took it off; Teague last, with the wrapped arm at his side and the other doing the work of two, laying the stone as he read a job, in order, from the feet up, as if the order mattered, which it did.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md-6-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md-7-The six carried at his leave. He gave it without looking up — "Carry, if you're carrying" — and they carried, from the stream-bed to the mouth, Brom with the bound forearm and the good one, Lira with two, Karis with one hand and the case set down for the first time since a bridge, Vastin from where he sat, handing stones up from the ground to whoever was passing, because he could not stand yet and would not not do it, and Seln, who had come down off the shelf's lip when the ridge stayed empty and had not gone back up. And himself, with the ribs saying their one thing at every stone and the stones going on anyway, because the ribs were not the thing that wanted doing.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md-8-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md:9:No one answered the boy's crew had not said. That was the rule and nobody had made it; a crew that has heard a name said once at a stream-bed, by the man it was not said to, does not say it back, and the six had heard it once and would not, and did not ask it again. The stone went on until it was a shape, and the shape was the size of a boy with a pack too big for him, and then Teague put the last one on, at the head, and stood, and the tall woman picked up the spear.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md-10-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md-11-Oryn stood where she had stood. Three strides. Her kit closed. She had not moved from that distance since her hands had come off, and she did not move from it now, and when the last stone went on she did not look away from it, as she had not looked away from it in the stream-bed, and Cael understood that this was what she had instead of the thing she could not do, and that it was not nothing, and that she would not have called it anything.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md-12-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md:13:Lira and Seln went back for the mule. There was a way. The team had used it: up the shelf's stair, along the ridge above the pass, and down the far side of the hills to the west, where the ground came round to the pass's west mouth from above, and the two of them went that way at Teague's last stone without being asked and came back by dusk, the mule between them with Karis's boxes on it and its head down, having stood tied to a rock all day on the far side of a fall with nothing to say about it, because a mule did not know what had happened on the other side of a heap of stone and a dead tree, and was the only one of the twelve who did not, as it had been the only one on a bridge.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md-14-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md-15-Eleven, and a mule. He entered the count. It was the first time all year that the count had gone the other way. He had counted up at every halt since a landing in the snow — five, six, twelve — and had thought of the counting as a thing that went one way, as a ledger's lines do, and it did not, and he had known that in the abstract for five years and had it now in the particular, and the particular was a boy with a pack too big for him.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md-16-
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md-34-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md-35-"I know the difference."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md-36-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md:37:"Good," she said, and went to Brom's forearm, which was the next thing, and he sat with the ribs and the difference and watched her do the crew in order — the forearm; the broad man's shoulder, at the older woman's leave, her hands in the other's work again; Vastin's knee, which she read and said nothing about, because there was nothing on the twenty-seventh day of a month that had not been on the twenty-sixth; Karis's hand, which she rebound and said "You've been writing with it" and Karis said "Yes" and that was the whole of that — and did not do Teague, because Teague was at the fire's far side with his three and his back to the plain, counting his people, and coming up one short, and not saying the number.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md-38-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md-39-Cael could see him not say it. A man who reads his crew in order, face after face, does not stop reading them because there is one fewer; he reads them in the old order and comes to the gap and goes on past it, and the going-on-past has a shape, and Cael had watched the man for a year and could see the shape from across a fire. He did not enter the number either. It was not his to enter.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md-40-
