books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-12-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-13-*The two inns have priced the winter. The upper inn wants a mark a head a week and the lower inn wants the same, and Pike, who keeps the board's room at the lower inn and has chosen the smoke over the cold, said to the book without looking up, "Meadow rate is four a month for a crew that's on my sheet," and the woman who keeps the lower inn said, "Pike," and Pike said, "Eleventh," and that was the price: four a month for the five of us, twenty for the five months, paid in front, which leaves seventy-six. Brom paid it out of the pouch and counted it twice and the woman counted it once, and the five of us have a room with a floor and a fire and a door that shuts, and Karis has the back room for the sheets, and the boxes are under the bed where the mule can't get at them, and the mule is in the ferryman's shed for a mark a month that he has not asked for and will.*
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-14-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md:15:*Teague's crew have the upper inn. Four of them and the healer of no Path, at the table by the window, with the winter road's pay on it. Teague looked across the yard at the five of us going in under our own roof and lifted his chin a finger's width, which is the whole of the etiquette between crews in a town with one street, and is, in that town, a sentence. His term stands: spring, whatever's on that bearing, his crew comes. He said it at the plank and I said the one word back, and the season line above our names stayed blank, which was a thing Pike had done on purpose, and a thing Pike does on purpose is a thing.*
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-16-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-17-*Hundred and fortieth day.*
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-18-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-19-*Pike's board in winter. Eleven slips where there were forty. A roof. A well-house. Wood cut and stacked for the upper inn. A fence, ours, forty, held over from the autumn. Nothing that needs a Path and nothing that would let you use one, and the board does not care, because the board has never once asked what a crew was and is not going to start when the river is a road.*
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-20-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md:21:*We walked the fence. Two days out on the winter road to the axe-head hold, with the ground iron underfoot and the posts Teague's crew had set in the autumn standing in a line to where they had stopped, and Brom read the line like a wall, and said, "Straight," and we set the rest. Forty-one posts. Seln found the last two of Teague's posts a hand off true and said nothing to anyone but Brom, and Brom reset them, and that is the kind of thing that goes in a ledger nobody reads. The hold's factor paid forty at the gate, in silver, and wrote it in a book of his own, and did not ask for a name, because the slip said Pike's five and that was the name.*
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-22-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-23-*A hundred and sixteen in hand. Fence: paid. Pike wrote PAID and the five names under it and the line under that, and that was the tenth line under our names on his page, and he did not say tenth, because the season line above it is still blank, and he was not going to write a number under a blank he had left on purpose.*
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-24-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md-196-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md-197-He took it by its corner and swung it on the bench, so that the writing faced the other way. Cael had read every line of that book upside down for a hundred and twenty days, across an oak plank. It lay the right way up before him now. He read the season in a square black hand, and at the end of it a ruled space with nothing in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md-198-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:199:"Teague says you need a column I don't have. I've kept this ledger thirty years. First time it's been wrong about a crew by being too small."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md-200-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md-201-He did not say it sadly. He said it as he had once said what a Bronze was worth who lost a carter: as arithmetic.
