editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md-226-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:227:It was the third time that he had given that man the word. Teague nodded, by the width of a finger, and went back to his bench. The boy who sat there had stopped with a heel of bread half way to his mouth. He did not start again until his captain was down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md-228-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md-229-Brom had not waited for any of it. He had gone straight down the room to the lean old woman, as he was told at a fork. He stood in front of her like a boy with a torn coat. She did not get up. She looked at four yards of webbing, once, from the top turn to the bottom, and at how he held the arm inside it; then she went back to her bowl.
