editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md-233-Pike had heard, and he scratched the stump of his arm through its pinned sleeve.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md-234-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:235:"Fence. Forty. Teague says it's yours if you want it; he's got the cold road," he said, to the fire, in the size of voice that he kept for things nobody had asked him. "It's the long one, east, with the axe-head on its gate. He'd a day's posts still to set when he went off after you, and there's the whole far side to mend before the thaw. A hold will feed a crew that's mending its fence."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md-236-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md-237-All four of them looked at Brom. He had asked a one-armed man once what a board paid somebody with no Path at all, and had carried the answer against his skin for seventy days.
