books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-02.md-52-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-02.md-53-"Nothing," Vastin said. "Out here. Nothing yet."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-02.md-54-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-02.md:55:"Then he's nothing yet." Pike wrote a line in the book, which was not a line about Vastin, and blotted it. "Meadow's free till the tents fill. Upper inn's full — Teague's crew has it — and the lower's a mark a night in the season for a man alone, and the board's a plank; anyone can stand at it. It doesn't ask. It writes what gets done." He looked up once more, and for a breath Cael thought he had seen something — the name, or the stance, or forty years of a thing coming off a boat — and then Pike said, "You'll want to eat. Door end," and went back to the mail.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-02.md-56-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-02.md-57-Vastin stood at the plank a moment longer than he needed to. Then he picked up his pack and went to the door end, past Cael, past Seln, and sat at the table by the road door with his back to the wall and his stick against his knee, and did not look at anyone, and a woman brought him bread and he paid for it, and Cael watched the man who had commanded the Compact's enforcement for the length of Cael's life count out a copper and a half on a plank in a town on no map, and put the change in his coat.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-02.md-58-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-02.md:59:Teague's crew came down from the upper inn while the bread was still on the table, because a ferry was a thing every crew came down for, and they came in by the road door in the order they walked in — the tall woman with the spear first, the broad man with the crossbow and the limp, the young one, Teague last, the healer of no Path not with them — and each of them, passing the door end, looked at the brown coat for exactly as long as a crew looks at a man alone at a plank town, which is the length of a step, and did not look again. Teague looked a step longer. He had spent a career reading what a man's hands had done, and the hands on the table had signed things, and Teague saw that, and filed it wherever Teague filed things, and went to the plank to read the fourteen slips as if there were forty.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-02.md-60-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-02.md:61:Vastin ate his bread. He did not look at Teague's crew and he did not look at the fire, and Cael understood, watching him, that the man was doing a thing he had done ten thousand times in a career and never once for himself: sitting in a room as the least of the people in it, and letting the room decide when it was ready.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-02.md-62-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-02.md-63-Lira came in from the yard with the mule's bucket and stopped in the door.
