books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md-76-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md-77-Teague was already up.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md-78-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md:79:He came to the plank as he had come to it in the snow, before the room had decided what the term was; and the tall woman with the spear at the table behind him did not turn round, and the broad man with the crossbow shifted his weight off the limp and did not turn round either, and the young one at the table's end put down what he was eating and looked, because he was young and had not yet learned not to, and the fifth — the older woman with the cloth bag — looked at Brom's shoulder across the room, once, and went back to her cup.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md-80-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md-81-"Spring," Teague said.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md-82-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md-83-"Noted," Cael said.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md-84-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md:85:That was all the term, restated, and neither of them made it larger. Teague put a hand flat on the plank's edge, the wrist of it thick and the fingers square, a labourer's hand on a captain, and said the rest, which was the part with a price in it.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md-86-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md-87-"Ten of us on a wall's count. Your six and my four. Your researcher's on it, because a wall counts who walks a site and not who swings, and she's the one who found the site. Healers don't go on a wall's count — yours is on a north road and mine won't thank you for putting her on it." He did not look round at her. "Shares by count, whichever way the site pays, and I'll not argue the researcher's share, because I've read her map and she's the reason there's a site. Field's mine. I've had a crew at the top of that sheet three years and you've had one eleventh, and the wall's the wall, and I'll not pretend otherwise to be polite. The reading's hers." A tilt of the head toward Karis, who had not stopped at the plank and was already at the back room's door with the case. "I don't read. If she says a thing's old, I'll take it that it's old, and I'll walk to it, and my crew walks where I do."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md-88-
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md-96-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md-97-"It is."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md-98-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md:99:Teague went back to his table, and the tall woman moved the jug a hand's width to make room for his elbow without looking at him, which was the whole of a crew, and Cael stood at the plank and priced it honestly, because the ledger was going to be his and the ledger did not take a price that had been softened at a plank.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md-100-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md:101:Teague was the better field captain. He had been for three years and he was now, and the difference had not closed in a winter, and the thing was not courage and was not Path and was not luck. It was that he read a job off the faces of the people who were going to do it before they had done it, and moved them to where the job was going to be, and did not say so. It had not got smaller. The rival clock still ran — in the young one's look, and in Seln's look at the man's back, and in the sheet on the wall with Teague's four at the top and the five names eleventh — and it would run along all the bearing, and he did not enter it as a fault, because a clock was not a fault. It was a count. And it was a contract: a man who had walked three days along a bearing in the autumn with a healer of no Path to bind a rival's shoulder, and had set a term in the snow and wintered keeping it, and had now stated it again at the size he had stated it before, was not a friend, and had said so, and was owed the honesty of being taken at his word. Cael took him at it.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md-102-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md-103----
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md-104-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md-105-The cart was in the meadow the next morning, before the table, and he catalogued it because it was the first time he had seen the thing done.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md-106-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md:107:A carter's cart, loaded, off the landing with a hide-load for the holds east, with the near wheel down to the hub in the soft ground at the meadow's edge where the ice had gone into the grass and not out of it. Two of Teague's at the wheel — the broad man with the limp and the young one — with their shoulders to it, and the wheel not coming, because a wheel down to its hub in spring ground does not come for two men's shoulders; and Teague coming across the grass from the landing without hurrying, and putting the two of them aside with a hand each, not roughly, and standing at the cart's tail.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md-108-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md-109-He did not set his shoulder to it. He put one palm flat on the tail-board at the height of his own chest, and set his feet, and Cael, at the inn's door with the log, saw the feet: square, a stride apart, all the man lined up from the heels through the hips through the shoulder to the flat of the hand, as if somebody had run a straightedge down him and found no daylight. And the cart went.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md-110-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md-111-Not far. A body's length, which was the length of the man from heel to palm; it came up out of the rut and forward a stride and a half onto firm ground and stopped, and the carter's beast, which had not been asked, took a step to keep the traces from going slack and looked round at the cart as an animal looks at a thing that has done what it was not built to do.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md-112-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md:113:Teague stood a moment with the hand still out. Then he took it back, and shook the wrist, once, and Cael heard it click.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md-114-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md:115:It was not loud. It was the sound a knee makes on a stair, a small dry sound in a wrist that was forty years old and had been putting a cart's weight through itself for twenty of them. The young one had a cloth out of his coat before the sound had finished, and Teague took it without looking and wrapped the wrist, twice round, tight, and tied it off with his teeth and the other hand, and gave the cloth back, and all of it had been done by two people who had done it a great many times and did not discuss it.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md-116-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md-117-Force. He had the card for it from a marsh and the thing itself now, and they agreed. Not a Path a man carried in his body, as Iron went, or set in front of it, as a wall. A Path that went *through* him: impulse, at the moment of contact, along the line of the frame from the ground to the hand, and nothing to the side of the line, and nothing at a distance, and nothing at all without a thing to push against. And the cost came back along the same line it had gone out on. The wrist clicked because the wrist was where the line came out, and the man had a boy with a cloth, and that was all of Force at Bronze in one cart in one meadow.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md-118-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md:119:"You'll have seen that," Teague said, coming past the door. He did not stop.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md-120-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md-121-"I've seen it."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md-122-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md:123:"Then you know what it costs. Don't put it in front of anything that needs doing twice." And he was through the road door and at his table with the wrist under it, where nobody had to look at it, and the tall woman had moved the jug again.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md-124-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md-125-Cael entered the line, from the heel to the palm, and the fact that everything the man had went out along it and everything he paid came back along it, because there was a place ahead on a bearing where a thing with one line and one cost might matter, and he did not know yet how.
