books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md-130-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md-131-He catalogued it from the far mouth, where he had walked to because it was east and east was where the thing was that he had come for, and because he could not stand where the others were standing and be any use, and because the ribs wanted him to walk and not to bend.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md-132-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md:133:Brom on a stone with the forearm bound, looking at the fall that had closed the mouth behind them, pricing it, because Brom priced everything and the fall was a thing that had been built by hands. Lira on the shelf's stair, sitting, with the blade across her knees and her two hands on it, looking at nothing. The tall woman on the shelf with the spear grounded, not looking down, because a crew does not look at its captain when he is doing the thing he was doing. The older woman with the cloth bag kneeling by the broad man, whose shoulder she had bound, whose stick was beside him, who was not looking at anything either. Seln on the shelf's lip with the cache, watching the ridge, because somebody had to and it was always him.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md-134-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md:135:And Teague, in the stream-bed, on his knees by his own dead.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md-136-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md-137-He knelt as he read a job. That was the thing Cael had watched him do since a board's room in the autumn — read his people in order, one face after another, before a thing was decided, and move them to where it was going to be — and he was doing it now, with one of them, on the stone, in the order he had always done it, except that the face did not read back, and the man knelt there anyway with the wrapped arm at his side and the good hand on the boy's chest where a healer's had been, and did not say anything, and did not look up, and the pass let him.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md-138-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md-56-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md-57-They were a day old. The dew had softened their edges, but the gravel gave way there to a strip of grey silt, and the silt had kept everything. Four people had come up out of the river in single file, each a crew's length behind the one in front, and two mules after them on one rein. He crouched, as far as five bands of linen would let him, and read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md-58-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:59:The first prints were a man's, of the middle size, set down evenly. Beside the right foot, here and there, was a small round dent, where the haft of a hand-axe had swung at his thigh and touched ground on the slope. The second walker had grounded a spear at every step. The third set of marks was shorter on the left side than on the right by the width of two fingers, every stride, and no deeper for it; whoever made them had been favouring that hip for so long that the body no longer argued. The fourth were the smallest feet of the four and went in the deepest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md-60-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md-61-"That's the boy," said Lira, over his shoulder. She was standing on one boot and one bare foot, with her arms wrapped round herself. "He's carrying more than the others. Look how his heels go in."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md-62-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md-298-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md-299----
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md-300-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:301:Teague counted his three on the sill, before he looked at the room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md-302-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:303:He did it without turning his head. He had done it so for longer than the boy had been able to lift a pack, at every door and gate and ford, and he no longer knew that he was doing it until it was done. The spear was on his left, half a pace back, grounded. The bow was on his right and a full pace back, standing on the good leg, as the bow always stood when there had been a day's walking. The boy was between and behind, where he had been told to be, and by the sound of him he was already looking for the table with the food on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md-304-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md-305-Three. Whole, and fed, and none of them carrying anything they had not set out with.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md-306-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md-307-That was the first thing, and the second was the job.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md-308-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:309:It was a week, and he had the whole of it in his head already, a stage at a time, as he would have had a load. Salt went up to the top holds before the frost shut the pass, and it went on mules, and the mules went on a track that was a ledge for two miles of its length with a wind across it. He had done it nine times, and the price was fair. The bow's leg would stand a week of that if he set the pace for the leg and gave some other reason; and the boy would carry more than his share and have to be stopped, and the spear would say nothing at all for seven days and miss nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md-310-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md-311-The third thing was the plank, and he liked it less.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md-312-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md-332-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md-333-The small pale one had a notebook open on the table, in an inn, among cups, and a skin of some sort pinned under her fingers. As he watched she copied something from the one to the other, and looked at what she had written, and ruled a line beneath it with real satisfaction.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md-334-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:335:Teague felt the corner of his mouth begin to go, and stopped it. He had seen that look before, on people who kept tallies, and he had always got on with them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md-336-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md-337-Four.
