editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-158-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-159-Four people were coming down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-160-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:161:She knew the first of them, as anybody on those roads did. It was Teague, off the north water, with a string of two mules behind him and his three; and she gave them what she gave everything on legs, which was a look from a stride and a half as they drew level. A spear, carried upright and grounded at each step, by somebody who never hurried. A boy who was all wrists. And a bow, on the back of one who walked a hair short on the left, at the hip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-162-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-163-Her fingers woke for that. She had not touched it, and she did not need to. It was old, ten years or more; the joint had been out and gone back in by itself, a little wrong, and grown a seat there. She knew what it would cost to unmake that. She knew, too, that the captain had stopped his walk by half a pace to let her see it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-164-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-232-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-233----
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-234-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:235:It was still the eighty-fourth day, and the light was going off the hill above Oxhollow, when Teague counted his three. He had done it before the healer's mule was out of hearing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-236-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:237:They were behind him on the hill. The spear had not changed pace for the stop. The boy had both mules by one rein and was looking back up the road after the woman, with his mouth a little open, as he looked after everything. The bow had stood square on both legs for as long as the healer's eyes were on that hip, and now the weight had gone over to the right again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-238-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-239-Three. Whole, and fed at noon, and one of them no better off than a week ago.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-240-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-254-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-255-There was no cart. That was the first thing, and it was the largest. He had heard on the road that they had hired the smith's, and there were three boxes standing against the gate-house wall on the bare ground, and a crew that has wheels does not stand its goods on the ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-256-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:257:The mild one was by the gate-post. Teague found him at once, this time, and was pleased with himself for a breath, until he saw that the man had been looking at him since the shoulder of the hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-258-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:259:The big one sat on the well's coping with a box-rope coiled over his knee. His left wrist had a cuff of clean linen on it, tied with a knot that Teague knew, since he had seen it on his own people. Mended, then, and under orders. The girl was on a trough. She had one boot, and the other foot was bare and strapped, crosswise, by somebody who had been shown how. It was on its whole sole. He had read those feet in an inn as the quickest thing in the room, and he would not have laid a copper on them now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-260-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-261-The small pale one was asleep sitting up, with her head on a box and a book shut under her hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-262-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-263-There were four of them, and he had counted them rightly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-264-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:265:The old keeper was on his bench by the stone house, with a tally-stick, and he gave Teague the rest in the time it took two mules to pass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-266-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-267-"It's a no," he said. "Over the top. I've paid seventy to be told so, and I'd pay it again. They put their own cart in my gorge finding out."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-268-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-270-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-271-"All five. On their feet, the most of them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-272-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:273:Teague went on down to the water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-274-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-275-So he had it, and there was nothing in it that a man could not have read off a yard and a keeper. They had cleared a ford with five, in a way that nobody on those roads had cleared one before, and they had walked a mountain and brought back a true answer; and they had lost a cart doing it, and come down with three of them marked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-276-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-278-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-279-The first was the two jobs, set side by side with the same number on each. It would cost him nothing, and it would tell the boy that he was being read, and by what. That was fair. The boy had read him across a room and not troubled to hide it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-280-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:281:The second cost Teague something, and he had known that he would pay it since the fifty-seventh day. The ford had been good work. To say so aloud was to say that the slip had gone where it ought, and that he had no claim left on it, even in his own head. It was true, and a true thing was owed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-282-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-283-The third would land hardest, and he turned it over longest. He did not mean it unkindly. A cart was not a carter. Nobody lay in that gorge. But a crew that went out with another man's wheels and came back without them had done a thing that the board would enter, and it was better that they heard the word for it from him, on a river bank, than read it on a wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-284-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-285-The fourth was the only one that he might have kept. It gave something away. It told five people at the bottom of a sheet that the name at the top expected to stand on a road beside them before the cold term was out; and it set a term on that, which was his to set, since he had three behind him who would be standing there too.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-286-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:287:He looked back, once. The spear. The boy. The bow, on the good leg.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-288-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-289-He decided to say it. A man who meant to share a contract owed the other crew the warning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-290-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-291-The boy was sitting on the gravel at the water's edge, alone, with a book on his knee. He held one arm in a little against his left side, and under the shirt, when he breathed, something drew a line across his ribs. There was a healer's tape on him. That meant ribs; and ribs, by her rule, were three weeks under linen, counted from whichever day the mountain had done it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-292-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:293:Teague still could not tell what he was. A Force stood one way and a Wind another, and there was no Path in the set of that boy that a man could name, and no want of one. He went down the bank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-294-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-295----
