--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-318-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-319-He nodded, the width of a finger. Then he walked on into the river, and his three came after him, each at the same distance from the next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-320-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:321:They crossed without a sound. Cael watched it as he would have watched a thing well made. The one with the spear went second and grounded it at every step. The one with the bow walked a little short on one side and kept the pace regardless. A boy of sixteen or so brought the two mules through on a slack rein, and not one of the four looked down at the water. They had kept the hold on roads since before he knew there was one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-322-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md-323-He opened the book again while there was light.
