editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md-18-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md-19-It was a little after noon when Seln came down off the rise, where he had been since the cord was coiled.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md-20-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:21:"Five, on the track," he said. "They are in file, a crew's length apart, with two mules. One has a spear. One favours a leg. The fifth I do not know; it is a woman, not young, who carries a staff and does not lean on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md-22-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md-23-They came over the crown of the rise as he finished. Cael knew the man in front by the way he stopped there, with his thumbs in his belt, and looked at everything before he came on. He looked at the hall first, and for some time. Then his eyes came down the old ruts to a rope of stiff breeches between two bundles of reeds. They went to a big man lying on his back with an arm strapped across him, and to a girl on a grey stone with a crossbow on her knee. Last of all they went, as they always went, to the number of people who were there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md-24-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:25:He left his three on the rise. They sat down where they stood, a crew's length apart, and the boy got out his bread. Teague walked down the ruts with the woman at his shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md-26-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md-27-"You're a long way off any road," said Teague.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md-28-
