editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md-30-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md-31-"I walked it after you. A child could have." His eyes did not leave Cael's face. "There was nobody else on it. I'd have you know that I looked."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md-32-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:33:The woman had not waited to be made known to anybody. She was sixty, or near it, and as lean as a rake. Her grey hair was knotted up under a cap, and the satchel on her hip had been mended more often than Teague's coat. She had gone straight past the fire to the blanket where Brom lay, and she stood over him with her head on one side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md-34-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md-35-"Whose is that?"
