editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md-165-The tenth did not stir, since it never asked for anything. But he knew what was within his reach, as a man with a key in his pocket knows a lock when he passes one. It would take two fingers, and a count of ten. At three he would have this man's banks under his hand, and at ten he would know the Path as he knew a friend's step. In a minute more he would have what ran in it, and how deep. That was the Bronze which a one-armed man had guessed at under his eave, and had never been told. A registrar would have given a year's pay for what lay a foot from his fingers. He could have had the whole of it and told nobody.
