editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md-286-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md-287-They did not talk. He noticed that first, and it was a good hour before he believed it. An old man led, with two mules, and behind him came a boy of fifteen or so with one, and then the woman; and when the old man wanted the boy he did not call back to him, but lifted a hand, and the boy looked. Once the woman clicked her tongue, twice, and the old man slowed. From the middle of the morning until the sun stood in the west Cael heard no word spoken above the creak of the wheels.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md-288-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:289:They wore steel, and they wore it outside. The old man had a hatchet through his belt, over his coat, with the edge forward, and the woman carried a long knife on her thigh and had a boar spear slung in rings along the cart's side, where one pull would free it. Even the boy had a hedging bill, too big for him. None of it was hidden and none of it was held; it was simply there to be seen, like the courier's hatchet in its loop.
