editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md-81-The price came first, and it was a hundred, which was too much. A surveyor's kit was a thing that one man carried on a strap; he had seen such a kit once, years ago and a long way west. It was two days out to that rise and two home, on a road he knew, and nobody paid a hundred marks for four days and a case. But *too much* was only a reason if a man could say what it was too much for, and he could not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md-82-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:83:The hirer was the broker, by the wax. Teague had carried that man's bales. He paid to the day, and argued every copper beforehand and none after; and he had never in his life sent a crew farther than his own landing. That was odd, and it was not wrong; a broker posts for whoever puts money in his hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md-84-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:85:The ground he knew. He had been round the Fallow Ring eleven times, with carts, by the ruts, at noon, and had looked across the grass each time at a row of grey stumps and kept walking. Every carter's child knew what was said of it. He had never put it to the proof, and he did not need a paper to tell him why not: he had three behind him, and in that place the spear would be a man with a spear, and the bow a man with a bad hip, and the boy a boy. He would not take them in for a hundred, or for twice a hundred.
