editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md-103-They would be at the ferry tomorrow. He had kept a day ahead of them from Oxhollow without trying to, and he knew their pace, with a taped boy and a girl on one boot. The boy would read this plank as he had read a doorway, from one end to the other, leaving nothing out. He would find the stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md-104-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:105:Teague had said four things to him on a river bank, and every one of them had been counted before it was spoken. This was not a thing that could be counted. A captain did not walk up to another crew and hand it a feeling, as though a feeling were a figure; if he had, he would have been asked what it rested on, and rightly, and he would have had no answer but the hair on his own neck.
