editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md-321-## 8. Major fights and set pieces: planning allowances (private staging the author owns)
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md-322-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md-323-- **The cut (M3).** A hundred strides of road cut to a cart's width, with scree on both sides and a frozen runnel a stride wide down the middle. A sink lies fifty-five yards below [B7-reconciled 2026-10-11: amendment 6] on the north side, iced and in shadow; there are eight hounds. Positions: Brom on stone at the south edge with a rock; Vastin a stride behind him; Lira on the south scree; Seln on the north scree, unseen; Cael twenty strides west, past the sink; Karis, Oryn and the mule at the east end. The decisions: the wall goes up and everything turns to the loudest thing; Brom and Lira work its south end silently; Cael bursts **away**, then goes still on the scree as the lock beat lands; the pack returns to the wall and breaks on it; the heap; the runnel. The opponent learns too: the den has learned the road. Costs to carry: Brom's foot; Vastin's knee (a month); Cael's burst bill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:324:- **The alley (M5).** Twenty-two steps in five flights, a stride and a half wide, walled three men high with no doors. There are four at the top (a crossbow and a signaller on the landing) and two coming up from the yard gate with staves. The plane sits at the second flight's top step, wall to wall; Cael stands two feet below it. Brom has a loose step-stone. Lira drops from the second flight. Seln's cord comes from the roof onto the signaller's arm and the rail. It lasts "a minute and some". The opponent's plan was built for a declaration and meets a wall and a rock. Costs: Karis's hand; Vastin past his limit; Brom's good shoulder used wrong.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md-325-- **The Span (M8).** A shelf a cart's width wide, with the sink's three-man drop on the left and the iron on the right; the line lies a stride past the iron. A barricade of two timbers stands at the shelf's narrowest; past the fire is a pinch a stride and a half wide, then a perimeter road a pace wide. There are seven defenders: two crossbows and an empty-handed low practitioner. Exchange one belongs to the watch. Exchange two: Teague's line, with Cael at his left a stride back. Exchange three: width (five inside two strides in, four on the shelf; walk; eleven seconds). Then the fall. **Fairness is entered before the act:** nothing is gained inside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md-326-- **The pass (M9).** A dry defile a hundred strides long with a loose-stone stream-bed. The west wall is scree to a shelf two-thirds up, carrying two timber-braced stacks, one with a roped dead pine; the east wall is sheer. The wall is a lean-to slant, high edge at the west wall ahead, low edge at the mouth behind. There are seven numbered volleys: Brom's forearm (1); the wall up (2); the fall (4); the broad man (5); the young one (6); Seln over the lip (7). Cael's push takes the second stack along the shelf, not off it. Teague goes up the stair left-handed; the tall woman reverses her spear. The team breaks along the ridge. **Win sentence:** Seln removed the team's ability to hold the high ground by climbing the slope its own rig had swept clean, and the wall bought him the climb.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md-327-- **New set pieces** (shale-back fence; ice road; silent yard; Lira's bout; ferry landing; the tail; the seep). Each develops a problem the reader can follow: terrain, positions, objective, limit, a change of decision, and a cost carried forward. **Never put a Path or declaration on display that a sequence lock forbids** (§13).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md-328-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md-329----
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md-330-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:331:## 9. The record passages: exact limits
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md-332-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md-333-- **The leaf is read aloud once**, by Karis, in the chamber, on the 293rd morning (M6). It is not quoted again by anyone who heard it. Cael gives Lira the page to read; nobody says it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:334:- **"Four hundred years"** appears only in the record's voice (the leaf's head, given in Karis's single paraphrase) and at the fixed positions: Cael's pole-two sentence, the 298th log, the 305th close dialogue and the closing prose. Do not add uses.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:335:- **"The Architect"** is the record's **title** ("a title, not a name; the name's gone"). After the leaf it appears again only as a historical comparison ("as old as the Architect"). Nobody uses it as a present agent.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md-336-- **The chronicle's hole stays a hole.** "Marked." There is no gloss, guess or second source.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md-337-- **The un-bound / [UNBOUND]:** the word is live as a historical *kind* in the leaf. Before that, Karis uses it once as a stratum limit ("Older than [UNBOUND]") at Cleon's table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md-338-- **Two pages, no line.** The leaf and the lamps finding are never joined: not in dialogue, not in any interior (including cutaways), not in the narration. Vastin's sentence stops twice (in the chamber; on the shale). The words *terminal*, *circuit* and *infrastructure* are never used for Arbiters.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md-363-(Notice typography follows the edition's convention for system text. The notices must read aloud as prose.)
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md-364-15. **Hesk's dividers note (S14):** "A rule says a line is true. A divider says how far, and carries it. You've the first. Here's the second, and mind the points; they'll go through a thumb as easy as a sheet. Lines are for finding out how far a thing is from another thing. Everything is. — H."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md-365-16. **The grade (S17):** "A mark is a grade. The one they give a boy at fourteen says what he's *for*." … "The one on a sheet says how *old* the thing is." / "Somebody laid them down in order" / "It's observation. I'm telling you what the paper says. Not what it means." / Teague: "Oldest last." … "Then that's where we're going."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:366:17. **Pole two (S16):** Lira: "So the whole time they weren't afraid you'd break something. They were afraid you'd *exist*." Cael: "Since my Kindling I thought the system forgot to make room for me," … "It didn't forget. It was *built* so there'd be no room. Four hundred years of a whole world, so that I wouldn't happen." … "I happened anyway." / Lira: "Yeah." … "Now what." / Cael: "Now we find out how old the oldest one is. Karis has a bearing and the marks, and the records to read them against. There's a site at the end of the line that's been quiet since they built the machine." … "I want to stand in the middle of it." [B7-reconciled 2026-10-11: OWNER-pending #48]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md-367-18. **Vastin at the fire (S16):** "I believed it was imperfect." … "Now you're telling me it was *aimed*." … "At a category. Which turned out to be a person. Who's sitting across the fire." / Lira: "You signed the mark at kindlings." / "I did." / "His?" / "No. I read it, after. I did not sign it." … "I would have." / "Yes," Lira said. "You would have."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md-368-19. **Ribs (S22):** "That's a line," she said. "That's not yours." / "It's mine now."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md-369-20. **Teague's lesson (S21):** "A wall keeps everyone behind it, and one of mine wasn't."
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md-388-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md-389-### Tier C — source-fixed lines and closers (keep verbatim)
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md-390-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:391:- **Ch1 closing log (270th):** "The ferry runs when the ice goes. Karis has a bearing, an index she can't read, and a language she can't read, and she says the answers are in a border archive nobody's opened since before the Compact. Since my Kindling I've stayed out of registries because a registry is where they file you. She wants me to walk into the oldest one on the continent. The kit reads everyone but me. So does the world, mostly. Nineteen. Ice goes next week. West, and inside, for the first time since the academy above Ostrand — which is a sentence I did not think I'd write again." [B7-reconciled 2026-10-11: OWNER-pending #48] And the 168th: "Claim: something. Evidence: a cord, two edges, a hair. Ruling: hold."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md-392-- **Thornwater:** "Five months and twenty days." [OWNER-pending #56] / "That's a season. Take it off." / "Will you mend it." / "Lie still" / "It's an arm. Use it. It'll be slow for a month and it'll ache in the wet for a year and then it won't. Don't fall on it." / "I don't know what that is." … "Neither do you. Write that down too."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md-393-- **S2 doorway:** "You came through the board," / "I came through the board." / "Then you already know it doesn't care who you were." / "I'm learning," Vastin said. **S2 close:** "Because a council would file it," Vastin said, "and I spent forty years being the man they sent to make sure the filing held. I know exactly how deep a thing has to be buried before no one goes looking. This is deeper than that." … "I helped bury smaller things. I'd like to dig one up before I'm done." Also: "Not that. Not for some months. Vastin will do; it did for my father." / "Three hundred miles is a demonstration." / "Not *will not*. *Cannot.* There is no key."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md-394-- **S4 lesson and log:** "The wall held the line," … "And someone else had to be the reason they wanted the line." … "I have never once in forty years needed someone else to be the reason." / "You do now," / "I know fields. I don't know packs." … "I'll learn or I won't." Log: "Vastin fought. He rooted the road and nearly got eaten for it — a wall's no good if you're the only thing making noise behind it. He knows fields. He doesn't know packs. He'll learn or he won't. Watched an Iron Wall go up two feet from me. Filed it. Note the day: an Archmarshal took a wound out here and there was no one to report it to. He looked almost free. Almost."
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md-404-- **S14 log lines:** "Hesk sent dividers. Lines are for finding out how far a thing is from another thing. Everything is. I'll enter that as a rule and see what it's worth." / "The promise stands. Not the steps. Not the square." Also: "Out here you count who's owed a seat," … "It's a different ledger."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md-405-- **S17 log:** "Karis cracked the grade. The sites are ranked by age and the bearing runs oldest-last — somebody laid them out in order, and the order runs into the deep edge, toward the first one they ever made. Second-tier's the fourth site, the one the Compact fenced. Third-tier's out past it, on the line, quiet for centuries. Teague's crew came after the thaw like he said — ten of us now, and he's still better at this than I am, and the ledger still says so. Two crews, one bearing, and a straightedge my guardian sent me because my grandmother said a man who catalogues everything eventually needs to draw a line through it. Turns out she was right about the line. Turns out the line was already drawn four hundred years ago. We're just the first to follow it to the end." [B7-reconciled 2026-10-11: OWNER-pending #48] Also: "Two words," … "Both of them ours." / "Noted."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md-406-- **S19:** "Nobody declares. Nobody dies. They're seven people on a posting. Noted?" / "Noted," / "You'll die in there." / "We've been in three times," / win sentence: "The crews had removed the watch's ability to hold the approach by making the only safe retreat a line the watch would not cross and the crews would." / Teague's arm: "A third time on that arm and I'll set it in a splint. That's not a healer's opinion. That's a fact about a line." / "Noted," / "Everybody says that now," Log body: "Twelfth—no, count it again—thirteenth. Wind, Pressure, Iron, Compression, Ember, Shadow, Storm, Anchor, Blade, Tide, Iron Wall, Rune, Force. Thirteen things that aren't a Path, and I got three of them in one city and one bridge. Rate's climbing and I'm writing that down because Hesk taught me to note the trend. The Span's the fourth site and the Compact fenced it, years ago by the scale on the iron, and left men on it. They knew. They've always known. The bearing runs through it and keeps going, oldest-last, and the oldest one's out there in the deep edge, quiet since they built the machine. One more site. I've wanted to stand in the middle of it since Karis said *someone made this.* Tomorrow I find out what standing in the middle of the first one feels like."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:407:- **S20 close:** "It's here," Karis said. "Third-tier, documented four hundred years back — as old as the record, as old as the Architect, as old as the machine. The first one they made, or near it." / "And it's on the line." / "It's the *end* of the line. Oldest-last. Everything before it was practice, or maintenance, or — I don't have the word. This is the one that's been quiet since before quiet had a name." … "You still want to stand in the middle of it." / "More than I've wanted anything since I was fourteen." / "Why." / "Because every other site, my Path worked while everyone's died. I want to know what the *oldest* one does to me. I think it's going to tell me something the records can't." [OWNER-pending #57] / He was right. He did not yet know how right.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md-408-- **S21:** "Behind it. Noted?" / "Noted," (the young one). The far-mouth realization: the line "was not ink".
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md-409-- **S22 log body:** "Turned the Rune fragment on myself. Cleon said don't. The reading only ever told me the still place was nothing — no flow, no current, a hole where a man should have a middle. The Rune sense says it's not a hole. It says it's *built like something* — like one of those floors with nothing standing on it. Same shape to that fragment as the sites are to Cleon's. I'm not going to write what that might mean, because I don't know, and because the last time I wrote something true in this log a healer made me swear an oath about it. The instruments read wronger for me than they did at Lowmarch. The trend's climbing. My Arbiter's still dark—nothing in me to go quiet, same as always. Tomorrow, the oldest one. I've spent the whole year wanting to stand in the middle of it. I think part of me already knows what I'm going to feel. Nothing. I think I'm going to feel nothing, and I think that's going to be the loudest thing that's ever happened to me." [OWNER-pending #58] ("the whole year", never "the whole book".) [OWNER-pending #58]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md-410-- **S23 close:** "Every other one, yours worked and ours died." / "Yes." / "You think this one's different." / "I think this one's the first one they made," … "I think if any place on the continent is going to tell me what I am, it's the one they built to make sure I'd never be." / He stepped over the line. / His Path did not die. It also did not *notice* it should have. [OWNER-pending #57] / "Wait here," he said, and did not look back, and walked toward the center of the oldest quiet, alone.
--
