books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-18.md-172-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-18.md-173-Twelve on the road, two of whom were on no wall's count — a healer of Tide and a healer of no Path, on either end of the ten that were, walking the same bearing for reasons that were their own — and a mule — hers since a fork, and back on the bearing because she was — which had Karis's boxes on it now beside her roll, because Oryn had put them there herself at the ford and had said nothing about it and the mule had not minded.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-18.md-174-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-18.md:175:The bearing ran east from the ford, past the Stair's bowl and the Hall's marsh, five days to the fourth site with the Compact's cordon round it, which none of them had crossed, and past that, on the line, to a point in a straightedge's shadow that had been quiet since there was paper to say so. The sun came up along it. He had not planned that; a bearing that ran east had the sun come up along it every morning, and he entered it anyway, because it was true this morning and he was the one who wrote down what was true.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-18.md-176-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-18.md-177-A healer's route and a researcher's archive, on one mule, on one road, walking the same way for as long as the road ran the same way; and the twelve of them behind it, in the order the road took them, going east into the light with the wheel turning behind them in its race, slow, and the ford-keeper at the mill's door with flour to the elbow, counting them out, and arriving at the number, and saying it to nobody.
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-2-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-3-He logged the leg as a leg, in five entries, one a night, because the road was the road and the sites were not, and he wanted the two kept apart on the page.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-4-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md:5:*Two hundred and ninety-ninth.* East from the ford on the bearing, twelve and a mule, the Stair road under them to the Stair's bowl by the last of the light — the rim, the floor below it with its terraces going down, the iron a stride apart round it as the registry had set it; not entered; Karis at the rim with the cord for the hour there was, checking a number she already had; camp on the rim. *Three hundredth.* The road to Oryn's last three holds turned north off the bearing at a cairn at noon, and she looked at it once, straight, and walked on east with the roll on the mule, and nobody spoke, because the terms had been said and the day she had to was not this one. The Hall's marsh by dusk the same day; the stone at its edge where he had read himself twice by a fire in the autumn. *Three hundred and first. Three hundred and second.* Past the marsh, on the bearing, three days of ground none of the six had walked and Teague's four had walked once, in the autumn, carrying a healer to a rival's shoulder; two camps with no water. *Three hundred and third.* The crest.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-6-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-7-He entered, under each date, the same line, because it was true under each: *known*. Not followed — Seln had the count at every halt and there was nothing behind — but read, off a sheet, by somebody who had the sheet, and he did not stop entering it, because a feeling that recurs is a fact about the road.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-8-
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-10-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-11-The crest at dusk on the three hundred and third, and the thing below it, and he catalogued both, because one of them had not been there in the autumn.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-12-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md:13:The circle first, as it had been. Eight hundred metres across by Karis's cords, twice the others; the ground dropping away inside it on the north side into a sink, a bowl where the earth had gone down and stayed down, and at the middle, four hundred metres in past the iron, the shape against the last of the sky — not a mound, not a hall; a curve, with a crown taller than a man, and dark under it where the sky came through. The iron round all of it, a hand's height out of the ground, a pace apart, the number and the stroke-and-bar on every stake, and the line Lira had called in the autumn a hundred and ninety paces below the crest, where it had been.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-14-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-15-And then the thing that had not been there.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-16-
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-136-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-137-Four hundred metres of quiet, over ground that went down toward the sink's bowl and then along the bowl's rim, with the shape at the middle getting a shape as they came. And then it had one, and he catalogued it from the rim, because he had wanted to stand in the middle of it since the autumn and was going to, and wanted the outside first.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-138-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md:139:A span. A bridge — an arch, of the grey stone that had no seams, rising off two piers set into the sink's two sides where the ground had dropped away, and going over, the crown of it taller than a man against the noon sky and the dark under it where the bowl went down; the fourth floor, and not a floor. A thing built to cross a gap that had not been there when it was built, or had; he did not know, and Karis, at the rim with the cord already out and the notebook in the bound hand, did not know either, and said so, and went to the piers.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-140-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-141-The carvings were on the piers. The same make — he had seen four floors' worth and knew the hand as he knew Hesk's — cut into the seamless stone in rows on the inner faces of both piers, where a man standing under the span would see them and a man standing on it would not, and Karis had the charcoal against the first sheet before Brom had the sheet flat, and her body did the thing it did at a source, and Cael left her to it, because that was hers, and went up.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md-142-
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-02.md-20-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-02.md-21-He catalogued the man as the ferry grounded, because a catalogue was what he had, and because the man he was looking at was not the man he had a catalogue for.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-02.md-22-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-02.md:23:Fifty-three, or near it, and the winter and the road had put more on him than the months since a room in Ostrand had. The hair gone from iron to something with less iron in it. A traveling coat, brown, road-made, the kind a carter's factor wears, with the elbows already through and the hem cut short for mud. Boots that had been good boots a long way west of here and had done three hundred miles since. A pack, one strap mended with cord. A stick, ash, cut for walking and not for show, with the top worn to the shape of a hand. And the walk, when he stepped off onto the landing with the pack on one shoulder — the left leg taking the weight and the right coming after it a half-count late, a knee that had been asked for more miles than it had agreed to and had kept the account.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-02.md-24-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-02.md-25-No grey. No seal. No clerk behind him with a book. No two men in road-colour a pace back and a pace to either side. He came up the landing alone with the ferryman's boy in front of him carrying the sack, and the boy did not look round at him, because to the boy he was a man who had paid his passage, and that was all a man was on a ferry.
