books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-12.md:81:He catalogued it as the ford had taught him to catalogue a pack that has lost its first mover: not a rout; a *decision*, taken all at once by six people who had built a plan for a declaration and had got a wall, a rock, a knife in a stave, a cord round an arm, and a boy standing still, and had understood, in the time it takes a man to draw one, that the plan was not going to happen and that the next plan would be made somewhere else. The three at the plane went back up the first flight into the landing's shadow, and one of them cut the fourth man's cord with a knife, and the four of them were four shapes on a landing, and then they were not, because a landing in a city has three ways off it and they took all three. The two on the steps got up — the one with the knee, slowly — and went down past Vastin's plane on the wrong side of it, which was the party's side, because the party let them, because Brom stood on the stave and Lira stood on the step and neither of them touched a man who was walking away, and the two went through the inn's yard gate into the ring road and were gone into a district that had a thousand people in it and no faces.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-12.md-82-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-12.md:83:None taken. No faces. Nobody dead. Two staves and a spent bolt on the steps, and a weighted cord on a rail, and six hoods that had gone into a city like water into ground.
