books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-12.md:129:*Two hundred and ninetieth day. Treswick, the cut between the first-tier stair and the inn's yard. Six of them, faceless, hooded, a crossbow and staves; one of the six on the landing to say when. They came for a declaration. None taken. Nobody dead. The cache kept — it was on a roof. Karis, left hand, a cut, bound with thread and a shirt. Brom, the shoulder carried; the good one used wrong. Lira, nothing, two arms. Seln, nothing; a cord on a rail. Vastin, spent past a road; the knee again. Me, the burst not used, on purpose; nothing declared; a minute at a plane's foot, being the reason. A hundred and thirty-six in hand.*
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-12.md-130-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-12.md-131-*Eleventh. Iron Wall, from Vastin, in an alley, while he held a line for us with nothing behind him but the decision to. It's not Brom's — Brom gets harder, this holds. I've spent five years learning to give ground and disappear. Now I've got the opposite in me, from the man who spent forty years being the wall they put in front of people like me. He held it for us instead. I don't have a word for what that is. I wrote it down anyway.*
