books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md-98-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md:99:He had the numbers from Lowmarch in the log, from the first week of the winter, in the plain hand, and he read them by the fire's smear before he touched anything: *the yard, forty-two strides for everyone and forty-one and a piece for me, three times; the Ternhall edge touching three and the fourth a hair short in my hand; Hesk's edge a hair long.* A cord and two brass edges that read everyone but him. He had had it then as a hold, with Karis's rule beside it, and he had carried it since, and at the Span, on the crown, Karis had counted a row of sheets near his hands and it had come out a sheet short, again, as at a spring, and she had looked at his hands, once, and said nothing, and he had entered that under the same hold.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md-100-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md:101:He took the cord out of the kit's case, quietly, and paced the camp's edge with it in the dark. Twenty strides, by the cord, from the mule to the last stone of the fire's ring. He paced it back. Nineteen and a piece. He paced it a third time. Nineteen and a piece. He put the cord down, and did not pace it a fourth time, because three was the number and a fourth would have been wanting a different answer.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md-102-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md-103-Then the edges. He laid the Ternhall edge along the map's line by the fire's last light, as he had at Lowmarch, and it touched three of the five circles and missed the fourth and the fifth by more than a hair — he sighted along it and there was daylight, a hair and a half, two, where at Lowmarch there had been one — and he laid Hesk's edge, which was true to a hair by the man who had cut every gauge in a shop in Denvash, and it was long, and it was longer than it had been on a landing in the snow, and he sat with both edges in his hands and the fire going out and did the thing Hesk had taught him to do with any two numbers taken at two times, which was to put them side by side and say which way they went.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md-104-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md-105-They went up.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md-106-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md:107:At Lowmarch a hair. Now more than a hair. A cord a piece short in the winter and a whole stride short tonight. The instruments read wrong for him and for nobody else, and they read *wronger* than they had, and he had two dates and two numbers and a direction, which was a trend, and Hesk had said that a man who catalogues everything eventually needs to note the trend, and he noted it, and could not source it, and did not try. He put the two facts beside each other on the page — *the instruments read wronger; the oldest one is a half-day east* — and did not draw a line between them, because a line was a thing you drew when you had measured both ends, and he had measured one and was going to stand on the other tomorrow, and standing was not measuring either.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md-108-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md-109-The fire went out. The plain was dark to the east, and the haze had gone with the light, and there was nothing to see where the thing was, and he did not look, because it did not know he was coming and he did not need to look at a thing that did not know.
