books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md-54-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md-55-The bearing ended.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md-56-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md:57:That was the last thing the pacing found, and it found it at the far side, at the hundred and fifty-ninth cord, when the circle was closed and Karis laid the straightedge along the map's line for the last time on this side of anything. The line ran from the west, through four circles, to this one, the fifth, and through it, to the middle — and stopped. She laid the edge past the middle and there was nothing on the far side of the fifth circle for it to touch: no sixth; no distance on any sheet; nothing in the index; nothing on the plain, which went on east, level, worn, empty, to a horizon that had nothing on it. Oldest-last. The end of the line.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md-58-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md-59-"It's the end," she said. "There's nothing past it. Not on the paper and not on the ground." She folded the map. "I've paced it. A thousand across, sharp, perfect, oldest by the strata, and at the end of the line. That's all that the outside can say."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md-60-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md-61-He wrote it at the mule, standing, in the plain hand, because a thing paced should be entered before it is stood in.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md-62-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md:63:*Three hundred and seventh day. The court — the fifth, and the last; the end of the line. A thousand and twelve across by the cord; sharp; perfect; oldest by the strata; the marks the first cut. Eleven and a mule at the line. A hundred and twenty-three in hand. Nineteen. Thirteen. Nothing past it.*
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md-64-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md-65-The council took less than a sunset, as the one on a crest above the fourth had, and it was the same council with the answer the other way round, and he catalogued that, because a thing decided twice in two directions is a thing whose reasons should both be on the page. On the crest there had been five, and no healer within four days, and a circle twice the size of anything they had entered, and three sentences of reasons not to, and they had not. At the rim there were eleven, and a healer a stride off, and a circle two and a half times the Ring, and the same three sentences, and one more, which was that the line ended here and there was nothing past it to walk to instead. Nobody said the fourth sentence. Karis folded the map. Lira looked at him. That was the council, and it had decided what it had decided on a bridge and at a fire and over a map, which was that he was going in, alone, and that the eleven were going to stand at the line while he did, with their Paths gone at a stride and a healer's hands useless a stride in, because that was the only way it could be done and every one of them had known it since a landing in the snow.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md-66-
