books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-03.md-124-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-03.md-125-The part that came next had always been his. A problem brought to a table; a response; a team, a warrant, a man sent, an order given before the problem had finished being spoken, because that was what the table was for and that was what he was for at it. He had done it ten thousand times. He did not know how to sit at a table where the problem had been set down and the response belonged to somebody else, and Cael watched him not know it — the hands flat, the stick against the table's edge, the eyes on the six rows going the length of the wood and on the woman at the near end who had already begun to own them.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-03.md-126-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-03.md:127:Because Karis had. She had the notebook open and the pencil going now, and she was not writing the line again; she was writing the three questions she was going to ask the records in the morning, in the order she had them, and the questions were hers, and the records were hers, and the page on the bare end of the table was, whatever else it was, a place she was going to go, and the man who had carried all of it three hundred miles on a knee that said so watched her take it the way a man watches a thing he used to do being done by somebody else, and did not reach for it, and Cael saw the not-reaching cost him, and saw him pay it.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-03.md-128-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-03.md-129-"You'll want to read them," Vastin said, to Karis, eventually. It was not an order. It was the nearest thing to one that he had left, and it came out as a thing a man says to fill a room.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-03.md-130-
