books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-13.md-24-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-13.md-25-Karis moved. Cael did not. He was labour, and labour held the lamp, and the lamp was two feet from the sheet and two feet from the man's hand, and he had held it there all week and was, he found, the person in the room best placed to watch a thing from inside, because the man had stopped seeing him.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-13.md-26-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-13.md:27:"A record," Cleon said, "sits. Every mark's where it is because a thing happened and somebody put the mark down after. You read it and the marks are *still*. They describe. Nothing in them wants anything." His hand went flat on the board, on his ten-year piece, and his eyes went to the unfocused place between the board and the sheet. "An instruction *leans*. Every mark's where it is because a thing is meant to happen next, and the mark's pointing at the next thing. You put your attention on it and it's not still. It has a direction. It's built so that the eye goes *along* it, bearer to bearer, in an order, and you can feel the order as you can feel which way a stair goes in the dark by where the treads are. That's what I see. Not words. The lean."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-13.md-28-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-13.md-29-He read the sheet.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-13.md-30-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-13.md-31-Cael watched him read it, from two feet, holding the lamp, and had, for the length of the reading, the thing he had had beside a plane in a cut: the sense of a craft being done at the edge of his eye by a body that was not hiding it. Cleon's hand did not move. His eyes did not move, either — that was the thing Cael had not seen from the wall on the other days — they held the unfocused place and did not travel the sheet as a reading eye travels, and it was the *attention* that travelled, and the attention went along the sheet's fourth row bearer to bearer, and Cael could not see it go and could see that it went, because the man's breath changed at each bearer, a little, as a man's breath changes on a stair when his foot finds the tread.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-13.md-32-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-13.md:33:"There," Cleon said, at the row's end. "Left to right. Six bearers, in order. It leans. The whole row goes somewhere. A record doesn't go anywhere; a record's a heap. This is a stair." He took his hand off the board. "That's the inside. That's the whole of the Path, and I've never once shown it to anyone, and I've shown it to you because in a day or two you'll need to have seen a thing lean, so that when I tell you, at the week's end, which way all of it leans you'll believe your own eyes and not mine."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-13.md-34-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-13.md-35-"Noted," said Karis, and wrote, and Cael saw her hand shake once and steady, because she had seen a thing lean and had not known that a made thing could.
