books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-20.md:45:She turned the records to the fifth column.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-20.md-46-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-20.md-47-He knew the column. He had known it since a spring: the office's abbreviation, every sheet, every year, that Seln had read for them as *not asking* — the sigil going out at the line, every practitioner, every site, the same distance, the same instant. He had known, too, where the sentence went that Karis had begun over that column at the spring and had stopped, with her pencil down and her hand off the sheet and a rule said aloud in the small voice, because he had heard her not go there twice, and had entered the not-going both times.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-20.md-48-
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-20.md-66-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-20.md-67-She said it over him, not loud, in the small voice, and he stopped, because it was her rule and she had said it at a table at Thornwater and at a spring and now at a fire, and a man who had spent forty years making findings into orders knew a rule when he heard one and knew that this one had been kept longer than any he had ever written.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-20.md-68-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-20.md:69:"I know where that sentence goes," she said, to him, not unkindly. "I've known since the leaf. You know where it goes. Everyone at this fire knows where it goes. There's a leaf that says a man made the measure and the seat and the tier and the mark, and set an apparatus over the land, and there's a column that says every mark goes out like a lamp when a wire's cut, and there's a word on both that's the same word, and the sentence that joins them is about six words long and I could say it in my sleep. I'm not going to." She opened the notebook, with the bound hand, and turned to a page, and wrote on it, and turned to another page, and wrote on that. "Two findings. Two pages. What the leaf says, which is a record and says what it says. What the column says, which is a measurement and says what it says. I've put them on separate pages and I'm not drawing a line between them, because a line's a thing you draw when you've measured both ends, and I've measured one end and read the other, and reading isn't measuring. I've spent a year at the foot of a stair learning the difference. I'll not throw it away at a fire because the sentence is easy."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-20.md-70-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-20.md-71-Vastin looked at her a long moment. Then he said, "Noted," and it was the third time, and he meant it as she meant her rule.
