books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-14.md-5-He catalogued the parcel before he opened it, because a thing from Hesk was a thing you owed a description before you owed it a use.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-14.md-6-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-14.md:7:Oilcloth, waxed, the kind the man wrapped a finished piece in for a customer who had a road to walk it on. Tied with the flat cord he used, in the knot he used, which was a knot that came undone with one pull and did not come undone by accident, and Cael had watched him tie it a hundred times at a bench with his hands going faster than his eyes and had never once managed it himself. A letter round the whole, folded twice, addressed on the outside in the dry square hand to *the boy with the log, at the edge, wherever the edge is this season*, which was the whole of the direction and had been enough, because a courier chain that ran from a bench in Denvash to a bench in Lowmarch and a river ferry and a broker's runner at a street door in the outer district of a city on the Line was a chain of people who knew what an instrument-maker's parcel looked like and passed it along by its shape.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-14.md-8-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-14.md-9-Karis was not there. She had gone up to the registry at its opening and down with Cleon on the seventh day of the week he had asked for, alone with him by her own asking, and she had said at the yard door, without looking round, "Today he tells me," and had gone down the stair with her bound hand against her coat and the flat under her other arm and had not come up at noon, and nobody had gone to see why, because a woman a day from the thing she had hunted her whole life was not a woman you went to see about.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-14.md-10-
