books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-14.md-72-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-14.md-73-*Two hundred and ninety-second day. Treswick, the outer district, the inn on the ring road, the long table. Inside walls. Five at the table; one below; one north. A hundred and thirty in hand; the week paid to the day; nothing owed. Brom's foot walks; the shoulder carried, bound fresh two days back; the good one used wrong in a cut and told him so. Lira, two arms, twenty-seven days. Seln, nothing; a slip in the fire. Vastin, the knee bound, a month running; at the table. Karis, the hand bound, below, on the seventh day.*
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-14.md-74-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-14.md:75:*Twelve. Wind, Pressure, Iron, Compression, Ember, Shadow, Storm, Anchor, Blade, Tide; Iron Wall, from a plane in a cut; Rune, from a lamp. The still place at the middle of them, not read, not touched, not the Quiet, not a fragment. A cord that reads everyone but me. A row of sheets that counts one short in my hands. A straightedge and now the thing that goes with it.*
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-14.md-76-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-14.md-77-*Hesk sent dividers. Lines are for finding out how far a thing is from another thing. Everything is. I'll enter that as a rule and see what it's worth.*
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-14.md-78-
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-14.md-88-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-14.md-89-And it did the thing.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-14.md-90-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-14.md:91:He would have said, after, if anyone had asked him — nobody did — that he had not seen it. That was true. He had not been looking at it. He was looking at the second gate and the coat at it and the queue doing the thing with its hands, and at the edge of his eye, on the crown of a hill four tiers up, a pale steady light that had been pale and steady since a rise did a thing that a pale steady light does not do: it was not steady. For a count of one. Not brighter, not dimmer; *wrong*, in the way the cord had been wrong in his hands and the row had been wrong in his hands — a flicker that was not a flicker, because a flicker is a thing you see, and this was a thing he felt, at the edge of his eye and somewhere under his ribs at once, a pressure, small, the pressure of a thing turning. An instrument turning toward him.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-14.md-92-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-14.md-93-Then it was steady.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-14.md-94-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-14.md-95-He stood at the stair's foot with his hands at his sides. Nobody on the ring road had stopped. A carter went past with a mule. The coat at the second gate read a pass and waved a hand through. The queue did the thing with its hands. Nobody, on a street with forty people on it, had felt a light four tiers up do a thing, once, briefly, because it had not done it for them, and he stood and let that be a fact and did not make it into anything else, because he did not have anything else to make it into and was not going to invent one.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-14.md-96-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-14.md:97:He had a page for it. That was all that he did with it. He had a page in the log for things the instruments did wrong around him and it had three entries on it — a still place a reading could not read; a cord that counted forty-one and a piece; a row of sheets that came out one short in his hands — and he added a fourth, standing, without taking the log out, in the part of him that wrote things down: a light on a hill that was not steady for the space of a step, once, for him. Deniable. Every one of the four was deniable. He had known since he was fourteen that he was the thing the instruments were not built for, and here was the biggest instrument on the continent, four tiers up, in the sun, doing the thing the small ones did, and he filed it where the small ones were filed, under the unreadability that had followed him across a continent, and let it stand, and did not cross.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-14.md-98-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-14.md-99-He stood a while longer, because a thing that had done something once might do it again and a cataloguer waits for the second time before he enters the first as a pattern, and it did not do it again. The light was pale and steady on the crown of the hill in the afternoon sun, and the queue went through the second gate, and the carter's mule was gone round the ring road's bend, and nothing four tiers up did anything but be what it had been for longer than the walls. He waited the length of time a man waits at a stair's foot before a coat at a gate begins to wonder about him, and then a little less than that, because Seln had taught him the length, and turned.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-14.md-100-
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-11.md-2-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-11.md-3-By the third day down he had learned the lamp.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-11.md-4-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-11.md:5:That was the whole of his trade in the chamber, and he learned it as he had learned to hold a cord's far end for a woman who was pacing: where the light had to be for the reader, which was never where the reader was looking but a hand to the left of it, so that the hand did not shadow the line; when to move it, which was before she reached for it and not after; how to hold a sheet flat on the table's edge with two fingers at its corners so that a man could read the whole of a carving-copy without the paper's curl putting a false line across a true one. Labour. He was good at it by the second hour of the second day and very good at it by the third, and Cleon had stopped saying "Lamp" and had started simply turning his head a finger's width, and the light was there, and Cael found that he did not mind being a thing a man turned his head for, and wrote that down at the bell, because it was true and he had not expected it.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-11.md-6-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-11.md-7-The two hundred and eighty-sixth day. The chamber under the registry, the lamp that was always lit and the one he held, Cleon in the cushioned chair and Karis in the one that had none, and on the table between them the Ring's eleven and the Stair's twenty-nine and the Hall's twelve, and beside the Hall's, on its own square of cloth, the thing Cleon had kept on the shelf behind him for ten years.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-11.md-8-
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-11.md-28-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-11.md-29-He sat back. He took both hands off the table.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-11.md-30-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-11.md:31:"A made thing's either a record or an instruction. It describes, or it commands. A ledger describes: this much, on this day, to this man. A recipe commands: do this, then this. They're built differently — the bones are different — and I can see which is which as you can see whether a wall's holding a roof or just standing there. These" — the sheets — "command. All fifty-two. Every run on every floor is built as an instruction's built, with the bearers where an instruction puts them. They're not the marks somebody cuts to say *we were here*, and they're not the marks somebody cuts to make a floor handsome. They're orders." He looked at the board. "This is an order too. Same make."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-11.md-32-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-11.md-33-Karis had not moved. Her pencil was on the notebook and had not touched it.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-11.md-34-
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-28-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-29-*The kit is on the table.*
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-30-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md:31:*It has been on the table since the first week, because Karis put it there, and nobody has moved it, because moving it would mean deciding whose it was, and it is not anyone's; it is the Compact's, recovered under a contract that paid nothing, and it sits on a table in an inn where the Compact has never been and is not going to come. The case, road-made, registry-stamped. The pacing cord, knotted at the fives, twenty strides to a cord, and the cord's end-loops worn to the shine that a thing gets when one pair of hands has used it for a season. The stake — thirty-two, by its number — with its point still bright from the twice it was driven at nothing. The mallet. The field ledger, in the surveyor's square hand, with the radius line in it, and the one entry that says what the Stair is — a designation, in an index, that nobody at this table can read — and under it the line that stops: third site confirmed on bearing.*
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-32-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md:33:*Karis has been learning it. Not the index; the kit. She says a tool teaches you the hand that used it, and that she wants to know that hand better than she knows her own before she meets it, and I do not ask her where she thinks she is going to meet it. She has paced the meadow with the cord in the snow, and the yard, and the inn's long room, and laid the two straightedges on the carter's map every night and moved them and laid them again, and they do not stop touching all four, and she does not stop laying them.*
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-34-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-35-*Hundred and sixty-eighth day.*
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-36-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-37-*Something to write down.*
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-38-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md:39:*Karis had us all pace the yard with the cord, for the learning of it — the cord, the count, the stone at each end, as it was done at the Ring — and the yard is forty-two strides by the wall and forty-two by Brom's count and forty-two by Lira's and Seln's, and forty-one and a piece by mine. Twice. Three times. With the same cord and the same wall and the same stones, and Karis at the far end holding what she held for everyone.*
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-40-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-41-*Then the straightedges. Karis laid the Ternhall edge along the map's bearing and it touched four. I laid it, and it touched three and the fourth by a hair short — a hair — and Lira laid it and it touched four, and I laid Hesk's edge and it was a hair long. Hesk's note had said true to a hair, and it was, in every hand but mine. Brom said it was the cold in the brass, and it might have been, except that the brass was the same temperature in his hand as in mine, and touched four in his.*
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-42-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md:43:*"A measurement is a fact about a ruler," Karis said, "not about the hand that held it." She said it as she had said it at the Stair, as a rule, and then she looked at the cord in my hand for a while, and said it again, and it was not a rule the second time.*
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-44-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md:45:*I am writing it down as what it is: the third instrument this winter that reads everyone but me, if you count Oryn's hands as an instrument, and I do. Claim: something. Evidence: a cord, two edges, a hair. Ruling: hold. It is the same unreadability, probably. The instruments the Compact makes cannot parse me and never could, and a cord is an instrument, and I have known since I was fourteen that I am the thing the instruments were not built for. Nobody at the table said that. Nobody said anything, and we went in to the fire, and the yard stayed forty-two strides for everyone else.*
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-46-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-47-*Two hundred and thirtieth day. The river is a river again at the middle.*
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-48-
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-158-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-159-"Then say it."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-160-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md:161:He told her about the cord. He told it as he had written it, because that was the only way he knew to tell a thing: the yard, forty-two strides for everyone and forty-one and a piece for him, three times; the edges, a hair short in his hand and a hair long, and true in everyone else's; Karis's sentence about a ruler, said twice. He told her because she was a healer who could not read him and had said so as a finding, and a man who is owed a finding owes the instruments back — every one that cannot read him either, as they turn up. She listened with her hands flat on the table, a chest under them for all the difference it made to her.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-162-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-163-"A hair," she said.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-164-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-165-"A hair," he said. "In my hands. Only mine."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-166-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md:167:She thought about it. He watched her think about it and not theorize, which was the thing he had walked two days to watch, though he had not known that was what he was walking for. "I put my hands on you four times," she said, "and got nothing three times and a floor the fourth. Now a cord gets nothing. I don't know what that is." She took her hands off the table. "Neither do you. Write that down too."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-168-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-169-"I did."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-170-
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-184-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-185-Karis made her case then, because there were six, and she had been waiting for six.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-186-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md:187:She made it plainly, in the order she had it, with her hand on the ledger. The carvings were instructions. She had known it at the Hall, from the shape of them — a record describes and an instruction commands, and these commanded, in the way a ledger's columns command — and she could not read a word of the language they commanded in, and neither could anyone on this side of the Line, because the language was older than the Line. The kit's ledger carried one entry for the Stair that was not a measurement — and under it a line that stopped, third site confirmed on bearing — a designation, in an index, in the surveyor's own hand, and she did not know what the index was, only that it was one, because a man does not write *index* in his own field book for his own convenience; he writes it because somebody will check it against a book he has never seen. The bearing ran east off the map and the answer to what it ran toward was not east.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-188-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md:189:"It's west," she said. "Treswick. The last Compact city on the Line. There's a registry office on the old town, and under the old town there's an archive that was there before the Compact was — the Deepstacks, the scholars call it, when they call it anything — records nobody converted because nobody was ordered to and nobody needed to. Whatever these are" — her hand on the oilcloth — "if there's a word for them anywhere on the continent, it's in a book under that registry that nobody has opened since before there was a registry to open it. And there's a provision. The old scholars' provision. It lets a registered historical-studies practitioner in. I'm not one, out here. There's a man there who is."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-190-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-191-Nobody answered. Brom looked at the pouch. Seln looked at the door. Lira looked at Cael, because Lira always looked at Cael when the word *registry* was said in a room, and had done since Norhold, and did not need to say why. Oryn looked at the map upside down.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-192-
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-206-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-207-*Two hundred and seventieth day. Lowmarch, the lower inn, by the fire. Ice going. Six of us.*
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-208-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md:209:*A hundred and eighteen in hand. Fence paid. Rope-house paid. Mule's shed paid. Nothing owed. Lira: two arms. A foot that's hers. Brom: the shoulder bound and carried and not yet read. Karis: three copies, one ledger, one map, two edges. Seln: the case. Oryn: her route, two stops of it here. Me: ten; the still place; a cord that reads everyone else.*
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-210-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-211-*The ferry runs when the ice goes. Karis has a bearing, an index she can't read, and a language she can't read, and she says the answers are in a border archive nobody's opened since before the Compact. I've spent five years staying out of registries because a registry is where they file you. She wants me to walk into the oldest one on the continent. The kit reads everyone but me. So does the world, mostly. Nineteen. Ice goes next week. West, and inside, for the first time since the academy above Ostrand — which is a sentence I did not think I'd write again.*
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md-98-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md-99-Seln came over the shelf's lip at the seventh volley, behind the crossbows.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md-100-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md:101:There were nine on the shelf and the nine were looking down. He had a cord — the weighted one, from a roof in a city — and he put it round the nearest crossbow's stock and the arm that held it and pulled, once, and the bolt went into the shelf's floor, and then he was among them, and the shelf was a shelf the width of a fire with ten people on it and one of them not where anybody had put him, and a crossbow is a thing you shoot from a distance, and there was no distance.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md-102-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md-103-Cael was on the scree by then.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md-104-
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md-118-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md-119-The team broke.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md-120-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md:121:Along the ridge, east, above the shelf, where there was a way for people who had built the shelf and knew it: nine hoods, and then eight on the ridge and one on the shelf's floor with a cord round his arm who got up when Seln let him and went after the others, because Seln let him. None taken. No faces. They went along the ridge above the pass's far mouth and over it and were the hills, and nobody on the shelf or the stair or the stream-bed went after them, because the pass was the thing that had wanted doing, and it was done.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md-122-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md-123-Seln had removed the team's ability to hold the high ground by climbing the slope its own rig had swept clean, and the wall had bought him the climb. Cael wrote it that night, in one sentence, and it was true, and it was the least of what he wrote.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md-124-
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-24.md-2-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-24.md-3-He counted the strides, because it was the only instrument he had left that read true in his hands, and because a man walking alone across the oldest floor on the continent should be doing something with his mouth that is not talking.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-24.md-4-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-24.md:5:Five hundred and six to the middle. He had the number from the cord's half, and he had it in the register he used for a price, and he walked it as he had walked a Span's arch and a Stair's terraces and a Ring's floor, on stone that had no seams and no give, with the last of the light lying along it from behind him, and his shadow going ahead of him toward the place, longer than he was, which was a thing he noted and did not make into anything.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-24.md-6-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-24.md-7-Ten. Twenty. The stumps behind him going from a rim to a row to a line, and the eleven at them going from people to a shape, and he did not look back, because he had said he would not and a thing said at a line is a thing kept. The thirteen rang in him faint as they rang at every rim — the ten that ran and the three that held, the bed and the plane and the line from heel to hand — as they had rung on four floors, doing nothing, because doing was not what a held thing did. The still place at the middle of them did what it did. And the thing under it, the thing that had turned at the line, turned with him: a stride at a time, as a needle turns when the lodestone is carried past it, not pulling, not pushing, *facing*.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-24.md-8-
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-10.md-2-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-10.md-3-The dark did not give the lamp back, and then it did, a little at a time, in the order the lamp asked for it.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-10.md-4-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-10.md:5:Rock first. The quarry's own rock, cut in the days before anyone cut rock square, in galleries that went off from the landing in three directions at a height a man could walk in and a width two men could pass in, and the cut-marks still on every face of it, the marks of tools nobody made any more, running as the quarrymen had run them and not as a mason would. Then the shelving, which was not shelving as the Compact shelved a thing — no cases, no doors, no labels — but timber pinned into the rock floor to roof along both walls of every gallery, grey with age and dry as bone, and on the timber, the records.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-10.md-6-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-10.md:7:He catalogued them by their shapes because he could not catalogue them by anything else. Rolls, the size of a man's forearm, tied, with the ties gone to dust and the rolls staying rolled by habit. Boards — flat pieces of wood the length of a hand with a face to them, a hundred to a shelf, standing on their edges like slates. Bound leaves, thick, sewn along one side with something that had been cord, in covers of a leather that had been a colour once. Things that were none of those: a sheet of hammered metal with marks on it, standing against the rock; a stone, squared, with a face cut into it; a bundle of sticks tied in a pattern that was, he understood after a while, the pattern itself, and not a bundle. Centuries of a country's paper, if it was paper, in scripts that were not the Compact's and had not been anyone's for longer than the Compact had had a name, kept because a decision had never been made about them, in a hole, in the dark, under a building whose whole purpose was to decide.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-10.md-8-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-10.md-9-And the smell. He had no catalogue for it. It was the smell of a thing that has been left alone for so long that the leaving-alone has become the thing, and he stood on the landing with a box on his shoulder and breathed it and understood that he had never once, in nineteen years, stood in a place where nothing had been decided, and that this was what it smelled like.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-10.md-10-
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-10.md-34-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-10.md-35-"A fourth." His hand had not moved. "And the word."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-10.md-36-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-10.md:37:Karis said it. "Older than [UNBOUND]," she said, as she had said it at a table in the autumn, once, as a stratum's limit and not as a meaning — the old word, the retired one, the one from the confiscated record that she had carried across two seasons and a Line and had put in a sentence exactly once — and Cleon closed his eyes.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-10.md-38-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-10.md-39-Not for long. For the length of a man deciding whether a thing he has hoped for and dreaded in equal measure for thirty years is the one or the other, and finding that it is both, and that both is not what the young man who started the work had imagined it would be. He opened them. He looked at the third set, at the Hall's twelve sheets, at a mark on the fourth of them that Cael could not have told from any other mark, and said, quietly, to the sheet and not to Karis:
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-10.md-40-
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-05.md:1:# Chapter 5 — What the Records Say
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-05.md-2-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-05.md-3-They read it at a spring a mile past the shoulder hold on the morning of the two hundred and eightieth day, because the road widened there to let carts pass and the ground was dry and the wind was at their backs, and because Brom's foot wanted an hour off it and Vastin's legs wanted more than that and would not say so.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-05.md-4-
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-05.md-16-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-05.md-17----
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-05.md-18-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-05.md:19:"Instruments," Karis said, first, because it was the first thing. "Not one man with a cord. An office's instruments, on an office's form." Her hand on a sheet in the second row. "This is a surveyor's abstract from — five years back, the Stair, the same hand as the kit. Radius, two hundred. Stake count. And here, in the column the kit's ledger doesn't have — the office's column — a note of what he carried and what he did with it: *cord, stakes, mallet; perimeter paced twice; stakes set at the fives.* Twice. He paced a floor in a bowl twice, five years ago, and set iron the whole way round, and wrote it in a form." She turned a sheet. "The Ring, four years back. Different hand. *Paced once; stakes set.* The Hall, three years back, from the marsh side — *perimeter paced along the bank; stakes not set; ground unsuitable.* They didn't go in. They walked the marsh's edge with a cord, as we walked the fourth's line, and got two hundred, and wrote it down." She put her hand flat on the row. "Six years. Instruments. Stakes at every one they could drive them at. They measured everything, and they measured it properly, and they wrote it in a form, and the form went on a shelf."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-05.md-20-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-05.md-21-"That's what an office does," Vastin said, from the stick. "It measures. It is very good at measuring."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-05.md-22-
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-05.md-60-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-05.md-61-"Here." Her finger above a sheet in the first row — the Ring — not on it. "Top corner, by the stamp. A mark. The stroke and the bar." She moved along the row. "Same mark. Same mark. Every Ring sheet, every year." The second row. "The Stair — the stroke and the bar. The Hall — the stroke and the bar." Her finger stopped over the fourth row. "The fourth. The stroke and the bar. The same. Twice the size and the same mark." She went on down the rows, the ones that were theirs, the sites the crew had never found, and her finger went over them and she did not say what was on them, because she had not checked, and a number said once was a number said. "It's the kit's mark. The designation in the field ledger — the entry I couldn't read, in the index I don't know — it's this. The same stroke and the same bar. The office puts it in the corner of every survey like a tier, and the kit's man put it in his ledger like a tier, and it *is* one; it's a grade; they've ranked these — by something — and the fourth's ranked the same as the three though it's twice their size, and I can't read it." She took her hand back. "I can see it's a rank. I can't see what it ranks them by, and I can't see why a thing twice the size gets the same mark, and I'm not going to guess. That's all I can say, and I'm not going to say more, because the next thing I'd say would be a guess wearing a finding's coat."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-05.md-62-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-05.md:63:"The fourth's the same," Brom said, from the slab's end. It was not a question. Brom had been listening with the foot up and the eyes shut and had heard one thing in it, which was the thing that had been four hundred meters to its middle and cordoned in iron.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-05.md-64-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-05.md-65-"The fourth's the same." Karis looked at the row. "The stroke and the bar, on every one of them, on the stake at the Stair and the iron at the fourth's line, and on the corner of every sheet for six years. I don't know if the same mark means the same kind or only that the office had one mark. I know a surveyor put it on every sheet, and I know we stood on a crest above it in the dark and Cael counted forty stakes and stopped because the dark stopped him, and I know that nobody at this slab is going to say what the mark means until somebody reads it to us." She put the row's stones back on its corners. "That's the shape of the thing. A mark I can't read, in a ledger I carried out of a hole, matching a mark I can't read on a shelf a marshal carried out of an office. Two copies of one ignorance."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-05.md-66-
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md-6-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md-7-He catalogued it because it was the thing, and because a thing you are about to hear read aloud once is a thing you owe a description before the reading, so that the description is not the reading's.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md-8-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md:9:Not a board. Not a roll. A leaf — a single leaf, the size of two hands, of a stuff that was not paper and not skin and had been, Cleon said, both once and had stopped being either a long time ago: stiff, the colour of old horn, with a face to it that took a mark and a back that did not, so that it had been written on one side and had lain, for as long as it had lain, with its written face down against the next leaf of a book that had been a book once and was now this leaf and eleven others in a bundle tied with a cord that had gone to dust. The writing on it was not the Compact's and was not the four scripts on the shelves and was not the board's; it was older than the board's, Cleon said, and the board's hand had learned from it as a child's hand learns from a master's — the same lean; the same bearers; the runs going left to right and the bends at the ends of the runs the same shape — and where the board had been cut with a tool, this had been written with a point, in a black that had gone brown, and had been written by somebody who wrote for a living, because every line was the same height and every run started at the same margin, and a hand that does that is a hand that has been paid to.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md-10-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md-11-And in the middle of the leaf, a third of the way down, a place where the face had been scraped.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md-12-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md-13-He saw it from the lamp's distance before he knew what it was: a patch the width of four or five of the runs' letters, if they were letters, where the horn-colour went pale and the surface went rough, as a table goes rough where a knife has been dragged across the grain — the face taken off, back to the fibre, by a blade, or by a thumb over a very long time, and at the patch's left edge, still there, the first stroke of the first mark that had stood in it, a single upright line, and after it nothing, and after the nothing the writing went on as if the patch were a word.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md-14-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md:15:"Somebody's name," Karis said. She had followed his eye. "There was one. There's the first stroke of it. There's nothing else, and there's not going to be, because it's not faded; it's *gone*. Scraped. Whether a man did it with a knife the day after it was written or a thousand thumbs did it over the years going to that one place on the page to touch it, I can't tell and Cleon can't tell and nobody ever will." She put her bound hand flat on the table beside the leaf, not on it. "I've read the rest. I read it yesterday, with the board and the Hall's fourth sheet as the key, and again last night, and a third time this morning, and I'm going to read it aloud now, once, because that's how a finding's said, and then I'm not going to say it again, and none of you are going to say it back to me, because I want it to be the record's and not mine."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md-16-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md-17-The silence held. That was correct.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md-18-
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md-24-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md-25-She read it as she read everything, at the size of a finding, and slowly, because it was a thing written in a hand centuries dead in a language the Compact had never converted, and she was reading it through a board and a floor and ten years of a man's notes, and she gave every word its own weight and did not give any word more than its own, and Cael held the lamp still and listened as the cataloguer listens, which is to the words and not to what they are about.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md-26-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md:27:"*In the years of the unmaking, when the un-bound walked in the land and no wall stood against them and no law held, and the cities of the east were emptied and the cities of the west were emptied and the rivers ran wrong* — that's the record's phrase; I've read it four times and it says what it says — *there came one who saw the shape of it, and set himself to the ordering. And he made the measure, and the seat, and the tier, and the mark that is given at the kindling, that each should know what he is and none should be what he is not. And he set the apparatus over all the land, and the land was ordered, and the un-bound came no more.*"
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md-28-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md-29-She stopped. She looked at the scraped place.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md-30-
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md-34-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md-35-The lamp did not move. He had held it still through all of it and he held it still now, because his hand was the one thing in the chamber he had charge of and he was not going to let it do anything the rest of him had not decided on.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md-36-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md:37:"That's the passage," Karis said. "There's more on the leaf — a list of the measure's parts, the seat's, the tier's; I've the shape of it and not the words yet; it's the same instruction-make Cleon read on the floors, and it's older than the floors, and the floors were cut from it or from a thing like it. But that's the passage. That's what the citation on Vastin's page pointed at across a continent. That's the thing under the registry." She took her hand off the table. "I'll say what it says, once, in my own words, and then I've said it. It says the thing the Compact calls the Path system was *made*. By a person. The record's word for him is the one on Vastin's page — the Architect; it's a title, not a name; the name's gone. It says he made it about four hundred years ago, by the date at the leaf's head, which is a date I can read because the board taught me the numerals. It says he made it *for a reason*, and the reason's the only thing on the leaf the writer cared that anybody remember, and the reason is the un-bound." She looked at the word on the leaf, where it stood, in the antique hand, live, a thing somebody had written down as the name of a thing that walked in the land. "That's the word from the confiscated record. That's the word I've carried since a stratum. It's not retired here. It's not a limit. It's a *kind*. There were people the record calls that, before the measure and the seat and the tier and the mark, and the whole of the apparatus was set over the land so that there would not be any more of them."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md-38-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md-39-She sat back.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md-40-
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md-64-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md-65-She drew a second leaf toward her — not from the bundle; from a different place on the table, a thing on its own square of cloth, smaller, of the same horn-coloured stuff but thinner and more broken, with its edges gone to crumbs and a hole through its middle the size of a thumb, so that what was on it was two-thirds of what had been.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md-66-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md:67:"A chronicle. Later than the first — the hand's the board's hand, or the board's teacher's; the same bearers, but the lean's softer; it's a record, not an instruction. Somebody, a long time after the apparatus, writing down what was remembered of the man who set it. Most of it's gone. There's a run here" — her finger above a place at the hole's edge — "that Cleon read the bones of and I read the words of, and I'll give it you as it stands and no straighter."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md-68-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md:69:She read it. It was shorter than the first, and she read it faster, because it was a record and a record sits.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md-70-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md-71-"*— and of him it is said that he did not die in the way of men, but went into the ordering he had made, and is in it —*" She stopped. "And there's the hole. Whatever came after *is in it* is a thumb's width of nothing." She looked at the leaf a moment longer, and took her finger back. "I'm marking it. I'm not reading it. I've written it down as it stands, with the hole drawn as a hole, and I've written nothing under it, and I'm going to leave it that way until somebody hands me a second source that says the same thing plainer, and if nobody ever does, it stays marked."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md-72-
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md-82-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md-83-"The apparatus," he said. "The measure, the seat, the tier, the mark. He made it and he set it over the land. And the mark that is given at the kindling — "
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md-84-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md:85:"Is a thing a record says a man made four hundred years ago," Karis said. She said it at once, and plainly, and not unkindly, and she said it looking at Vastin and not at the leaf, because it was a thing that had to be said to a face. "That's what the record says. It says he made a thing and set it over the land and it says why. It doesn't say what the thing he made has to do with yours, or with mine, or with the thing on a station's roof four tiers up, and I'm not going to say it either, because I've a leaf and a board and a floor, and a leaf and a board and a floor are facts about a leaf and a board and a floor." She put her hand flat on the notebook. "A measurement is a fact about a ruler. Not about the hand that held it. I've said that at a slab and a marsh and I'll say it in a hole. I've two findings this morning, and they go on two pages, and I'm not drawing a line between them, because I can't see the line and I'm not going to guess one."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md-86-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-15.md-87-Vastin looked at her for a while. Then he inclined his head, a finger's width — the Compact courtesy — and said, "Two pages," and did not finish the sentence, and Cael watched him not finish it, and understood that he had just watched the Compact's own held line decline to draw a line, at a table, on a researcher's word, and that the man had done it because the researcher was right and not because she was in charge, and that this was the first time in forty years the man had done that, and that it had cost him nothing, which was the strangest part.
