editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:303:He did it without turning his head. He had done it so for longer than the boy had been able to lift a pack, at every door and gate and ford, and he no longer knew that he was doing it until it was done. The spear was on his left, half a pace back, grounded. The bow was on his right and a full pace back, standing on the good leg, as the bow always stood when there had been a day's walking. The boy was between and behind, where he had been told to be, and by the sound of him he was already looking for the table with the food on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:309:It was a week, and he had the whole of it in his head already, a stage at a time, as he would have had a load. Salt went up to the top holds before the frost shut the pass, and it went on mules, and the mules went on a track that was a ledge for two miles of its length with a wind across it. He had done it nine times, and the price was fair. The bow's leg would stand a week of that if he set the pace for the leg and gave some other reason; and the boy would carry more than his share and have to be stopped, and the spear would say nothing at all for seven days and miss nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:59:The first prints were a man's, of the middle size, set down evenly. Beside the right foot, here and there, was a small round dent, where the haft of a hand-axe had swung at his thigh and touched ground on the slope. The second walker had grounded a spear at every step. The third set of marks was shorter on the left side than on the right by the width of two fingers, every stride, and no deeper for it; whoever made them had been favouring that hip for so long that the body no longer argued. The fourth were the smallest feet of the four and went in the deepest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:161:She knew the first of them, as anybody on those roads did. It was Teague, off the north water, with a string of two mules behind him and his three; and she gave them what she gave everything on legs, which was a look from a stride and a half as they drew level. A spear, carried upright and grounded at each step, by somebody who never hurried. A boy who was all wrists. And a bow, on the back of one who walked a hair short on the left, at the hip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:237:They were behind him on the hill. The spear had not changed pace for the stop. The boy had both mules by one rein and was looking back up the road after the woman, with his mouth a little open, as he looked after everything. The bow had stood square on both legs for as long as the healer's eyes were on that hip, and now the weight had gone over to the right again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:287:He looked back, once. The spear. The boy. The bow, on the good leg.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:321:They crossed without a sound. Cael watched it as he would have watched a thing well made. The one with the spear went second and grounded it at every step. The one with the bow walked a little short on one side and kept the pace regardless. A boy of sixteen or so brought the two mules through on a slack rein, and not one of the four looked down at the water. They had kept the hold on roads since before he knew there was one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:289:They wore steel, and they wore it outside. The old man had a hatchet through his belt, over his coat, with the edge forward, and the woman carried a long knife on her thigh and had a boar spear slung in rings along the cart's side, where one pull would free it. Even the boy had a hedging bill, too big for him. None of it was hidden and none of it was held; it was simply there to be seen, like the courier's hatchet in its loop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:21:"Five, on the track," he said. "They are in file, a crew's length apart, with two mules. One has a spear. One favours a leg. The fifth I do not know; it is a woman, not young, who carries a staff and does not lean on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:97:The boy had stopped eating. The spear had said nothing, and the old woman had looked at Teague over her bowl as she had looked at him for nine cold terms whenever he was about to do something that she would have to bind afterward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:135:There was a camp at the track's end with two fires. He counted his own first: a spear, and a bow, and a boy, and an old woman with a board. Then he counted the camp, and there were six in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:259:She pushed the strip along the trestle to the right, past the founding stone. It was as long as a tall man, and the board ended a hand beyond the founding stone; so the strip went on over the end of it, with nothing under it, and hung down toward the clay like a tongue.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:13:They were long strips, each of them as long as a tall man and a hand and a half wide, of the thin paper that she rubbed stones with; and Brom brought them to her one at a time on his two palms. She took the first and read the corner. She had written there, on a floor with the sun going down, the stone's number, and its height, and the way that it faced.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:55:He did it on the eastern landing, with the scow's rail still under his hand. The spear was ashore already, and grounded. The boy had the two mules by one rein and was looking up the bank at the lower inn's chimney, where there would be something in a pot. The bow came off last, and stepped down onto the planks with the right foot, and stood on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:85:The ground he knew. He had been round the Fallow Ring eleven times, with carts, by the ruts, at noon, and had looked across the grass each time at a row of grey stumps and kept walking. Every carter's child knew what was said of it. He had never put it to the proof, and he did not need a paper to tell him why not: he had three behind him, and in that place the spear would be a man with a spear, and the bow a man with a bad hip, and the boy a boy. He would not take them in for a hundred, or for twice a hundred.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:229:Teague was there. He stood a little way out from the eave, on the trodden grass, with his thumbs in his belt, and his three behind him at their distances: the spear, grounded, and the bow on the good leg, and the boy with a heel of bread. That made four. The Oxhollow carter had come up from his mules and stood chewing, which was five.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:229:"Four," said Seln, presently. "One with a spear. One who favours a leg."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:281:But he could be half a day from where a certain paper pointed, with a spear and a bow and a boy, on honest business. Nobody could say that it rested on nothing, since it rested on three stakes and four marks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:305:The spear said nothing, which was what he said to most things. The bow grunted, and went on easing his leg. The boy put the bread into his mouth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:311:He set two stakes with the spear and the boy, in water to the thigh, while the bow sat on the bank and sharpened the third. He did the sum as he drove them: it was an afternoon up to that place, by the ruts, and a morning to go in and bring a case out, if it was a walk and a carry, so that they would be down at the ford again by the middle of the day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:325:Not one of the three asked him why. The spear took his spear. The bow got up off the bank, on the good leg first, and slung his stave; and Teague, who had been about to tell him that he might stay with the mill and the mules, saw his face and held his tongue.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:355:He sent the spear south-about by the ruts with the lantern, and the boy with him. They were gone the better part of two hours. They came back by the north, having walked the whole of the ruts, with a crossbow each and a third across the boy's shoulders.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:357:"Lying down," said the spear. He was not a man of many words. "On their sides. String cut. Nothing in the lock."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:381:He put the spear on the southern rim, where two had stood, and sent the boy west, since the boy had the legs for it. The bow he set on the north, which was the shortest walk, and he took the brow himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:45:The spear stood on the south, a tall man, grounded, with the butt of his weapon on the frozen turf and both hands on the shaft; and he watched a girl with one arm find an edge with her foot, sixty times over, and said nothing whatever. On the west it was the boy, sitting on his heels in the ruts with a heel of bread. He stopped chewing when Lira came abreast of him, and he had not started again when they were a hundred yards on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:335:It was full day at the top, and bright, and so warm after that room that Cael's face ached. The sun stood in the south-west over the rim, and they went up the old road in file, with Seln carrying the middle box and twenty-nine sheets rolled under Karis's arm; and on the southern rim a tall man watched them come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:337:He had stood there since first light. When Brom came over the lip of the floor with a tarred case on his good shoulder, the man lifted the butt of his spear a hand's breadth from the turf and brought it down again, once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:51:They came from the old iron, on the north side, and they went to the newer one, eight feet away, at the height of a knee and a chest and a hand above a tall man's head. He saw them arrive, and they did not creep; they swung across as a gate swings to, all three together, and caught on the newer bar, and ran on past it to the south until they were beyond his seeing. On the north side they ran away from the old iron along the turf in the same three courses, and went out of his sight in that direction too.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md:5:Teague's crew did it. Cael catalogued the order, because it was theirs and he wanted it kept as theirs: the tall woman first, with the spear laid down for the first time in a year and a stone the size of her head lifted in both hands; the broad man on his stick, who could not lift and did, one-handed, small stones, one at a time, from the stream-bed to the place, and did not stop when the older woman with the cloth bag put a hand on his good shoulder, so she took it off; Teague last, with the wrapped arm at his side and the other doing the work of two, laying the stone as he read a job, in order, from the feet up, as if the order mattered, which it did.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-22.md:9:No one answered the boy's crew had not said. That was the rule and nobody had made it; a crew that has heard a name said once at a stream-bed, by the man it was not said to, does not say it back, and the six had heard it once and would not, and did not ask it again. The stone went on until it was a shape, and the shape was the size of a boy with a pack too big for him, and then Teague put the last one on, at the head, and stood, and the tall woman picked up the spear.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md:21:He catalogued the eleven on the plain's rock, because it was the last halt before the thing and he wanted the count. Brom with the forearm bound and the good one carrying, on a foot that had walked to a city and in one and out of it. Lira with two arms and the third box, and her eyes on the rim ahead and not on him, because she had looked at him at a pass's mouth and had said nothing and had decided to go on saying it until he said something first. Karis with the case and the map, reading the rock as she read everything, for the strata, with the bound hand holding the notebook she had been told not to write in. Seln at the back, where the count was kept, with nothing on the plain to keep it against. Vastin on the stick, the twenty-eighth day of a month, walking as a man walks who has two days left on an order and means to keep them. Oryn with the roll on her back and the mule's rope in her hand, three days past the end of the road that had been hers, walking east because a man's ribs were owed a reading she had said she would not take until he had stood where he was going, and because ten people had walked into four quiet places and she had been in reach for one. Teague and his three — the tall woman with the spear; the broad man on the leg that bore today, as she had said it would, and limped worse, as she had said it would; the older woman with the cloth bag — walking a stride apart from the six, as a crew walks that has buried one of its own the day before and has not yet found the distance it wants to walk at. And himself, with the middle box, and the brass in his coat, and the ribs saying their one thing at every stride, and thirteen things held to nothing, and the count.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md:41:Teague's three sat at the rim while the cord went round, on the plain's rock outside the stumps, and did not help and were not asked to, because the pacing was the six's and the contract had said so. The tall woman watched Lira's sigil go out at the stride and come on at the stride back, twenty times, and at the twentieth she looked at her own hand, at the mark on it, and closed the hand, and did not open it again that afternoon. The broad man sat with the leg out and the crossbow across his knees and looked at the floor like water he had been told was deep. Teague stood. He did not sit all afternoon; he stood at the place where the path had come in between two stumps and read the floor as he read a job, and Cael, passing with the cord's far stone, saw him do it, and saw him come to the end of the reading with nothing moved, because there was nothing on the floor to move anyone to, and saw him not like that, and enter it, in his way, which was to stand there longer.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md:73:The sigils went first. He watched them go, because a healer had said it was what she read for and a woman with a cord had said it was the finding she was afraid of what came after: every mark on every hand at the line going dark at the stride, at the same stride, at the same instant — Lira's, and Brom's, and the tall woman's and the broad man's and Teague's, and Vastin's, on a hand that had held a plane across a stream-bed the day before, and Karis's, on a hand that was bound, and Oryn's. The same. A hundred lamps. He did not say the rest of it and neither did she, and she looked at her own dark hand as she had looked at a column, and entered it, and drew nothing.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md:97:He did not go in again. He stood a stride back from the line with his three — the tall woman with the spear grounded and lifted, the broad man on his stick with the leg that bore today, the older woman with the cloth bag — and read his people in the old order, face after face, and came to the gap, and went past it, and looked at the floor, and said, at the size he said a term: "My crew's stood at four of these. It's stood at enough." He looked at Cael. "Field's mine. This isn't field." And that was the contract, kept to its last line, by a man who had lost one of four to a line the day before and was not going to put the three across another, and Cael entered it as the contract's best clause, and the man's.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md:23:"And then?" the tall woman said. It was the first thing she had said to any of the six in a year.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md:31:Vastin first, with the stick, on the twenty-seventh day of a month, because the wall went where he stood and he had to stand where it went. Cael at his shoulder. Brom at Vastin's other shoulder, bare of everything but himself. Teague and the tall woman. Lira. The broad man on his stick with the crossbow across his back, and the young one beside him, because a man on a stick has a man beside him. Karis with the case and the notebook, and behind her Oryn and the older woman with the cloth bag, with the roll open across Oryn's arm already, because a healer who is going to be needed does not wait to be needed to open the roll. The mule at the mouth with Karis's boxes, tied to a stone, because a mule does not go into a place a man has drawn.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md:109:The brace went a body's length. Which was his, from heel to palm, and it was enough: the timber went out from under the stack and the stack went, not down the scree, because he had set the line to take it along the shelf and not off it, but *along*, onto the shelf's far end, where three hoods with crossbows had been standing and were not, because a thing that has been holding a shelf for a season and starts to move is a thing that everyone on the shelf moves away from. The high ground was Seln's. The far end was stone. The near end had the shelf's own stair down it, cut for the team's coming and going, and Teague was on the stair with the tall woman, and Lira was past both of them, because Lira was faster than anyone in the pass and had been since a hall.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md:117:Cael saw it from under the shelf's lip: the man's wrapped arm at his side, useless by a healer's order, and the other hand out, and the frame lined up the other way round, left-handed, awkward, correct, and a hood at the stair's head braced across it with a stave, and Teague's palm on the stave's middle, and the stave and the man behind it a body's length back onto the shelf and down, and the stair open, and the tall woman through it with the spear reversed, the butt and not the point, because nobody on the crew had been told to kill and nobody did. Lira was already on the shelf, with the flat of the blade, at the speed she had.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md:133:Brom on a stone with the forearm bound, looking at the fall that had closed the mouth behind them, pricing it, because Brom priced everything and the fall was a thing that had been built by hands. Lira on the shelf's stair, sitting, with the blade across her knees and her two hands on it, looking at nothing. The tall woman on the shelf with the spear grounded, not looking down, because a crew does not look at its captain when he is doing the thing he was doing. The older woman with the cloth bag kneeling by the broad man, whose shoulder she had bound, whose stick was beside him, who was not looking at anything either. Seln on the shelf's lip with the cache, watching the ridge, because somebody had to and it was always him.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md:47:Brom first, with the kit off and the box down on the crest and his shoulders bare of anything but himself, because a man who was going to be a rock did not carry. Then Teague, and Cael at his left hand, a stride back, to watch the exchange. Lira behind them, the flat of the blade out. The tall woman with the spear, then Vastin with the stick, then the broad man with the crossbow, then the young one beside him, because a man on a stick has a man beside him; then Karis with the case under her arm, on the path and on the count, because a wall counted who walked a site and she was the one who had found it; then Seln — and Seln was not on the path; Seln had gone off the crest's east side in the dark an hour before, and was somewhere along the perimeter with the cache, and Cael had a count of nine on the path and one that was not, as he had had at a cut. Oryn and the older woman a hundred paces up the path with the roll open on a rock.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md:97:Five inside, two strides in, and four on the shelf level with them — Brom at the sink's lip with the rock in his hand, the broad man beside him with the crossbow across his back and the stick, Teague and the tall woman between them and the iron — and the line ran from the drop across all the shelf and two strides into the quiet, and nothing working in five of them but the one who did not count, and steel, and their feet.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md:117:Cael had not seen it; he had been counting, and looking at the watch. He heard it — a foot on the lip, the limp set wrong on loose ground at the shelf's edge where the line had ended at the sink, on a leg that did not walk well — and the man was gone, over, onto the slope three men's height down, and the loose stone went with him, and Teague, on the shelf a stride from him with the wrapped wrist at his side, did not turn round, because his crew was not the thing on the shelf that wanted doing, and the tall woman did not either, and Cael entered that as the hardest thing he had watched a captain do, and the most correct.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-02.md:59:Teague's crew came down from the upper inn while the bread was still on the table, because a ferry was a thing every crew came down for, and they came in by the road door in the order they walked in — the tall woman with the spear first, the broad man with the crossbow and the limp, the young one, Teague last, the healer of no Path not with them — and each of them, passing the door end, looked at the brown coat for exactly as long as a crew looks at a man alone at a plank town, which is the length of a step, and did not look again. Teague looked a step longer. He had spent a career reading what a man's hands had done, and the hands on the table had signed things, and Teague saw that, and filed it wherever Teague filed things, and went to the plank to read the fourteen slips as if there were forty.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-18.md:21:"And five that aren't." The look went past him, to the road, where Teague's four stood with the tall woman's spear grounded and the young one looking at the ford as the young look at everything, and to the fifth, the older woman with the cloth bag, who was looking at the lean-to as a woman looks at another woman's table. Oryn looked at her. The older woman looked back. It lasted as long as it took, and neither of them nodded, and neither of them looked away first, and then the older woman looked at Brom's shoulder, once, as she had looked at it in a marsh and across a room, and Oryn looked at it too, and then they had both looked at the same thing and priced it the same, and that was the whole of the exchange between the two healers on the bearing, then or after, and Cael entered it as complete.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-18.md:47:Teague, on the road, said nothing, which was the right answer, and Cael watched him hear it and file it, because the man filed things too, in his way — a healer of no Path and a healer of Tide, and both of them on their own terms, and neither of them his to move — and the tall woman moved the spear a hand's width, and that was Teague's crew hearing it, and it was done.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-18.md:169:Teague first, on the road above the ford, with the wrist wrapped and the hand that was on the end of it hanging as it hung, and the four of his behind him — the tall woman with the spear grounded and lifted, the broad man with the crossbow across his back and the limp set for the day, the young one with a pack that was too big for him and was going to get lighter, and the older woman with the cloth bag, who did not carry a weapon and never had. Then the crew's: Karis with the case and the map, and the map with the straightedge's line on it — he had laid the brass from Denvash along the bearing the night before by the lantern, and she had drawn along it, once, with the pencil, from the fourth circle out onto the blank sheet to the divider's point and past it, a line true to a hair on paper across a country that had none — and the bound hand holding the notebook with the other one writing, because a week was a week and she had not agreed to it. Brom with the kit and the first box. Lira with the third box and two arms. Seln at the back, where the count was kept. Vastin with the stick, day twenty, on the road, where a stick could be put down and would not be. And Oryn, with the roll on her back by its strap, and her hands out of her coat, walking as she walked to everything, square to it, on a road that ran east for two days with her holds and then did not, and she had said so, and was walking it anyway, on her terms, which were the only kind.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md:79:He came to the plank as he had come to it in the snow, before the room had decided what the term was; and the tall woman with the spear at the table behind him did not turn round, and the broad man with the crossbow shifted his weight off the limp and did not turn round either, and the young one at the table's end put down what he was eating and looked, because he was young and had not yet learned not to, and the fifth — the older woman with the cloth bag — looked at Brom's shoulder across the room, once, and went back to her cup.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md:99:Teague went back to his table, and the tall woman moved the jug a hand's width to make room for his elbow without looking at him, which was the whole of a crew, and Cael stood at the plank and priced it honestly, because the ledger was going to be his and the ledger did not take a price that had been softened at a plank.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md:123:"Then you know what it costs. Don't put it in front of anything that needs doing twice." And he was through the road door and at his table with the wrist under it, where nobody had to look at it, and the tall woman had moved the jug again.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md:179:"Good." He went out, and the tall woman in the passage behind him, who had not been in the room and had heard all of it, had the jug.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-24.md:109:Teague took it with the good hand and put it in his coat without reading the outside, because the man did not read and had said so, and looked at Cael across the dead fire with his three behind him — the tall woman with the spear, the broad man on the leg that bore, the older woman with the cloth bag — and the gap where a fourth had stood, which he had read past every morning for three mornings and read past now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:88:36. **KEEP — The pass (306th).** Seln's reconnaissance (the shelf, the rig, nine or more, three crossbows). "It's through." The doctrine is told to Teague's four; **the young one's whole-head nod**. **The tall woman speaks to the six for the first time.** [B7-reconciled 2026-10-11: amendment 6] The wall is set as a lean-to slant. Brom's forearm is bolted before the wall goes up. The pine and stack fall down the plane and close the mouth behind them. The scree is swept clean. Seln climbs. The broad man is hit at the slant's high edge; the young one drags him and is **killed by the sixth volley**, a stride outside the plane. Oryn's hands on him: the first time they come off with nothing done. The name is said once. [OWNER-pending #54] Seln goes over the lip at the seventh volley. **Cael's first use of Force** on the second stack's brace (stone, along the shelf); the recoil stops in the ribs. Teague takes the stair left-handed. The tall woman reverses her spear. The team breaks east along the ridge, none taken. Vastin goes down; Karis sits with her hand on his shoulder. Teague kneels. "A wall keeps everyone behind it, and one of mine wasn't." At the far mouth Cael understands the line was not drawn in ink.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:110:| **NEW — The silent yard** (cold-weather learning bouts with Teague's crew) [B7-reconciled 2026-10-11: amendment 6] | absent | 3 bouts × ~1,500 = 4,500 | No Paths, steel and bodies only, in the frozen meadow. Cael loses to the tall woman's spear reach (she never speaks), then corrects. One-armed Lira coaches from the bench. The rival clock appears on the page. This must not touch the sequence locks in §13 (no wall drill; Seln teaches nobody). |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:114:| **NEW — Water at the seep** (301st–302nd, dry camps) | "two camps with no water" | 3,000 | The two crews must water themselves and a mule at a seep where shale-backs stand on a slope. Teague's field captaincy is shown. The young one moves too fast and the tall woman stops him with a hand. That is his habit, set up for the pass. |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:153:The tall woman, the broad man and the older healer get two or three beats each, inside Teague's arc. **Keep every member of Teague's crew unnamed** (canon: "keep unnamed unless a later card needs one"; never invent a name for an existing role).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:326:- **The pass (M9).** A dry defile a hundred strides long with a loose-stone stream-bed. The west wall is scree to a shelf two-thirds up, carrying two timber-braced stacks, one with a roped dead pine; the east wall is sheer. The wall is a lean-to slant, high edge at the west wall ahead, low edge at the mouth behind. There are seven numbered volleys: Brom's forearm (1); the wall up (2); the fall (4); the broad man (5); the young one (6); Seln over the lip (7). Cael's push takes the second stack along the shelf, not off it. Teague goes up the stair left-handed; the tall woman reverses her spear. The team breaks along the ridge. **Win sentence:** Seln removed the team's ability to hold the high ground by climbing the slope its own rig had swept clean, and the wall bought him the climb.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:465:- **The tall woman says nothing to any of the six** until her question at the pass. **The broad man says nothing to them** until "Aye" (305th). **The older healer never speaks to them.** **The young one's name is never written** (said once, by the broad man, in the stream-bed). [OWNER-pending #54]
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md:189:"It's west," she said. "Treswick. The last Compact city on the Line. There's a registry office on the old town, and under the old town there's an archive that was there before the Compact was — the Deepstacks, the scholars call it, when they call it anything — records nobody converted because nobody was ordered to and nobody needed to. Whatever these are" — her hand on the oilcloth — "if there's a word for them anywhere on the continent, it's in a book under that registry that nobody has opened since before there was a registry to open it. And there's a provision. The old scholars' provision. It lets a registered historical-studies practitioner in. I'm not one, out here. There's a man there who is."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-10.md:5:Rock first. The quarry's own rock, cut in the days before anyone cut rock square, in galleries that went off from the landing in three directions at a height a man could walk in and a width two men could pass in, and the cut-marks still on every face of it, the marks of tools nobody made any more, running as the quarrymen had run them and not as a mason would. Then the shelving, which was not shelving as the Compact shelved a thing — no cases, no doors, no labels — but timber pinned into the rock floor to roof along both walls of every gallery, grey with age and dry as bone, and on the timber, the records.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md:43:The line was sharp. It was a hair sharp, as it had been at every site: a stride outside, Lira's sigil lit; a stride inside, gone; nothing between. The circle was perfect. Karis said so at the twentieth cord and again at the eightieth, in the voice for a finding, because a circle that stays perfect across eighty cords is a circle and not a guess. And it was larger, and it was older, and she had the first from the cord — "A thousand and twelve. Across. Call it a thousand" — and the second from the stumps.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md:45:She read the stumps between cords. Every one she passed. The marks on the inner faces were the same make; he had known that from the first between the shale and the rim, and knew it now from twenty of them: the rows, the bearers, the lean. But she did not read the marks. She read the *cuts*. She put her good hand into a row on a stump's inner face and ran a finger along the cut's bottom, and did it at the next stump, and the next, and at the twelfth she said the thing.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-20.md:13:The carter's sheet, with the four circles and the pencil line through them and the blank sheet pinned to its edge with the line running on across it to the divider's point. The kit's ledger, open at the index. The Compact's records in their rows — five rows now, because the Span's sheets had a row of their own, and the sheets that had never been laid out at a spring had a row too, at the end, that she had not read aloud to anyone. The fifty-two sheets of copied walls from the autumn, rolled, and the Span's piers copied that morning, flat, weighted at the corners with shale. The leaf's copy, in her hand and his, with the gap drawn. The list of parts. The dividers, closed. The two brass edges, side by side along the line.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-20.md:31:She laid the index against the records, then, and the records against the walls, and it was the work of an hour and he watched the whole hour and did not enter any of it, because it was a reconciliation and a reconciliation was one entry, at the end, when the columns agreed.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-20.md:45:She turned the records to the fifth column.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-20.md:97:"Six years," Karis said. She had the deep-layer sheet under her hand, the one from the spring with the office's *six years* on it in the office's rounding, and she looked at it beside the directive and beside the index, and did the thing she did, which was to say what the papers said and not one word past it. "Six years back, somebody measured all of it, and graded it, and ordered the grades to be held. Somebody was administering this — the sites, the marks, all of it — recently enough to keep records, and to fight over the records, and to seal the fight. Documented as *stable*. Somebody's still writing that word down." She looked at the fire. "The Compact. That's who keeps records. That's who has a ruling hand and a seat and a seal. I'm not going to say *someone else*, because I've no sheet for someone else, and I've a whole file for them."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-20.md:141:"Because every other site, my Path worked while everyone's died. I want to know what the *oldest* one does to me. I think it's going to tell me something the records can't."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-19.md:13:The circle first, as it had been. Eight hundred metres across by Karis's cords, twice the others; the ground dropping away inside it on the north side into a sink, a bowl where the earth had gone down and stayed down, and at the middle, four hundred metres in past the iron, the shape against the last of the sky — not a mound, not a hall; a curve, with a crown taller than a man, and dark under it where the sky came through. The iron round all of it, a hand's height out of the ground, a pace apart, the number and the stroke-and-bar on every stake, and the line Lira had called in the autumn a hundred and ninety paces below the crest, where it had been.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-06.md:5:It was a stone because Seln said it was, and because the border road's makers had set one there, before the walls, to say so: a squared post of grey rock at the road's edge on the evening of the two hundred and eightieth day, a day's walk west of the spring where the records had been read that morning, with a mark cut in its west face that counted down toward the Line and a second mark, older and worn nearly smooth, cut in its north face, that counted toward nothing the Compact had ever measured. The road went on west over a long rise with the sun going down behind it. The track went north-west, off the kept surface and onto ruts, toward a gap in the hills that had the last light in it, and Oryn looked at the gap once as they came up to the stone, and did not look at it again, and Cael, who had watched her not look at a road before, at another fork, in another season, catalogued the not-looking and let it be.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-05.md:1:# Chapter 5 — What the Records Say
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-03.md:13:"Three things," Vastin said. "In the order I decided on the road. I'll tell you the order and then I'll keep to it. First the records. Then the page. Then the thing in the records that I could not get past, which is the reason for the other two, and which I would have told you first if I were a different kind of man."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-03.md:21:The records first.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-03.md:57:He set it apart. He set it on the table's bare end, past the sixth row, and did not slide it toward Karis, and did not look at Cael, and Cael understood that the setting-apart was part of the order the man had decided on the road: the records were a stack; this was a page; a stack could be sorted and a page could only be read.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-03.md:59:"I copied it from a record older than the section it was filed in," Vastin said. "The classified history is not one history. It is an office's habit, kept for a long time, of putting a thing on a shelf it does not want read, and the shelf is older than the office. The oldest things on it are not Compact records at all. They are what the Compact found when it built the office, and did not throw away, because throwing a thing away is a decision and filing it is not. This was on the oldest shelf. It is a reference — a line in a longer record, citing another record that the Compact does not hold — and it is the only line in all the classified history that names a designer."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-03.md:91:Vastin did not take it out of anything. It was already on the table. It had been on the table since the first row went down, because it was in the records, and he had known where, and had let Karis lay it in its place among the survey sheets and had waited for her to be done sorting before he said which sheet it was, because that, too, was the order.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-03.md:127:Because Karis had. She had the notebook open and the pencil going now, and she was not writing the line again; she was writing the three questions she was going to ask the records in the morning, in the order she had them, and the questions were hers, and the records were hers, and the page on the bare end of the table was, whatever else it was, a place she was going to go, and the man who had carried all of it three hundred miles on a knee that said so watched her take it the way a man watches a thing he used to do being done by somebody else, and did not reach for it, and Cael saw the not-reaching cost him, and saw him pay it.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-16.md:119:He took the straightedge out of his coat, and the dividers came with it, and he put the dividers back and kept the edge. "Now we find out how old the oldest one is. Karis has a bearing and the marks, and the records to read them against. There's a site at the end of the line that's been quiet since they built the machine." He looked at the straightedge in his hands. "I want to stand in the middle of it."
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-04.md:27:Seven of them on it. Six, and the mule, and a man with a stick who walked at the back of the column where a man walks who has not been told where to walk and has decided that the back is where he can do the least harm. Oryn walked beside the mule with her hand on its rope and her roll on her back and the road west under her feet, which was not her road — her road went north from a fork on the far side of the shoulder hold, seven holds on it, and she had said nothing about the fork and nobody had asked her, because that was the condition. Lira walked ahead, on her own foot, with two arms swinging, thirteen days released and still, Cael saw, putting the left hand into her coat and taking it out again to make sure it went. Brom with the shoulder carried and the kit on the other one. Karis with the records flat in their oilcloth under her arm and the map in her coat and the two brass edges in her pocket, walking as she had walked the void roads with a straightedge open in her hand, looking at the ground ahead as if a line were drawn on it, which, on this road, there was: somebody had drawn it a long time ago, with instruments, and somebody else had been mending it since.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-04.md:171:Nobody answered that. It was not a thing that wanted an answer. Oryn went back to Brom's foot, and Karis came down the cut with the mule and the records under her arm and looked at the ice and the grey and the plane that was not there any more, and said, to the notebook, "Note the day," and wrote it, and the cut was a cut again, with a road through it that somebody kept.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md:27:The spring, on the evening of the two hundred and ninety-fifth: the stone-lined trough and the flat slab where the records had been laid out in rows with the wind at their backs. The wind was in their faces now. Nobody laid anything out. Beyond the spring the road went up over the long rise, and on the near side of the rise was the fork where a track went north to seven holds, and Karis at the trough did not look at it, because it was not her track. He looked at it for her. A day south of the last of the seven was a ford called Millrace, on the Stair road, and by a count he had kept since a bank in the autumn Oryn would be at Millrace's table on the two hundred and ninety-eighth, and so would they, and the count was the same for everyone, and he had not said so to anyone, and did not now.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md:131:He catalogued the table before she started, because she was going to change it. The carter's sheet, with its four circles and the bearing drawn through them in pencil and running on off the paper's edge, and a second sheet pinned to that edge, blank, cut in the winter for the bearing to run onto. The kit's ledger, open at the index. The Compact's records in their rows — the three rows from a spring, and the sheets that had been under the same cover that day and had not been laid out, because there had been no row for them: sites nobody at that spring had heard named, filed with the others because an office files what it has. The list of parts, copied, in her hand and his. The dividers, closed. And the two straightedges, brass on brass, lying along the bearing as they had lain since a landing in the snow.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md:147:Not old by the stone. Old by the *paper*. The records dated each site by its first sheet — the first time a stake had been stamped, the first time anybody with a pen had walked to it and written it down — and she read the dates off in the order the rows came, and set the dividers on the map at each circle as she read.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-17.md:155:"Third-tier," Karis said. "*Stable across centuries.* Documented back to the first sheet the office ever kept — before the current office, before the one before it; the form's own head calls that the setting, and doesn't say of what. The sheet's a copy of a copy, and says so, and the hand that made the first one is a hand nobody at that registry could read, and they filed it anyway because it had a stamp." The finger, above the point. "It's been quiet since there was paper to say so. Nobody's walked to it in the records. It hasn't changed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:36:- **Five sites lie on one bearing, ages ascending east.** The court is the fifth and the oldest, about 1,107 yards across, and the end of the line. [B7-reconciled 2026-10-11: amendment 5] It is **unnamed** ("the court", lowercase). The taxonomy is decoded: a stroke alone, a stroke and bar, a stroke and two bars.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:62:10. **KEEP — The handover (277th evening)**, in Vastin's stated order. First, the records: a courier's flat of six years of survey abstracts, the anomaly column ("Total"), six rows, "More than four", and the completion report as the last sheet. Second, the page: "—of the Architect: see the lower archive at Treswick, which holds it in full—". Third, the technician's sheet: the deep-layer line and the brush bar; Karis copies it and writes "not read". Seln goes still and does not say the sum. Vastin's vertigo; Karis owns the response. Cael's hand stays a hand's width from the page. Nobody says *west*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:90:38. **KEEP — The court (307th).** It lies low, and the plain is worn down to it. about 1,107 yards across (159 cords) [B7-reconciled 2026-10-11: amendment 6]. The stratum under everything. The first cut ("The others are copies"). The bearing ends, with no sixth. The council. **At the line:** every sigil dark at the same stride; the reactions: Lira's "Right."; Brom pricing the stump; Karis's "It's the first one."; Seln's count; Vastin's line (§10). Oryn steps in one stride: "Nothing. Everything, round it. Same as you." It is unanswered. Teague steps in one stride and steps back: "My crew's stood at four of these. It's stood at enough … Field's mine. This isn't field." The turning begins. Lira's fixed exchange. "Wait here."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:135:- **The court's pacing** (≈2,000). The 159 cords with Lira on the line and the count spoken.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:171:| 3 | The Border Road | 13–19 | 34,000 | S4, S5, S6 | First fight and first wall; the records read three ways; the sum Cael does not write; Oryn routes north with the promise in the log. |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:221:| 280 | The spring (records); the fork-stone at evening |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:280:- Iron Wall's first showing; the records' grade; the reading inward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:366:17. **Pole two (S16):** Lira: "So the whole time they weren't afraid you'd break something. They were afraid you'd *exist*." Cael: "Since my Kindling I thought the system forgot to make room for me," … "It didn't forget. It was *built* so there'd be no room. Four hundred years of a whole world, so that I wouldn't happen." … "I happened anyway." / Lira: "Yeah." … "Now what." / Cael: "Now we find out how old the oldest one is. Karis has a bearing and the marks, and the records to read them against. There's a site at the end of the line that's been quiet since they built the machine." … "I want to stand in the middle of it." [B7-reconciled 2026-10-11: OWNER-pending #48]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:407:- **S20 close:** "It's here," Karis said. "Third-tier, documented four hundred years back — as old as the record, as old as the Architect, as old as the machine. The first one they made, or near it." / "And it's on the line." / "It's the *end* of the line. Oldest-last. Everything before it was practice, or maintenance, or — I don't have the word. This is the one that's been quiet since before quiet had a name." … "You still want to stand in the middle of it." / "More than I've wanted anything since I was fourteen." / "Why." / "Because every other site, my Path worked while everyone's died. I want to know what the *oldest* one does to me. I think it's going to tell me something the records can't." [OWNER-pending #57] / He was right. He did not yet know how right.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:439:- **C2 — Academy-era spans given as numbers.** Examples: "The Compact spent four years trying to file this boy" (S2); "I read your file for four years" (S7); the cache carried "four years" (S11, S16); "in the four years Cael had known him" (S5); "it took four years and a hearing" (S8, a fixed line). These sit against the locked timeline (Kindling at fourteen, academy from about sixteen) and OWNER-DECISIONS #9/#18 ("since his Kindling", no number, for B4–B6). **Edition:** no numeric span since Kindling. Anchor academy/cache spans to events; office survey records may keep their own six-year rounding where explicitly identified as the office's. The protected S8 four-year line remains word for word under [OWNER-pending #53] and is never repeated as fact. [B7-reconciled 2026-10-11: amendment 11]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:444:- **C7 — Reveal locus.** CANON_RULES says "ancient records in edge-territory ruins"; the bible says a "pre-Compact archive at a border city". The source stages both: the leaf is in the Treswick archive and its key is the ruins' carvings. **Flag for the bible pass.**

