editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md-39-He had a question, and he had not yet found the words for it. It concerned the name at the head of the new sheet. A man had come in the night before, Pike had said at the plank, with news of a cart in a gorge; and for the whole of that man's time at the oak a sheet of cream paper had been lying under a river stone, where anybody who read a plank from end to end must come to it.
