editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:55:"We know what we may not do," she said. "No sigil, anywhere on that hill, for any reason. I should like to know what we may."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:65:He knew a clerk's way through a rule when he was the one finding it. He had been on the wrong end of such ways since he was fourteen, and he knew exactly what they were worth to the person on the other side. He shut the read, and kept it shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:137:"May I read that?" he said. "The ankle. It's the one thing I've got that does nothing unless there's a hand on somebody. I can't shut it for you. I could say how it lies."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:167:He did not deny it. A Force paid where the line came out of him. Every time that he had put his Path through a thing, the thing had gone where he sent it, and something of the same size had come home along the bone; and the bone kept the account. He had known that at twenty, and had not then known what the sum would look like with a good many cold terms of it added up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:1:# Chapter 6 — West, and Inside
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:245:"There is a town on the border, inside the Line. It is called Treswick. It has a wall. Under the wall, and older than the wall, there is an archive that was cut out of the rock before the registry had an office anywhere. The few who have written of it call it the Deepstacks. I do not think that anybody has been down into the oldest part of it since before the Compact."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:289:*The ferry runs when the ice goes. Karis has a bearing, an index she can't read, and a language she can't read, and she says the answers are in a border archive nobody's opened since before the Compact. Since my Kindling I've stayed out of registries because a registry is where they file you. She wants me to walk into the oldest one on the continent. The kit reads everyone but me. So does the world, mostly. Nineteen. Ice goes next week. West, and inside, for the first time since the academy above Ostrand — which is a sentence I did not think I'd write again.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:61:In the west each of them had been a line in a registry: an Ember of the third rank in Iron, and a Wind of the first; an Iron Skin who had stopped at Copper and said so without shame; and a Shadow, Bronze, whom fifteen years of service had taught to be overlooked. Nobody out here had asked any of them for a tier since the ninth day. The board at the foot of the meadow priced the job and never the person. By its reckoning the five of them stood eleventh among the crews on a sheet of sailcloth, which was all the rank that this country kept.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:189:He held it where it was. It was a defector's craft, and in any hands but its owner's it would tell a trained eye exactly who had stood near enough to teach it; and the man who had stood near enough was ten feet off, with his back turned, keeping a door. Cael had opened it twice out here, where nobody could see who might carry the seeing west. He did not mean to open it a third time for the sake of a page in a book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:87:Seln had stood at the north-west peg throughout, with his hands in his sleeves. He had a peeled hazel wand under one arm, and before the pairs were made he had cut a notch in it with his thumbnail, as he had on every morning since a clerk's bag went over the river. There were eighteen notches now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:167:He did not deny it. A Force paid where the line came out of him. Every time that he had put his Path through a thing, the thing had gone where he sent it, and something of the same size had come home along the bone; and the bone kept the account. He had known that at twenty, and had not then known what the sum would look like with a good many cold terms of it added up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:1:# Chapter 6 — West, and Inside
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:3:They came down to Thornwater's ford in the forenoon of the two hundred and sixty-fifth day, and Cael did not at first know the place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:35:She let him. She went into the river on the whole of her foot, as she had gone everywhere for the best part of two hundred days, and at the third stride it had her to the knee, and at the sixth to the thigh. Cael saw her stop, and feel about with the big boot, and shift a little upstream.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:61:Inside the fence the yard was as it had been, with the geese drawn up along the barn wall and the well in the middle, and a bench under the back room's window. The last of that day's people was getting up from it as they came in, an old man with a hand bound in clean linen; and behind him, drying her fingers one at a time on a strip of canvas, stood the healer. She was a Tide, of the fourth rank in Iron, and nobody at that gate had ever asked her for the figure.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:67:She had not asked after that animal in a hundred and forty-three days, by letter or by carter, and she did not ask now. The brown mule had lifted her head at the well. She walked across the flagstones with her pack on, between the geese, and stopped with her nose against a broad square chest; and Oryn's right hand went out, and found a small brown cheek where it had always been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:79:She had Lira on the bench on the two hundred and sixty-sixth morning, as soon as there was light to see by, with the frost still white on the flags.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:87:"Five months and twenty days."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:95:"I have. I've had my hands round that arm oftener than round most, and each time I've put it back in the wool myself, because you'd have had it out in a day if I'd left it to you." Oryn did not stir from where she stood. "I'm not putting it back. I'm not taking it out, either. It's been mine for a season. It stops being mine when you take it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:123:"I've felt it going wrong for two hundred days," said Lira's voice. "Get on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:129:She walked to the well, and it was not how she had walked since the fifty-third day. She went up on the ball of that foot and came down, like a girl at a rail trying a floor, and did it again. Then she stood on the right leg alone, with her two arms out, the thin one and the other; and all four of them saw her face, which she had forgotten to keep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:147:"I came off a mountain in this," she said. "I went round three of those places in it, and up a wall, and over that river yesterday. There's a whole shirt of mine in the toe. I don't know what a person does with it now."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:169:She told it as she would have entered it. The fourth lay two days beyond the fork, under the end of a ridge, and it was twice the size of the others, by the bend of its edge. There was a fence of iron round it, of the registry's pattern, with a bar to every pace and one number on every bar. Something stood in the middle that five people had looked at in the dusk and could not make out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:171:"Four hundred and forty yards, and two strides, from the iron to its middle," said Karis. "I had that by the bend, from outside. I have not one sheet of the place."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:185:She made no reply to that, and he had not looked for one. She had told six people in a loft that at any edge somebody ought to be standing on the right side with her sleeves pushed up; and he watched her understand, with her eyes on a ring of pencil, that five of them had taken her at her word on a hillside two days from anywhere, and had turned round. When she bent to the fourth ring again, she had lost her place on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:189:He had one more thing for her, which was not on the hide. He had carried it for ninety-eight days, and nobody had asked him to bring it over a river. But he was standing at the plank where he had once lain still and let her spend herself on him, and he found that he could not stand there and keep a thing back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:209:"I'm on the road in the morning. Lowmarch is my first stop after a thaw, and the mill's my second; they have been for thirty rounds, and I'd be walking down to that ferry tomorrow if I'd never set eyes on the five of you." She did not turn round. "So I'll be on your road for two days, since it's mine as well. I'd have that understood. Nobody asked me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:215:They were six at the lower inn's hearth on the night of the two hundred and sixty-eighth, with a hundred and eighteen marks in the purse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:219:"A hundred and sixteen. Five on. Three off," said Brom. "A hundred and eighteen in hand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:225:Cael counted the board, as he counted any room. There was a big man with one arm bound and six bowls laid; and a girl who kept turning her left hand over on the wood, as though it had come back to her from a mender's; and a small pale woman with a hide; and a quiet one with his back to the wall; and at the end, on her feet, a healer who had walked two days down a saddle because it was her road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:253:"It has a gate, with a book in it," said Seln. He spoke to the fire, as he did when he would rather not have been asked. "It has a registry office over that archive, and a station on its crown. There is a paper of ours that executes on the hour when any of us shows a certificate inside the Line; I told you so on the ninth day, and nothing has changed it. And there is one among us whose file is not shut."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:271:"I'm not ruling tonight," he said. "Nothing leaves this bank till the ice goes, and the ferryman says that's a week. I'll hear it again in daylight, when I've slept on it. All of it, with the six."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:279:He took a lit lantern down to the ferryman's shed at dusk on the two hundred and seventieth day, and he carried it by the chain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:281:He had begun a cold term in that doorway, with a mule and a frozen trough. The mule was there still, a hundred and thirty-nine days on, with her fee paid. The trough was open water. He went out to the end of the planks before he sat down, and gave the read the ground, as he had on the first morning; and he could feel the shelf going. It was grey, and soft, and full of water, and it took his weight on one side and let it drain away on the other. Out in the channel the river ran black from bank to bank between two rims of rotten ice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:287:*Two hundred and seventieth day.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:1:# Chapter 5 — A Hair
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:3:Karis drove her hazel peg into the inn yard at noon on the hundred and sixty-eighth day, with a dead man's mallet, and it took her three blows.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:93:There were five people in a room that had been built for sacks, and the only sound in it was the fire on the other side of the wall. Cael looked at two strips of brass. One had come from Ternhall in a researcher's coat, and one from Denvash in a bag, and he had laid both of them along a pencil line at a hearth thirty-seven days ago and found them true. He felt the cold of that yard in his stomach.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:109:Nobody answered her. She shut the notebook on the hair, and put the two edges back into their cloths, each in its own, and would not be spoken to for the rest of that day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:117:On the night of the hundred and seventieth Karis laid the dead man's cord out along the floor of the store, from the door to the meal-sacks and back, and measured it with a ruler.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:123:She had done this once before, on the hundred and sixteenth evening, at a fire, on the day after that cord came up out of the ground. She had the figure in her trials leaf. She did not look at it until she had her new one written down, since a person who knows what she is hoping to find will find it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:125:Then she took the leaf out of her coat. *Thirty-one, and a piece under half.* The cord had not altered by the width of her pencil in fifty-four days. She did the second knot, and the third, and they were as the first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:131:The two edges were sound as well. She had laid each to its own pricks six times since the hundred and sixty-eighth, at different hours, with her hands warm and with them cold, and they had not once shown her a hair. They agreed with one another, and with the table. There was nothing whatever the matter with any of the three things, and it was their soundness that she could not put down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:135:She had not made it for brass. She had made it as a girl, in a hall with a counter in it, on the day when a registrar laid a card before her that said *Iron*, and a numeral, and *certified*. The girl beside her had wept at hers. Karis had read her own twice, and had asked what instrument it had been taken with, and how that instrument was kept true; and the registrar had looked at her as at a child who inquires what the sky is fastened to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:155:*Hundred and sixty-eighth: one cord, two edges. Four hands agree. One does not, by a piece and by a hair. Cord re-measured on the hundred and seventieth: sound. Cause: not found. No inference entered.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:175:She had turned that over until it was as smooth as a river stone. Two had the mark, and two had not. The two that had it were not the two largest, since the Stair was of one size with the others. They were not the two nearest to anything. A girl with her arm in a sling had pointed out, on a morning by a marsh, which way the numbers ran, and Karis had entered the order and declined to say what was being ordered. She declined still. She had been at that mark for fifty-five days, and she could not have said what it counted, or whether it counted anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:201:He laid the lane on the hundred and seventy-sixth night, in the meadow below the ash, when the three fires were covered. Storm was the seventh of the ten, a Gold's worth of weather that had come to him out of a bout with a Gold, and it was the heaviest thing that he carried. It wanted the air. To give it the air he had to take the ground from the read, since at that weight he could have one or the other; and it was like shutting an eye.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:221:He learned something from each losing. But what he learned most from was a game that Teague set on the hundred and ninetieth day, with a sack of meal in the middle of the square and three people at either end. Whoever carried it over the other side's pegs had it. Cael stood with Seln and Karis, since they were the three of the five who had two arms apiece; and he turned to them to say what he thought they should do.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:233:The river was a river again at the middle on the two hundred and thirtieth day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:235:It had been coming for a week. The ferryman had pulled his green on the two hundred and twenty-fourth, bough by bough, and stood them against his rope-house. The road-ice went grey, and then it went dull, and a carter who tried it against advice put a wheel through at the landing's foot and was laughed at. By the two hundred and thirtieth morning there was a lane of black water down the channel, as wide as the scow was long, with the shelves standing white on either hand and cakes of ice turning in the current.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:237:"A hundred," said Seln.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:241:"A desk has had a hundred days. I had thought that I knew what one would do with them. I find that I should have liked to be wrong by more." He looked across the black lane at the far bank. "It cannot cross yet. The ferryman says that she is six weeks and more from carrying a boat, with those shelves. I shall begin a second wand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:243:The light had come back before the warmth did. Then the warmth came, by day, and the eaves ran. At night it froze again, so that every morning the meadow was a sheet of glass with last year's grass standing up through it. Lira walked on it with her foot set down whole and her eyes on the ground. She had not fallen since a road in the frost, and she did not mean to begin; and each evening she went down to the gravel bar and stood looking at the saddle to the east, where the snow still lay to the top.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:259:Cael had not touched the cord again. Karis had not offered it, and he had not asked; it lay coiled in its tarred case beside a stake that had never been driven, and one pale hair lay pressed in the fold of a notebook, and for ninety-five days the two of them had kept his ruling as carefully as if either had known what it was a ruling about.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:261:Karis said the other thing on the two hundred and sixty-third.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:1:# Chapter 4 — A Pace and a Half
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:3:On the hundred and fifty-eighth morning there were twenty-eight notches in Seln's hazel wand, and a boy of sixteen was standing in front of the bench with his cap in his two hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:27:"The book and my spear. It's the tenth day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:53:He had no answer ready for a pole with two ends. He stood off, and the tall one stood, and neither of them moved for some time. Then the rag twitched toward him, a foot, no more. His legs had been waiting ten days for that beat, and they went. There had been no *out*. He walked straight onto the point, which had never left home, and it stopped him by the breastbone as a hand stops a child at a door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:71:It had gone blue on the hundred and fifty-third night, as the ferryman had said that it would, with a groan that woke half the inn. He had walked it the next morning from bank to bank with an iron bar, sounding at every tenth stride, and had come home and cut spruce boughs. By noon there was a double row of them standing in the ice, a cart's width apart, going away up the north water as far as a person could see: his track. It swung wide at the bend above the landing, a furlong out of the straight, to keep clear of the place where the side water came in under the western bank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:75:The carts had kept to it. There had been six in seven days, coming down from the top holds on runners and wheels, with hides and tallow and the long grey sacks that meant salt. Each of them had lifted a hand to the keg in passing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:77:On the hundred and sixty-first day Cael was on the rope-house roof with a mouth full of nails. It was the job that Brom had put his hand up for, at five, and they had been at it on and off since the fence, whenever the frost let a shingle be split. Seln knelt at the ridge with a hammer. Brom stood on the ladder's fourth rung and passed shingles up left-handed, a dozen at a time, in a rope sling. Below them, at the shed's door, Lira had the brown mule by the cheek-strap and was telling her what she thought of a beast that ate a ferryman's straw and did nothing for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:239:*Hundred and sixty-first day. A cart through the river at the bend, eighty yards out. The carter, his two mules and the cart brought off on the ferryman's line, wound in on his drum by Oryn's mule, with Lira leading her. Brom fetched the man out lying down, left-handed. Seln had the beasts.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:253:He turned to the other leaf, and filled a line that he had ruled twenty-eight days ago.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:263:"I have left this for thirty-four days," she said. "I did not wish to read another person's findings until I had finished entering my own. I have finished."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:281:"Then it will wait for your eyes." She shut the book, and ran the cord through her fingers to the first knot. "On the hundred and sixty-eighth I shall want all five of you in the inn yard at noon, with dry hands. I have had this cord for forty-six days and I have not yet seen anybody measure with it. It is time that we learned how he did."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:1:# Chapter 1 — The Count Stopped Moving
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:7:So Cael took her down to the river later that same morning, the hundred and thirty-first past the Line, through a hand's depth of new snow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:9:She was a small brown mule, and she was not his. She belonged to that healer, who was two days off up a shut road, and who had lent her to five people at a hold's gate a long while ago without saying for how long. He led her by the cheek-strap across the landing's planks to the round shed where the ferry's gelding had walked his circle through all the dry months. The gelding was in the smith's stable now, under a blanket. The shed stood empty, with reed hurdles tied between its posts against the wind, and a great wooden drum in the middle of it that had nothing to wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:17:The prices were what he knew best. For a hundred and thirty days he had carried all ten down every road in this country and used them as seldom as he could, since the roads out here belonged to things that hunted whatever a Path gave off. He could have said to a grain what each of them charged him for being kept shut. He knew a good deal less about what each would do when it was let out, and where it would fail him, and what it cost afterward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:61:In the west each of them had been a line in a registry: an Ember of the third rank in Iron, and a Wind of the first; an Iron Skin who had stopped at Copper and said so without shame; and a Shadow, Bronze, whom fifteen years of service had taught to be overlooked. Nobody out here had asked any of them for a tier since the ninth day. The board at the foot of the meadow priced the job and never the person. By its reckoning the five of them stood eleventh among the crews on a sheet of sailcloth, which was all the rank that this country kept.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:63:The kit's case lay open on the deal. It had come up out of a hole in the ground sixteen days ago, on Brom's good shoulder, and nobody had yet done more than look into it. There was the cord, coiled, with a knot at every fifth stride. There was the mallet. And there was the stake, which Karis had lifted out and was holding in both hands, as a person holds something that belongs to somebody else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:81:"The ferryman's shed, after dark. It has hurdles round it, and it's a hundred yards from the nearest window." He held up a finger for each. "Three rules. Nothing where anybody could watch who might carry what they saw over that river at the thaw. Shadow stays shut; I'll not spend what protects the person it came from. And nothing with a hand on anybody unless I've asked first and been told yes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:99:"There is a thing that I am counting as well, and I had better say so once. A packet went over that river yesterday in a clerk's bag. I know the road that it takes, and the kind of desk that it stops at, and about how long such a desk sits on a paper before it writes another. Nothing can come back while the ice holds." He looked down at the landing. "On the day that it goes, something can."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:101:"How many days?" said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:109:"Three." Lira was looking at the grey wool across her own breast, and at a knot in it that was not hers. "She said first snow, near enough. Then she said that she'd say when, and then she wrote us a letter with a thaw in it. So mine runs out on the same day as his, and I can't so much as shovel while I wait for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:151:The horse-walk was a ring of beaten earth round the drum, eight strides across. He chose the post that faced him on the far side. Wind had been in his feet since a painted floor, a long while ago; it was the first of the ten, and hers, and for a hundred and thirty days it had leaned him toward the first stride of everything while he told it no.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:197:Pike brought his book out to the plank at noon on the hundred and thirty-third day, for the first time since the ferry shut, and there were three slips on twelve feet of oak.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:213:"And there's the other matter, which I'd as soon do in daylight. You're stopping."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:223:"Standing." Pike considered him in his double fashion, and seemed to find the question a proper one. "That's the hearth's end you're lying at, which is the keeper's floor and my money, and I'll not explain that twice in one day. It's the pot at dusk, a bowl a head; he'll charge you for a second and he'll be right. And it's your names said to anybody who comes asking after a crew, which nobody will, the river being shut. That's three things."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:237:Karis took out the purse. She had paid six to the keeper without looking, four days before, with her eyes on a door. Today she opened her notebook first, and did the sum, and did it again beneath; then she counted twenty marks onto the tarred oak in four heaps of five.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:269:He wrote the day that night at the meal-sack end of the deal, by Karis's lamp, while a strip of brass went down a calfskin on the other side of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:271:*Hundred and thirty-third day. Lowmarch, the lower inn. The cold stay is paid: four a month, five months, twenty in front. In hand, seventy-six. The reserve is not counted here. Nine lines, and no number against the season, on purpose. Eleventh on the cloth. The board has a roof, a well-house and a fence. The fence is ours, and we go out to it on the hundred and thirty-eighth. The roof is ours as well, at five, paid when it has kept a thaw out. The mule stands in the ferryman's shed, at a fee he has not asked for and will.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:1:# Chapter 2 — Never Below Them
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:3:They went out to the fence at first light on the hundred and thirty-eighth day, by the cold road, with the mule carrying meal and two of the keeper's shovels.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:7:They walked for two days up the north water under a sky like a pewter dish, and slept the night between in a hay barn that a carter had told Brom of, with the mule's breath going up among the rafters and Seln sitting out his watch in the doorway. Lira set the great boot down whole at every stride of both days. The bow rode on her back with its one bolt; nobody had proposed that she should stay by a hearth while a fence was mended, and nobody who knew her would have tried.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:25:The hillside rose behind the byre, steeply, for four hundred yards, to a band of grey rock that ran across it like the coping of a wall. The fence left the yard at the gate, and climbed that hill aslant, and then ran east along the foot of the rock. Teague's posts stood out of the snow the whole way up from the gate, six strides apart, as straight as a ruled line, for as far as he had got. Beyond the last of them a row of flat stones went on across the slope in the same line, each the size of a hearth-loaf, with a wisp of straw showing at its edge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:35:Cael had taken them for boulders. They lay along the eastern end of the row of stones, close in under the rock band, where no snow had drifted: a line of long grey backs, keeled, each as broad as a cart and roofed with plates that overlapped like slates. Snow lay in the seams between the plates. He had last seen such backs on a ledge over a bay, on the seventy-eighth day, a breath before a mountain came down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:39:"They came off the tops with the first snow. Ten days." The factor spoke lower than he had in the yard. "There's a lee under that rock, and they've found it. They've lain on my line ever since, and they've not shifted forty yards. I've sixty head in that byre." It stood directly below the grey backs, four hundred yards down, with the long house beside it. "If those go, they go downhill. They'll go through my beasts and my roof and my wife's mother, and I'll not be standing here to pay anybody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:51:Karis read out her page in the yard on the hundred and fortieth morning, before it was properly light, with her back against the byre wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:53:It was the leaf that she had made on a mountain, and all five of them knew what was on it. A shale-back did not answer fire, or noise, or a blow struck on its own rock. It answered one thing: fright in another of its kind, which the whole herd felt in the same instant, and fled from. By that, and by what the carters said, it would answer a declaration in the same fashion. She had written *derived* beneath it, and *not tested*, and sixty-five days had not altered either word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:59:He had thought of nothing else since the gate. It was the one thing that he owned which did not feel like doing anything; but he had written on a road, on the eighteenth day, that he did not know what looking sounded like out here, and he had never found out. Each of the ten fragments went out of him a little when it answered, as other people's Paths went out of them. The read went out least. Least was not the same as none.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:63:She had Seln pace it along the outside of the standing fence. From the yard gate to the nearest grey back it came to three hundred and fifty yards, which was the distance at which she had once lit a fire under a ledge and seen nothing stir. Brom and the mule dragged the factor's hay sledge up the hill to the last of Teague's posts, and Lira sat on it with the bow across her knees. She had seventeen heads before her there, a hundred and fifty yards off, and the whole of the slope beside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:73:He climbed beside Teague's posts and did it again at two hundred yards, and again at sixty, a long way beyond her, where he could see the lichen on the nearest animal's plates. At sixty the read told him nothing whatever about the herd, since its whole world was a pace and a half across. It told him about the slope under his boots. He shut it, and looked back along the hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:81:"The read, three times, for a breath apiece. No answer at three hundred and fifty yards, at two hundred, or at sixty." She read it out as she had read the other. "It is one fragment and one herd and one morning. I shall not write that it is safe. I shall write that three things did not happen."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:157:It was the smallest calf that undid it. Its plates were paler than the rest, and it had kept to the inside of the line all day; but there was a trough on the fence line below, a hollowed log where the factor salted his cattle in the dry months, and a calf is a calf. It left the line. It came down the slope toward the salt at a wandering trot, with its beak out, and it set a forefoot on the thirty-eighth stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:233:They began on the posts at first light on the hundred and forty-first, and it was Brom's work from the first hole to the last.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:243:They set fourteen that day, and fifteen on the next, and twelve on the hundred and forty-third.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:249:At dusk there was a maul going, away toward the gate, long after the day's fifteen were in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:265:They pinned the far side on the hundred and forty-fourth, with the herd mowing a grass tongue a furlong above them, and on the next morning the factor walked his line from the gate to the last rail. He kicked six posts. He counted the new ones with his tally stick, twice, as a man counts who has been cheated before; and then he looked up the hill at seventeen grey backs, for some time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:271:"A hundred and sixteen in hand," said Brom, on the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:273:Karis walked beside the mule that day with her lips moving, and at the first halt she said a thing to nobody, as she had in the back room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:279:Pike said their line at dusk on the hundred and forty-sixth, in a room with thirty people in it and a fire that a man could have stood in. He gave it out as he gave everything, with his shoulder turned: a fence, at the axe-head hold, the long one; forty-one posts set and the far side pinned; a herd shifted off the line and nothing run; witnessed. He dipped his pen for the last column.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:1:# Chapter 3 — Reach
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:3:Teague came down to the lower inn at dusk on the hundred and forty-seventh day, and stopped a stride from their end of the hearth, with his thumbs in his belt over the hand-axe.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:11:"I said that my crew comes, and it comes. But I'll not walk it up to one of those places as it is. In there a Path's a thing that you had yesterday." He looked at the fire while he told it off, as he might have told off a load. "My spear's a tall body with a pole. My bow's a man with a bad hip. The boy's a boy."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:77:He stood in the trodden snow with his breath going up. He had been touched on the thigh, and the shoulder, and the right side; and in each of those exchanges his left had stood open, since he had been guarding it for seventy days by habit, and three times that pole's end had gone past the opening without so much as looking at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:85:"Ten days, and you'll have that again. Bow, you're with the big one's bill. He's not to swing it; you're to show me how a man gets by a hook."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:131:"Six right in twenty," she said at last. "That's worse than I was. You've ten days."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:157:On the night of the hundred and forty-eighth Teague sat at the upper inn's fire with his right arm laid along the old woman's board, and let her unlace him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:199:"Ask her yourself," he said. "In daylight, with your cap off. She'll name what she wants for it, and you'll pay what she names."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:223:He thought of the cloth, which hung on its nail beside that same hearth. He could not read the sheet from here, since it was in another house, a hundred yards down a bank, in the dark; and he did not need to. His own name stood by itself on the first line, as it had for more cold terms than he cared to reckon, and ten lines under it there were five names in a row. It had said so at first snow, and it would say so, to the letter, until the first boat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:235:On the hundred and fiftieth night Brom hung a sack of bran from the shed's cross-beam by the tail of the ferry's rope, and drew it back left-handed as far as it would go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:293:*Hundred and fiftieth day. The shed. Trials. Not counted.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:301:*Tide, from Oryn: asked, on the hundred and forty-eighth, for Lira's ankle. Told no. Not used. That is the rule working.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:309:"You've eight days left," said Lira. "Out. Home."

