books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md:137:He knelt as he read a job. That was the thing Cael had watched him do since a board's room in the autumn — read his people in order, one face after another, before a thing was decided, and move them to where it was going to be — and he was doing it now, with one of them, on the stone, in the order he had always done it, except that the face did not read back, and the man knelt there anyway with the wrapped arm at his side and the good hand on the boy's chest where a healer's had been, and did not say anything, and did not look up, and the pass let him.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md-138-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-21.md-139-Oryn stood back from it. Three strides. Her kit closed, the roll on her back by its strap, her hands out of her coat and at her sides, doing nothing, because there was nothing for them to do and she did not pretend there was. She had healed a leg on a slope and a hand at a ford and a knee at a cut and ribs on a bench and had never once been anywhere she could not do the thing she owned, and she stood three strides from the first place she could not, and did not look away from it, and did not go nearer, and that was a healer's whole verdict, and Teague did not need it said.
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-08.md-51-He walked them the way Karis would have paced a perimeter: the ring road round the bluff's foot from the bridge gate east along the river to the ferry landing and past it to where the first wall came down to the water and stopped, and back west along the wall's foot the other way, past the smith and the rope-walk and the brokers' yards to where the wall came down to the water on the far side. A circle, near enough. A line, with the city inside it and him outside, and everything on his side of the line a thing that had no rank and everything on the other side a thing that did, and the line was a wall, a real one, ochre, with a gate in it that a man with a coloured coat sat at, and he could not have crossed it if he had wanted to, and he did not want to, and the not-wanting was the strangest thing he had felt in a year.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-08.md-52-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-08.md:53:He had spent nine months in a country that did not read him. It had not asked. It had not measured. It had priced what he did and written it in a book and stopped numbering him when the number got wrong, and he had written *no file I can see* in a log until the sentence had gone soft with use, and had found, in the winter, that a country that did not read you was also a country that did not answer, and had not liked finding it. And here was a country that read. Here was the whole machine — the tiers, the gates, the passes, the office on the old town, the thing on the crown — stacked up a hill in front of him in three rings with the sun on it, and it read everyone who walked up its stairs, and it would read him the moment he set a foot on one, and he stood at the foot of it with his hands at his sides and felt the walls as walls, which was a thing he had not felt since a bluff above Ostrand, and found that he had missed it, and did not know what to do with having missed it, and wrote that down.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-08.md-54-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-08.md-55-The road up to the registry went through the square.
--
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md-43-The line was sharp. It was a hair sharp, as it had been at every site: a stride outside, Lira's sigil lit; a stride inside, gone; nothing between. The circle was perfect. Karis said so at the twentieth cord and again at the eightieth, in the voice for a finding, because a circle that stays perfect across eighty cords is a circle and not a guess. And it was larger, and it was older, and she had the first from the cord — "A thousand and twelve. Across. Call it a thousand" — and the second from the stumps.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md-44-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-23.md:45:She read the stumps between cords. Every one she passed. The marks on the inner faces were the same make; he had known that from the first between the shale and the rim, and knew it now from twenty of them: the rows, the bearers, the lean. But she did not read the marks. She read the *cuts*. She put her good hand into a row on a stump's inner face and ran a finger along the cut's bottom, and did it at the next stump, and the next, and at the twelfth she said the thing.
