books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-13.md-89-Cleon had a cup on the table. He had had it there all week — water, from the spring at the gallery's head, in a clay cup that had been a cup for thirty years — and he had not once, in Cael's seeing, drunk from it while he read, because a man who reads with his whole attention does not have a hand to spare for a cup. He picked it up now, and drank.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-13.md-90-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-13.md:91:"I'm going to pretend you didn't tell me that," he said, "for your sake, not mine. But I'll tell you one thing for free, since you can apparently take my whole trade by watching me work it for a minute." He set down the cup. "Everything I read is *made*. Stone, ink, seal — somebody built it, so I can see how. If that fragment ever reads something for you that shouldn't be *made* and reads it anyway — " He stopped. "Don't tell me. Don't tell anyone. Just remember I said it."
