books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-181-Lira with two arms on the table and a foot under it that went down flat because she chose to, and Oryn's crossbow on the wall behind her where she had hung it in the autumn and had not been asked for it. Brom with the pouch — a hundred and eighteen in hand, the mule's shed paid, five, and the ferryman's rope-house roof done for seven in the last week of the ice — and his right shoulder carried as it had been carried since the binding, still and forward, which Oryn had looked at once across the inn's table that night and had said nothing about, which meant she would. Karis with the three copies in their oilcloth on the table's corner and the kit's ledger under her hand and the map under the ledger and both straightedges on the map, because Karis did not put a thing down that she was still using and she was still using all of it. Seln at the door end with the case against his leg and his eyes on the landing door, which had a spring's worth of strangers coming through it now, three tents' worth, and every one of them was a face he had not seen, which was the thing Seln did with a face. Oryn with her hands flat on the table and her roll on the floor by her chair and her route in her head, two stops of it here and one a day south, and the rest of it north, waiting.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-182-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md:183:And himself. Ten. The still place at the middle of them that he did not touch. No tier, no rank, no file he could see, in a town where nobody had asked him for one in nine months, and where the not-asking had stopped, some time in the winter, being the thing it had been in the autumn. He could not have said when. A country that does not read you is a country that does not answer, either, and he had written that down in the hundred and sixties and had not liked it and had let it stand.
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-184-
books/book-08-before-the-paths/chapters/chapter-01.md-185-Karis made her case then, because there were six, and she had been waiting for six.
