editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:283:Lira had been ready since before it was light. The bow was on her back, with its one bolt, and the sling was knotted high at her collar-bone as a healer had left it at a fork. She had not spoken of that arm since a strip of bark was read out at a hearth, and she did not speak of it now. She went up the saddle road first, on the whole of her foot, and the rest of them followed her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:67:He tried the outside. He went round to the tall one's left at a fighter's shuffle, to make the pole come across its owner's body; and the tall one turned on the back heel, a hand's breadth, with no more effort than a door takes, so that the rag was pointing at his breastbone again. He ducked in under it. It drew back through the leading hand like a bolt through a staple, a yard, and came out, and struck him on the point of the right shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:85:"Ten days, and you'll have that again. Bow, you're with the big one's bill. He's not to swing it; you're to show me how a man gets by a hook."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:171:"There's a block down on the second ledge. The mules can't pass it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:219:He put that with the rest. There was a boy who had come out of a hole in the ground declaring more than one kind of thing. There were six pairs of frozen breeches on a rope by a marsh, and a strip of brass that ran off the edge of a calfskin. And this morning there had been a pair of boots in the snow that leaned before every step as if something had them by the laces, and were dragged back each time, and went the plain way and were hit for it. He could not say what the boy had been refusing. He knew a man refusing when he saw one, and he had seen it three times in as many touches.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:223:He thought of the cloth, which hung on its nail beside that same hearth. He could not read the sheet from here, since it was in another house, a hundred yards down a bank, in the dark; and he did not need to. His own name stood by itself on the first line, as it had for more cold terms than he cared to reckon, and ten lines under it there were five names in a row. It had said so at first snow, and it would say so, to the letter, until the first boat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:235:On the hundred and fiftieth night Brom hung a sack of bran from the shed's cross-beam by the tail of the ferry's rope, and drew it back left-handed as far as it would go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:261:He set a block of river ice on the mounting block, where Lira had been sitting, and stood six feet from it, and drew the breath. It gathered under his breastbone for the better part of that breath, slowly, where anything alive might have felt it coming; and Brom, who was watching his chest, shook his head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:299:*Blade, from Ephram: an edge on my knife for about as long as a match burns. The steel pays. The knife won't cut wool.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:77:On the hundred and sixty-first day Cael was on the rope-house roof with a mouth full of nails. It was the job that Brom had put his hand up for, at five, and they had been at it on and off since the fence, whenever the frost let a shingle be split. Seln knelt at the ridge with a hammer. Brom stood on the ladder's fourth rung and passed shingles up left-handed, a dozen at a time, in a rope sling. Below them, at the shed's door, Lira had the brown mule by the cheek-strap and was telling her what she thought of a beast that ate a ferryman's straw and did nothing for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:81:He had the whole river from that roof. A cart had left the green. It was a heavy thing on four wheels, behind two mules, and its driver had cut the bend to save himself the furlong. It stood eighty yards out, a little upstream of the landing, with its tail gone down through the ice to the bed's edge and its pole pointing at the sky. The two mules were on their knees in the traces. Something dark was moving in the broken water behind the tailboard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:85:Brom was off the ladder already. The ferryman had not got up from his keg, and he did not get up now; he spoke as the three of them came round the corner of his shed, in the same voice that he had used for a mule and a trough.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:123:It was going slowly. Each time one of the mules heaved, the lip took the weight, and sagged, and did not quite come back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:145:Brom reached out with the bill and got its hook through the carter's belt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:155:That left the mules, and what was behind them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:157:Seln had gone to their heads by a way of his own, well out to the left, and Cael had not once had to tell him where to tread. He had his coat off and laid across the near mule's eyes. He had the end of the ferryman's line through the ring of the cart's pole, with a knot in it that would never be picked, and he looked back along eighty yards of rope to the round shed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:169:It was the ferryman's boy, at the shed's door, with his hands cupped; and inside the shed a brown mule leaned into a capstan bar, with a girl's right hand on her cheek-strap, and began to go round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:171:Cael did not see the drum turn. He saw the rope come up out of the snow. It rose along its whole length, dripping, and drew as straight as a ruled line from the shed to the cart's pole, and creaked. The two cart mules felt the collar ease. They got their feet under them, with Seln's voice going on between their ears as evenly as a clock, and the tilted bed began to rise.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:177:It broke back a yard, and the wheels dropped into the gap, and climbed again. It would be so the whole way, he saw: the ice would give under that weight plate by plate until the wheels found something thick enough to bear them. The question was only which way the thick lay. He had the read under his chest and his forearms now, where it gave him the same pace and a half that it gave his boots, and he crawled backward before the mules' knees, feeling.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:181:Seln brought their heads round, and the rope swung with them. In the cart's bed a lashing had parted; a barrel came loose from the dozen, and rolled down the slant of the boards, and went over the tailboard into the black water without any sound that he could hear above the mules.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:191:He could see almost nothing by then. The snow a foot from his face was a white blur; he could not have counted his own fingers. He had never held the read for half so long, or leaned on it so hard, and it had taken his near sight from him piece by piece as the price. He went on backward on his elbows with his eyes shut, since they were of no use open, and called the ground to a man whom he could hear and could not see. *Left. Straight. Stand. Walk.* The boy sent each word up the bank, and up there a girl with one arm walked a mule round a drum and stopped her dead at every *stand*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:193:He was told afterward how it had gone in the shed. Lira could not see the river from the horse-walk, or so much as the door. She had gone round that ring backward, with the mule's nose against her chest and her right hand shut on the cheek-strap, hearing two words shouted over and over by a boy whose voice was breaking; and each time that the second came she had set her heels and stopped a beast three times her own weight with a word. Her left arm had lain in its sling throughout and held nothing. The ferryman had turned on his keg to watch her do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:201:He lay on his face on two hands of blue ice and breathed. His left leg was wet to the knee, where a plate had let him through as he went down, and the cold of it had begun to bite; and his head felt like a room from which the lamp has been carried out. He heard the cart mules blowing. He heard a ferryman's rope go slack and fall across eighty yards of snow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:211:They carried the carter up to the lower inn on a hurdle. He was alive, and swearing in a whisper at his own mules, and the keeper had him by the hearth in a blanket before anybody had asked whose he was. Nobody did ask. He was a man off the ice; in that country it was all the name that anyone required of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:213:The ferryman had come down off his keg at last. He stood at the shed's door and looked at his long line, lying wet and black across the river, and at the brown mule, who was eating straw.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:215:"That's my rope," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:223:"You will." He considered the mule. "She's wound a cart out of my river."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:239:*Hundred and sixty-first day. A cart through the river at the bend, eighty yards out. The carter, his two mules and the cart brought off on the ferryman's line, wound in on his drum by Oryn's mule, with Lira leading her. Brom fetched the man out lying down, left-handed. Seln had the beasts.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:247:"I never saw any of it," she said. "I heard a boy shout two words forty times, and I walked a mule in a ring. That's my whole account of the thing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:251:"She's a mule. You tell her, and she does it." Lira shut the hand. "It's a good deal more than I could say of some people on a count."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:279:"I can't make out a letter of it tonight."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:3:The keeper would not have a mule under his eave for a whole cold term, and he said so at the inn's door, with his cloth over his shoulder, while five people were still looking at a healer's letter on a strip of bark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:9:She was a small brown mule, and she was not his. She belonged to that healer, who was two days off up a shut road, and who had lent her to five people at a hold's gate a long while ago without saying for how long. He led her by the cheek-strap across the landing's planks to the round shed where the ferry's gelding had walked his circle through all the dry months. The gelding was in the smith's stable now, under a blanket. The shed stood empty, with reed hurdles tied between its posts against the wind, and a great wooden drum in the middle of it that had nothing to wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:13:He stood over it, and Ember made its offer. It was the fifth of the ten things that he carried, and it would set one small hot point on whatever he looked at for long enough; it was looking at the ice now, through his eyes, like a child at a window. He told it no. A point was a thing spent, and what it burned was the hand that carried it. He put his heel through the ice instead, twice, and the mule pushed past his knee and drank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:21:He left the mule to her water and went out to the end of the planks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:31:The ferryman was sitting on a keg in the lee of his rope-house, with a running block across his knees and a pot of tallow beside him. He had been there the whole time. He had watched a stranger stable a mule and break a trough and walk to the end of a landing, and he had not thought any of it worth a word until now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:39:"A month. Less, if it holds as cold as this." His eyes went to the shed's door, where a brown rump showed. "That's a mule in my shed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:49:He had kept five counts since the Line, in Hesk's book, and every one of them had stopped at this river. The ferry had made her last crossing, and her cable hung bare from bank to bank with rime on it; no post would come over until the thaw, so that a letter to Denvash and another to a yard in a western city would lie unanswered for as long as the ice chose. The board was ruled off. There were nine lines under five names in a one-armed man's ledger, and a sheet of sailcloth by the inn's hearth with those names on its eleventh line, and neither would change by a stroke while the holds were shut. There were ninety-six marks in a purse. There were twenty-seven uses written against the ten fragments.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:109:"Three." Lira was looking at the grey wool across her own breast, and at a knot in it that was not hers. "She said first snow, near enough. Then she said that she'd say when, and then she wrote us a letter with a thaw in it. So mine runs out on the same day as his, and I can't so much as shovel while I wait for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:115:"I know that you can. It's why I'm saying it to you and not to the mule."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:123:"What they say. I have fifty-two sheets, and forty-one kinds of mark, and I can tell you the order in which those kinds stand on three floors. I cannot tell you what a single one of them means." She squinted along a strip from the Fallow. "I have never learned to read a thing from the thing alone. Nobody has. There must be something to lay beside it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:137:The mule had been moved to the far side of the drum. Seln stood in the gap between two hurdles with his back to the shed and his face to the landing; he had not spoken since he took the place, and Cael understood that a man with his back turned could never afterward be asked what he had seen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:159:He had known that it would. For one full count nothing in him answered: he could not turn his head, or lift the hand that was a finger from the post, or so much as shift his weight from the foot that had come down first. He stood in a ferryman's shed like a thing nailed there, and heard the mule blow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:181:He looked round the shed. There was the drum, as tall as his chest and as thick as a cart, with the hauling rope coiled against its foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:199:Cael read them from the left-hand post. Each was on bark, in the square black hand, with a little picture beside the words for people who had no letters.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:201:*Roof, the ferryman's rope-house; shingles found; five, when it has kept a thaw out.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:223:"Standing." Pike considered him in his double fashion, and seemed to find the question a proper one. "That's the hearth's end you're lying at, which is the keeper's floor and my money, and I'll not explain that twice in one day. It's the pot at dusk, a bowl a head; he'll charge you for a second and he'll be right. And it's your names said to anybody who comes asking after a crew, which nobody will, the river being shut. That's three things."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:227:"It's not bread. It's not candles. It's not the room behind his chimney; you've paid him that, six, and I have it down as heard and not as seen. And it's not that mule."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:251:Across the inn yard, by the smith's rail, five people were loading two mules. There were sacks of salt on the snow, and a board lashed along one pack; a tall figure stood at the mules' heads with a grounded spear, and a broad man was easing his weight onto his right leg, and a boy of sixteen or so was trying to lift two sacks where he had been given one. A lean old woman in a cap stood by with a staff that she did not lean on. The man who was testing the girths had a band of black leather laced on his right wrist. Pike had guessed him a Bronze once, under that eave, and had never been told. He finished the buckle that he was at. Then his head came up, and his eyes went over the plank, and the heaps of money on it, and five people standing in front of three slips.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:271:*Hundred and thirty-third day. Lowmarch, the lower inn. The cold stay is paid: four a month, five months, twenty in front. In hand, seventy-six. The reserve is not counted here. Nine lines, and no number against the season, on purpose. Eleventh on the cloth. The board has a roof, a well-house and a fence. The fence is ours, and we go out to it on the hundred and thirty-eighth. The roof is ours as well, at five, paid when it has kept a thaw out. The mule stands in the ferryman's shed, at a fee he has not asked for and will.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:293:"Every figure that I keep has stopped," he said. "The purse won't move till the fence. The cloth can't move at all. I've nine lines and no post, and a number that I've promised her not to change."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:3:They went out to the fence at first light on the hundred and thirty-eighth day, by the cold road, with the mule carrying meal and two of the keeper's shovels.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:7:They walked for two days up the north water under a sky like a pewter dish, and slept the night between in a hay barn that a carter had told Brom of, with the mule's breath going up among the rafters and Seln sitting out his watch in the doorway. Lira set the great boot down whole at every stride of both days. The bow rode on her back with its one bolt; nobody had proposed that she should stay by a hearth while a fence was mended, and nobody who knew her would have tried.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:51:Karis read out her page in the yard on the hundred and fortieth morning, before it was properly light, with her back against the byre wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:63:She had Seln pace it along the outside of the standing fence. From the yard gate to the nearest grey back it came to three hundred and fifty yards, which was the distance at which she had once lit a fire under a ledge and seen nothing stir. Brom and the mule dragged the factor's hay sledge up the hill to the last of Teague's posts, and Lira sat on it with the bow across her knees. She had seventeen heads before her there, a hundred and fifty yards off, and the whole of the slope beside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:239:He took that line off Teague's posts and carried it on across the hill. It went as slowly as anything goes that is done properly in frozen ground. A cap-stone came up, and the straw came out of the hole beneath it, dry, since the frost had never got in; then Brom walked nine feet of ash upright into its place against his good shoulder, and Seln and Cael drove split stone down round its foot with the maul until it rang, and rammed gravel on top. Lira sat astride a rail at the standing end, sighting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:271:"A hundred and sixteen in hand," said Brom, on the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:273:Karis walked beside the mule that day with her lips moving, and at the first halt she said a thing to nobody, as she had in the back room.

