    43	"I rode a mule off a mountain once, for that," said Lira. "I'll walk home on it."
    44	
    45	Nobody at that fire said what *it* was, and Cael noticed that none of them had, in six days. They spoke of the foot, and the boot, and the tape; and the name of the joint itself went all the way from a fence of iron to a river without being spoken by anybody.
    46	
    47	He woke on the hundred and twenty-ninth morning lying on his left side.
    48	
    49	He had rolled onto it in his sleep, and it had let him. He lay and breathed, slowly, to the bottom of his chest, and nothing answered. It was ten days to the morning, as she had said.
    50	
    51	---
    52	
    53	On the hundred and twenty-fourth night, the first of the road home, Seln read a page and a half that he had by heart already.
    54	
    55	He read it on his watch, as he had on every night since a room under a floor. He sat with his back to four sleepers and the limp little book open on his knee, tilted to what the embers gave. He did not need the light. He used it regardless, since there is a difference between remembering a hand and looking at it.
   232	### The Quieting site taxonomy — PROVISIONAL (decoded Ch17, fixed Ch20)
   233	The Compact's suppressed records and the recovered survey kit's index use the same grading. Every site sheet carries a mark by the stamp: **a stroke alone** (first-tier — the form rules a box for a site recent on paper, a life or less; the office never had one to fill); **the stroke and the bar** (second-tier, "stable across generations" — the Fallow Ring, the Long Stair, the Drowned Hall, the fourth, the fourth's mark twice the size); **the stroke and two bars** (third-tier, "stable across centuries", documented back to the first sheet the office ever kept — "the setting", the form's own word — one sheet, a copy of a copy). Age is by FIRST SHEET: Ring 61 years, Stair 94, Hall 138, the fourth 206; the third-tier site older than the office. The sites' ages ASCEND along the bearing, oldest last, into the deep edge ("somebody laid them out in order"). Observational only: who graded them and why is not on the page. The carvings' rows LEAN along the bearing (Cleon's reading); which end is first is undecided on the page.
   234	
   235	### The Sunken Span — PROVISIONAL (the fourth site, entered Ch19)
   236	Eight hundred metres across (twice the Ring); the Compact's registry-stamped iron a pace apart, numbered (thirty-four on the crest's stakes), the stroke-and-bar beside the number, in the ground "years, not one winter" (undated). Inside: the ground drops into a SINK; at the centre an arched bridge of seamless grey stone on two piers over the sink, the crown taller than a man; carvings on the piers' inner faces, same make, copied. The bearing runs along the arch. A Compact WATCH-POST on the perimeter shelf (timber hut, fire, kit under a hide, a two-timber barricade; seven hooded, two crossbows, one low practitioner) routed there after the completion report; broke east along the perimeter road; not pursued; no faces. Nothing happened to Cael on the crown (as at every site).
   237	
   238	### The Court — PROVISIONAL (the fifth site; the oldest; entered Ch23–24; NOT named on the page — "the court" as description)
   239	A half-day beyond a dry defile (the pass) east of the Span, at the END of the bearing: a level seamless grey floor 1,012 metres across (Karis's cord), uprights round it weathered to stumps knee-to-waist high, the plain's rock worn DOWN to the floor's level from a mile out; a stratum in the rock a hair below the stumps' base under everything; the marks on the stumps' inner faces the same notation with the cuts worn deeper — by the strata the FIRST cut ("the others are copies" — a finding about rock). Nothing on the floor. The line sharp and perfect; every sigil dark at the stride identically (the last time the book shows it). At the centre (506 strides): NOTHING — no edge to feel; "the same kind of made" (Cael's own revision of his sentence; 'Not the Quiet' and 'Not a fragment' stand). The bearing ends here; no site is on any sheet past it.
   240	
   241	### The leaf — PROVISIONAL (the primary source, read Ch15; the reveal LOCKED as drafted)
   242	Under Treswick's registry, in the Deepstacks (the old quarry the walls were cut from; a single iron leaf on pins; Cleon's key), among a bundle Cleon pulled from the galleries thirty years ago: twelve horn-coloured point-written leaves older than the board. One passage, read aloud once by Karis: the years of the unmaking, when the un-bound walked in the land; one who "made the measure, and the seat, and the tier, and the mark that is given at the kindling … and set the apparatus over all the land"; the name scraped to the fibre (four or five letters; the first stroke); "that the un-bound should not come again"; ~four hundred years by the leaf's head. **The Architect** is the record's TITLE for the maker (Karis: "a title, not a name; the name's gone"). A later chronicle leaf: "he did not die in the way of men, but went into the ordering he had made, and is in it —" then a thumb's width of nothing — MARKED, not read (Book 11's plant). The leaf's "list of parts" names the mark at the kindling and the mark on a sheet in the same line ("A mark is a grade"). The leaf stays on Cleon's shelf; the crew carries copies with the gap drawn.
    37	She paced it along the line from outside, as she had paced the fourth, because that was how you paced a thing you had decided not to go into yet, and it took the afternoon, and he catalogued the afternoon by the count, because the count was the thing that was going to be entered.
    38	
    39	Lira on the line. She had the job from the Ring and the Stair and the fourth: walk the perimeter with her sigil lit, a stride at a time, until it went out, and step back until it came on, and call it. She did it as she had done it at four sites, and it went out at the stride, at a place a pace inside the stumps' inner faces, and she said "Gone," and stepped back, and said "Back," and Karis put a stone down, and they went on. Cord and stride and stone. Brom marking. Seln at the mule with the case, keeping the count of the plain, which was eleven and a mule and nothing else, because there was nothing else, because there was nothing on the plain to hide anything behind, and Seln, who had spent fifteen years using the ground, stood in the one place on the continent where there was no ground to use and did not like it and said so once, to the mule.
    40	
    41	Teague's three sat at the rim while the cord went round, on the plain's rock outside the stumps, and did not help and were not asked to, because the pacing was the six's and the contract had said so. The tall woman watched Lira's sigil go out at the stride and come on at the stride back, twenty times, and at the twentieth she looked at her own hand, at the mark on it, and closed the hand, and did not open it again that afternoon. The broad man sat with the leg out and the crossbow across his knees and looked at the floor like water he had been told was deep. Teague stood. He did not sit all afternoon; he stood at the place where the path had come in between two stumps and read the floor as he read a job, and Cael, passing with the cord's far stone, saw him do it, and saw him come to the end of the reading with nothing moved, because there was nothing on the floor to move anyone to, and saw him not like that, and enter it, in his way, which was to stand there longer.
    42	
    43	The line was sharp. It was a hair sharp, as it had been at every site: a stride outside, Lira's sigil lit; a stride inside, gone; nothing between. The circle was perfect. Karis said so at the twentieth cord and again at the eightieth, in the voice for a finding, because a circle that stays perfect across eighty cords is a circle and not a guess. And it was larger, and it was older, and she had the first from the cord — "A thousand and twelve. Across. Call it a thousand" — and the second from the stumps.
    44	
    45	She read the stumps between cords. Every one she passed. The marks on the inner faces were the same make; he had known that from the first between the shale and the rim, and knew it now from twenty of them: the rows, the bearers, the lean. But she did not read the marks. She read the *cuts*. She put her good hand into a row on a stump's inner face and ran a finger along the cut's bottom, and did it at the next stump, and the next, and at the twelfth she said the thing.
    46	
    47	"Deeper."

